    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Sherbrooke-680/Assistant-Professor_JR0000079402": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-24\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The Department of Languages, Literatures, and Cultures within the Faculty of Arts of McGill University invites applications for a tenure-track position in Russian Studies at the rank of Assistant Professor. Applicants should demonstrate a strong record of, or clear potential for, significant research and publication, as well as excellence in university-level teaching. We seek a dynamic teacher and scholar who will contribute actively to the development of the Russian Studies program and demonstrate potential for collaborative research across disciplines and media.   Russian Studies at McGill is housed within the multidisciplinary Department of Languages, Literatures, and Cultures, which offers courses and programs in German, Italian, Russian, and Hispanic languages, literatures, cultures, thought, cinema, and digital humanities. The Department combines sustained engagement with particular linguistic and cultural traditions with transnational, comparative, and interdisciplinary approaches. Candidates should indicate how their research and teaching would contribute to this broader intellectual and multicultural environment.   The successful candidate will be expected to teach a broad range of undergraduate and graduate courses in nineteenth- and twentieth-century Russian literature and culture. We particularly welcome candidates whose research and teaching also engage with areas such as cross-cultural and comparative studies, regional studies, media studies, environmental humanities, decolonial and postcolonial studies, performance studies, or intellectual history.   Applicants must hold a PhD by the date of appointment and possess proficiency in Russian and English appropriate for effective university-level teaching, research, and service.     APPLICATION PROCESS   Applications must be submitted online through Workday (McGill’s employment portal). The system is easiest to navigate if all required documents are combined into a single PDF file.   Applications should include a letter of interest, curriculum vitae, teaching portfolio, two syllabi for content-based courses taught in English, and up to two writing samples.   Candidates selected for the longlist will be contacted at a later date and asked to provide the names and contact information of three referees.   If you have questions about the application process or experience any issues navigating the application portal, please contact Cecily Raynor at cecily.raynor@mcgill.ca. Salary: Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience.   Application deadline: October 24 , 2026   Job Type: Tenure-track Rank: Assistant Professor Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bronfman-Building/Grader---MRKT-453_JR0000079646": {
      "title": "Grader - MRKT 453",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-20\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). A grader provides support with marking exams and assignments for a particular course.  Background knowledge of the subject matter is an asset.   Hourly Salary: $16.60 Position Start Date: 2026-08-30 Position End Date: 2027-01-16 Deadline to Apply: McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
