    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McGill-College-2001/Faculty-Lecturer-General-Dentistry_JR0000080781": {
      "title": "Faculty Lecturer-General Dentistry",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nPart time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position description The Faculty of Dental Medicine and Oral Health Sciences at McGill University is a dynamic faculty with a strong record of national and international leadership in the advancement of dental education, research and the profession. We are inviting applications for a part-time Ranked Contract Academic Staff Position at the level of Faculty Lecturer in the field of General Dentistry starting in the academic year 2026-2027. This is a ranked appointment as a member of the Contract Academic Staff and does not confer eligibility for tenure. The primary responsibility of the successful candidate will be clinical supervision and clinical teaching in the field of oral diagnosis, preventive dentistry & periodontics, oral radiology but can be extended to comprehensive care.  Community-oriented responsibilities will also be expected, commensurate with the importance the Faculty places on these areas. We seek to recruit well-rounded, enthusiastic individuals who champion Faculty vision, mission and shared values. Candidates must be committed to showing leadership in the areas of interest. Job Duties Successful applicants will be expected to: Provide clinical training/teaching to undergraduate students. Supervise students during clinical procedures while ensuring patient well-being and maintaining a supportive learning environment. Evaluate students and contribute to their professional development. Perform all professional dental duties in accordance with current standards of practice. Collaborate with faculty members and clinical training coordinators to ensure consistency in student supervision and the integration of evidence-based practice into professional care. Participate in all aspects of McGill’s academic mission (i.e. teaching and involvement in academic and administrative committees). Qualifications Applicants must have a D.M.D. or D.D.S. or equivalent from an accredited school. Applicants must be licensed or be eligible to be licensed by the Ordre des dentistes du Quebec. In accordance with career stage, candidates are expected to demonstrate evidence of recent clinical practice and high-quality teaching, inclusive pedagogy, and supervision of a diverse student body. Applicants who share our values and commitment to excellence in education, community service and research are encouraged to apply. Faculty/Department/Unit Faculty of Dental Medicine and Oral Health Sciences   Application deadline 30 October, 2026 Required Documents The following supporting documents are required: A cover letter The proof of DMD or DDS degree from a recognized accredited school curriculum vitae statement of teaching (for example, teaching statement, course materials) Provide evidence on teaching and educational skills and equivalent expertise in the field of interest. the names and contact information of three references from the previous institutions/organizations that they work in and a letter of the Dean of the Faculty that they have graduated from. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Macdonald-Eng-Bldg/Course-Lecturer--Winter-2027--MECH-532---Aircraft-Performance--Stability-and-Control_JR0000080928": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer (Winter 2027) MECH 532 - Aircraft Performance, Stability and Control",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Department of Mechanical Engineering Faculty of Engineering McGill University Winter 2027 Course Lecturer Position Deadline to Apply October 14, 2026 The Department of Mechanical Engineering is inviting applications for a course lecturer position to teach in Winter 2027. Please include your cover letter and Curriculum Vitae. Course Lecturer MECH 532 - Aircraft Performance, Stability and Control (3 credits) Instructor must cover all material as written in the Course Outline on the McGill Course Catalogue page, as follows: Aircraft performance criteria such as range, endurance, rate of climb, maximum ceiling for steady and accelerated flight. Landing and take-off distances. Static and dynamic stability in the longitudinal (stick-fixed and stick-free) and coupled lateral and directional modes. Control response for all three modes. Teaching Qualification Requirements: Minimum of Ph.D. in Mechanical Engineering and/or Aerospace Engineering. Preference will be given to those with work and/or research experience focused on aircraft dynamics and control. Demonstrated knowledge of spoken and written English. Understanding cross-cultural practices. Strong problem-solving skills and flexible approach to teaching. Date: January 5, 2027 - April 30, 2027 Salary: $11,824 per course Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-14 For any questions, please contact apptadmin.mecheng@mcgill.ca Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hiring Unit: Department of Mechanical Engineering Course Title: Aircraft Performance, Stability and Control Subject Code: MECH 513 Location: ENGTR 0060 Schedule: Tuesday 5:35 pm - 8:25 pm Deadline to Apply: McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Macdonald-Eng-Bldg/Course-Lecturer--Winter-2027--MECH-513---Control-Systems_JR0000080835": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer (Winter 2027) MECH 513 – Control Systems",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Department of Mechanical Engineering Faculty of Engineering McGill University Winter 2027 Course Lecturer Position Deadline to Apply October 14, 2026 The Department of Mechanical Engineering is inviting applications for a course lecturer position to teach in Winter 2027. Please include your cover letter and Curriculum Vitae. MECH 513 – Control Systems (3 credits) Instructor must cover all material as written in the Course Outline on the McGill Course Catalogue page, as follows: State-space modelling and related linear algebra. Controllability and observability of linear time-invariant systems and corresponding tests, system realizations. Stability: Bounded-Input-Bounded-Output (BIBO), internal, Lyapunov. Linear state feedback control: pole placement and root locus design methods, linear quadratic regulator. State observers: full- and reduced-order designs, separation principle, Linear Quadratic Gaussian (LQG) design. Introduction to optimal control and optimal state estimation. Teaching Qualification Requirements: Minimum of Ph.D. degree in Mechanical or Electrical Engineering.  Minimum of five years’ experience in the analysis and/or design of control systems. Prior experience in teaching.  Familiarity with state-space modeling, analysis of control stability and other advanced control topics.  Demonstrated knowledge of spoken and written English.  Strong problem-solving skills and flexible approach to teaching.  Awareness of cross-cultural practices.  Date: January 5, 2027 - April 30, 2027 Salary: $11,824 per course Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-14 For any questions, please contact apptadmin.mecheng@mcgill.ca. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hiring Unit: Department of Mechanical Engineering Course Title: Control Systems Subject Code: MECH 513 Location: ENGTR 0060 Schedule: Tuesday 5:35 pm - 8:25 pm Deadline to Apply: McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Macdonald-Eng-Bldg/Course-Lecturer---Winter-2027----MECH-346-Heat-Transfer_JR0000080834": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer  (Winter 2027) - MECH 346 Heat Transfer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Department of Mechanical Engineering Faculty of Engineering McGill University Winter 2027 Course Lecturer Position Deadline to Apply October 14, 2026 at 11:59 p.m. The Department of Mechanical Engineering is inviting applications for a course lecturer position to teach in Winter 2027. Please include your cover letter and Curriculum Vitae. Course Lecturer MECH 346 Heat Transfer Instructor must cover all material as written in the Course Outline on the McGill Course Catalogue page, as follows: Basic concepts and overview. Steady and unsteady heat conduction. Fin Theory. Convective heat transfer: governing equations; dimensionless parameters; analogy between momentum and heat transfer. Design correlations for forced, natural, and mixed convection. Heat exchangers. Radiative heat transfer: black- and gray-body radiation; shape factors; enclosure theory. Thermal engineering design project. Teaching Qualification Requirements: Minimum of a Ph.D. in Mechanical Engineering. Evidence of highly effective teaching ability or of superior communications skills and potential for teaching excellence with previous teaching experience. Strong problem-solving skills. The candidate area of specialization should be as close as possible to that of the course being offered. Demonstrated knowledge of spoken and written English. Understanding cross-cultural practices. Date: January 5, 2027 – April 30, 2027 Salary: $11,824 per course Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-14 For any questions, please contact apptadmin.mecheng@mcgill.ca Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hiring Unit: Department of Mechanical Engineering Course Title: Heat Transfer Subject Code: MECH 346 Location: ENGMC 13 Schedule: Wednesday and Friday 2:35 pm – 3:55pm Deadline to Apply: McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Macdonald-Eng-Bldg/Course-Lecturer-MECH-289--Winter-2027----Design-Graphics--Section-II--Civil-Engineering-section-_JR0000080832": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer MECH 289 (Winter 2027) - Design Graphics, Section II (Civil Engineering section)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Department of Mechanical Engineering Faculty of Engineering McGill University Winter 2027 Course Lecturer Position Deadline to Apply October 14 2026 The Department of Mechanical Engineering is inviting applications for a course lecturer position to teach in Winter 2027. Please include your cover letter and Curriculum Vitae. MECH 289 Design Graphics, Section II (Civil Engineering section) Instructor must cover all material as written in the Course Outline on the McGill Course Catalogue page, as follows: The design process, including free-hand sketching; from geometry construction to engineering construction; the technology and standards of engineering graphic communication; designing with CAD software. The role of visualization in the production of engineering designs. Teaching Qualification Requirements: Minimum of Master’s degree in Civil Engineering or Architecture. Minimum of three years’ teaching experience or in industry related to Design Graphics software. Good knowledge of Design Graphics software, Auto CAD, and hands-free sketching. Demonstrated knowledge of spoken and written English. Understanding of cross-cultural practices. Strong problem-solving skills and flexible approach to teaching. Date: January 5, 2027 - April 30, 2027 Salary: $11,824 per course Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-14 For any questions, please contact Mechanical Engineering Appointment Administration: apptadmin.mecheng@mcgill.ca Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hiring Unit: Department of Mechanical Engineering Course Title: Design Graphics, Section II Subject Code: MECH 289 2 Location: ENGMD 276 Schedule: Tuesday and Thursday 11:35am – 12:55 pm Deadline to Apply: McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bronfman-Building/Faculty-Position-in-Information-Systems---Ranked-Contract-Academic-Staff--CAS---Desautels-Faculty-of-Management--McGill-University_JR0000080822": {
      "title": "Faculty Position in Information Systems - Ranked Contract Academic Staff (CAS), Desautels Faculty of Management, McGill University",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-28\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Faculty Position in Information Systems Ranked Contract Academic Staff (CAS) Desautels Faculty of Management McGill University Position Summary McGill’s Desautels Faculty of Management invites applications for one Ranked Contractual Academic Staff (CAS) position in the Information Systems (IS) Area. Note that this is not a tenure-track position. The start date for the appointment will be January 1 2027. With the exception of Ranked CAS who are appointed at the rank of Professor, a Ranked CAS is appointed to an initial term of one, two or three years; the contract is renewable. The position is open to candidates with a PhD in Information Systems or a related field, preferably with 5 years of teaching experience. We have a particular interest in candidates whose teaching connects to the Faculty priority area of Analytics, AI & Advanced Digital Technologies. Successful candidates should have the ability to teach courses in the areas of Analytics, AI, and advanced digital technologies, and contribute to the administration of the Areas and Faculty. The largest share of the teaching load will be in the Master of Management in Analytics program, with the remaining sections allocated across other programs, such as Master of Business Administration and Bachelor of Commerce, depending on teaching needs in a given academic year. This position requires teaching up to 8 course sections per academic year. We have a preference for candidates with a teaching portfolio and an established record in teaching AI and machine learning related courses at Master’s level. McGill University is an English-language university where teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing; but knowledge of French or a willingness to learn French is desirable. McGill University McGill is a world-renowned institution of higher learning with a strong emphasis on scholarly activities and groundbreaking research across various disciplines and is consistently ranked as one of the top universities, both nationally and internationally. Located in downtown Montreal, McGill’s Desautels Faculty of Management brings together a diverse community of scholars whose expertise contributes to a dynamic and collaborative academic environment. The Faculty offers a personalized and experiential approach to education, emphasizing responsible management by applying interdisciplinary, integrated and globally minded perspectives to teaching, research and partnerships. Faculty members across  Areas  conduct research using a wide range of theoretical and methodological approaches and enjoy a high degree of independence in their research and teaching. The Faculty places strong emphasis on research quality, innovation, and impact, with opportunities to engage in various  research centres and institutes .  Montreal is a vibrant, multilingual city that blends rich history with modern innovation, making it an ideal place to live and work. With its safe neighborhoods, affordable housing, excellent public transit and year-round activities, it’s a welcoming environment for individuals and families. From world-class culture and cuisine to thriving industries and diverse communities, Montreal offers a dynamic lifestyle that’s both enriching and accessible. Discover Montreal       Best cities to study in the world      Desautels Faculty of Management Compensation levels are competitive and commensurate with experience. Application process and deadline These materials must be submitted through Workday (all in the same section as you submit your CV) : Cover letter Curriculum vitae Evidence of teaching effectiveness We are not requesting reference letters at this point; reference check is a secondary step which may follow.   Any questions about the position or receipt of materials should be addressed to the chair of the hiring committee, Professor Warut Khern-am-nuai, at warut.khern-am-nuai@mcgill.ca . All application materials should be submitted by October 31, 2026 ; applications after the deadline may be considered. The hiring process including the review of application materials, interviews and campus visits with short-listed candidates will be ongoing until the position is filled or the search is terminated. Short-listed candidates invited to meet with members of the Faculty should expect to interview, if health and safety standards allow it, in-person in Montreal. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based. The cover letter in the application may be addressed to: Professor Animesh Desautels Faculty of Management McGill University 1001 Sherbrooke Street West Montreal, Quebec, Canada H3A 1G5 McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McGill-College-2001/Postdoctoral-Researcher_JR0000081624": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). POSITION SUMMARY The Pandemic and Emergency Readiness Lab (PERL) within the Department of Global and Public Health invites applications for a Postdoctoral Researcher in Pandemic Prevention and Global Health Security to support a CIHR STEPS Team Grant focused on strengthening Canada's public health emergency response systems. This fellowship offers a unique opportunity to conduct policy-relevant research that informs national preparedness strategies and contributes to the development of innovative civilian surge capacity models. The successful candidate will be supervised by Professor Prativa Baral and will work closely with interdisciplinary researchers, policymakers, public health leaders, and community partners while contributing to high-impact research and knowledge mobilization activities. The incumbent will hold a dual status as both a student and a McGill employee, and is a member of the AMURE (Postdocs) bargaining unit. RESPONSIBILITIES The incumbent will provide technical leadership and cross-workstream integration across a CIHR STEPS Team Grant. The fellow will function as an investigator, lead key research activities, and translate findings into policy-ready outputs. They will be encouraged to apply for independent salary and research funding. Lead integration of findings across the project’s four workstreams: policy mapping, economic evaluation, participatory co-design, and qualitative research  Conduct and coordinate key research activities including stakeholder interviews (n≈50), focus groups, and contribute to national survey design and analysis  Contribute to economic evaluation workflows, including cost-minimization, cost-effectiveness, budget impact, and ROI modelling, in collaboration with health economics expert. Facilitate co-design workshops with the pan-Canadian advisory network and support knowledge mobilization with government partners  First-author peer-reviewed publications and draft policy briefs, toolkits, and implementation reports for federal and provincial audiences    The project focuses on three public health system building blocks:  Governance : Mapping and comparing civilian reserve models across Canadian provinces/territories (Québec RIUSC, Ontario Corps, Nova Scotia Guard) and international cases (Nordic countries, Global South), with focus on activation triggers, command structures, and integration with public health systems  Finance : Economic evaluation of civilian versus military surge (CAF deployments vs. NGO/civilian alternatives), including cost-minimization, cost-effectiveness, budget impact analysis, and 10–20-year ROI modelling under climate and pandemic scenarios  Workforce : Participatory co-design with a pan-Canadian advisory network and two national workshops to prototype governance, financing, and tiered workforce models, with explicit SGBA+ and equity lenses throughout  Qualitative Research & National Survey : Semi-structured interviews, regional focus groups, and a national stakeholder survey examining feasibility, acceptability, equity implications, and barriers/enablers to civilian reserve participation  Fellows will be integrated into the broader McGill global health community and encouraged to develop their own scholarly identity and independent funding applications aligned with PERL’s mission.     QUALIFICATIONS We’re seeking a rigorous, applied researcher with experience in health systems, emergency management, or public health policy — someone who can lead mixed-methods research and translate findings into real policy change.    Applicants must have: Possess a PhD (within the last 1.5-2 years) in public health, health policy, political science, emergency management, epidemiology, health economics, or a closely related field  Demonstrated experience with qualitative and/or mixed-methods research (e.g., interviews, thematic analysis, focus groups, policy analysis)  Proven ability to translate research into policy-relevant outputs, briefs, toolkits, stakeholder reports, or public engagement  Strong writing and analytical skills, with a peer-reviewed publication record in a relevant area  Experience in emergency preparedness, disaster research, civil protection, or health systems strengthening (highly desirable)  Bilingualism (English and French), strongly preferred given Québec-based co-leadership and national stakeholder engagement  Familiarity with federal-provincial governance and emergency management landscape (an asset, either in Canada or elsewhere with a similar model). The postdoctoral researcher must be resident in Québec to perform work for this position.    Hiring Unit:  Department of Global and Public Health    Number of Positions:  1     Supervisor:  Professor Prativa Baral    Total Hours Per Week:  35   Salary:  $75,000/year    Planned Start and End Date:  November 1, 2026  -  October 31, 2027   Location:  2001 McGill College Application Procedure: Interested candidates should submit (upload in Workday): A cover letter (max 2 pages) describing your research background and your specific interest in this project, as well as how your experience aligns with the civilian reserve research program  Curriculum Vitae  One writing sample (published article or manuscript in progress)  Names and contact information for two academic references    Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis until the position is filled. We thank all applicants; only those selected for an interview will be contacted. * Candidates’ eligibility to hold a Postdoctoral Research Fellowship expires 5 years after the date on which their PhD was awarded, regardless of when they are hired.  Be sure to refer to the following link for further information on eligibility criteria: http://www.mcgill.ca/gps/postdocs/fellows/registration/eligibility .   ** Any offer to non-Canadian citizens/non-permanent residents is contingent on the candidate obtaining a work permit after an offer has been made and a letter of invitation sent, see: http://www.mcgill.ca/gps/postdocs/international/workpermit   « Connaissance du français et de l'anglais: L'Université McGill est une université de langue anglaise où les tâches quotidiennes peuvent nécessiter une communication verbale et écrite en anglais.  Le niveau d’anglais requis pour ce poste a été déterminé comme étant de niveau #4 (qualificatif) sur une échelle de 0-4.»   “Knowledge of French and English:   McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4 on a scale of 0-4.”   In accordance with Canadian and Quebec law, the present offer is conditional upon your obtaining the appropriate work authorization from any or all of the following as applicable: Citizenship & Immigration Canada, Immigration Quebec and Human Resources & Skills Development Canada (HRSDC).  Annual Salary: $75,000.00 Hours per Week: 35 (Full time) Location: McGill College 2001 Supervisor: Assistant Professor (Research) Position Start Date: 2026-09-30 Position End Date: 2027-10-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-30 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Wong-Building/Course-Lecturer--CHEE384--Materials-Engineering-_JR0000081618": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer: CHEE384 (Materials Engineering)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The Faculty is inviting applications to teach a course in Winter 2027.  Please complete the online application form and upload required documents. Applications will be ranked according to the collective agreement between McGill University and the MCLIU. Preference will be given to the qualified applicant with the most priority points. Hiring Unit: Chemical Engineering Course Title: Industrial Water pollution Control Subject Code: CHEE384 (3 credits) Term: Winter 2027 Location: TBD Schedule: TBD Teaching Qualification Requirements (TQRs): Education MSc in Chemical Engineering with a focus on Materials Engineering. Experience: Experience with traditional metal, polymer, ceramic processes (casting, molding, forming, cutting, joining techniques) as well as expertise with novel processing technologies (3D printing, laser machining, etc.), also speciality techniques as they apply to composite materials; Knowledge of structure-property relationships as they result from material processing history and their effect on chemical safety considerations; Understanding and experience with heat treatment and the resulting structure -property changes; Expertise in sustainability considerations with respect to the material engineering life cycle (i.e. capable of discussing social and environmental considerations concerning mining, processing, use phase, to end of life fate (recycling, reusing, upcyclability, effect of processing aids in recyclability) Other qualification requirement(s): Excellent communication skills (written, spoken). Hiring Unit: 