      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-30\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Physiology Course Title: Introductory Physiology Lab 2 Course Code: PHGY 213 Estimated Number of Positions: 5 Total Hours of Work per Term: 63 Position Summary: Attending one 1 hour orientation session Demonstrating in 6-10 x 3-1/2 hour lab sessions Grading lab reports/Quizzes  Giving a one-hour tutorial session Qualifications: B.Sc. in Physiology or allied science Knowledge of the area of Physiology in the given lab session Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-05 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-01 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Sherbrooke-1010/Access-Services-Advisor_JR0000081612-1": {
      "title": "Access Services Advisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit:   Student Accessibility & Achievement   Student Accessibility and Achievement provide an array of learner support services to McGill students of all levels of study, including reasonable academic accommodations for those with documented disabilities of either a permanent, temporary, or episodic nature. ​They foster an accessible and inclusive university experience for McGill’s diverse student population in collaboration with other Student Services and the wider McGill community through resources and services.   Additional Information: Overtime, including evening and weekend hours may be required during peak periods. Position Summary: Under the supervision of the Associate Director, Advising and Learning Supports, the Access Services Advisor serves as a primary point of contact for students with disabilities experiencing barriers to their learning and full participation at McGill. Ensures appropriate support and academic accommodations are identified and implemented to facilitate academic engagement and achievement. Conducts need assessments, provides expert advice to faculty, and collaborates with internal and external stakeholders to ensure seamless service delivery and proper case management. Additionally, the role involves facilitating outreach and education initiatives to raise awareness and foster an inclusive campus environment. Duties and Responsibilities: Conduct comprehensive needs assessments with students facing academic or physical barriers due to disabilities, mental health conditions, chronic illnesses, or other impairments, ensuring accommodations align with both the student’s needs and medical documentation. Collaborate with internal and external professionals when necessary to clarify diagnostic details and ensure a fulsome understanding of the barriers being faced. Advise faculty and appropriate University departments on the development and implementation of reasonable accommodations in the classroom and other university settings. Act as a liaison between students and faculty to ensure effective communication and accommodation provision. Collaborate with key departments within Student Services and across the university to promote understanding and support for students with disabilities. Actively engage in initiatives to enhance campus-wide accessibility and inclusion. Manage a portfolio of outreach, promotional and educational activities designed to increase awareness of services and foster a supportive environment for students with disabilities. Stay current with best practices and emerging trends in disability services, ensuring that all accommodations and services are compliant with legal and university standards. Undertake special projects and initiatives as assigned by the Access Services Advisor – Team Lead, contributing to the continuous improvement of disability support services. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Graduate degree in Educational Psychology, Counselling Psychology, Inclusive Education or other psychosocial field, required.  Experience working in a professional service provider role with people who have disabilities. Experience working on a one-to-one basis with a potentially high stress, constantly changing clientele. Ability to advise faculty and administrators on student disability-related academic accommodations and issues. Proven proficiency in crisis intervention and de-escalation required. Proven experience reviewing medical documentation required.  Experience in the disability field with a particular emphasis on current trends in post-secondary disability service provision, including the Social Model, Universal Design and ecological modes of intervention. Knowledge of post-secondary Universal Design for Learning principles. Must be self-directed and able to work autonomously and as part of a multi-disciplinary team. Tact, diplomacy, excellent interpersonal, organizational and communication skills are essential. Experience leading discussion groups and classes. Awareness and knowledge of adaptive technology for students with disabilities. Proven presentation skills.  English and French, spoken and written. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 4 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 05) $73,470.00 - $91,840.00 - $110,210.00 Job Profile: MPEX-SAF2B - Student Services - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Assoc Dir Stu W Disabilities Position End Date (If applicable): 2028-04-30 Deadline to Apply: McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Penfield-740/Assistant-Professor--Tenure-Track--in-Biosensing--Canada-Impact--Emerging-Leaders-Program--Department-of-Biomedical-Engineering--RL-L001829_JR0000081518": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor (Tenure-Track) in Biosensing, Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders Program, Department of Biomedical Engineering, RL#L001829",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Assistant Professor (Tenure-Track) in Biosensing, Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders Program, Department of Biomedical Engineering, RL#L001829 Program Description The Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders program is a one-time initiative designed to support institutions in recruiting internationally based Early Career Researchers to Canada. Emerging Leaders will receive research funding and institutional support to advance transformative research in Canada’s strategic priority areas , build partnerships across sectors and borders, and translate their research into applications and impacts that benefit Canadians and the world. The Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences and School of Biomedical Sciences at McGill University invites applications for a full-time tenure track Assistant Professor position with a focus on Biosensing, including molecular, cellular, chemical, or physical biosensors. The Department of Biomedical Engineering seeks a candidate who will develop an independent research program on biosensing, to study the impact of the environment (including toxic exposures, nutrition, microbiome, etc.)  on human health. We welcome candidates with expertise in biosensing, including in the design and/or use of biosensors, such as biosensors based (i) on molecules (e.g. affinity binders, antibodies, aptamers enzymes); (ii) on natural or artificial cells and synthetic biology; or (iii) on physical and chemical sensors; and (iv) in the integration of biosensors with detection systems and instrumentation including, but not limited to microfluidics, lab-on-a-chip, wearables, implantables and ingestibles. Expertise in continuous monitoring, small molecule (e.g. metabolite) sensing, or computational and machine learning-based biosensors and biosensing will be considered assets.   The successful candidate will advance transformational research programs that support the growth of Canada’s global leadership in the strategic priority area of ‘Health, including biotechnology’. This position contributes to the broader goal of transforming medicine from reactive approaches toward preventative and sustainable models of health, informed by a One Health perspective. The research program should align with McGill’s Strategic Research Plan, particularly its Health and Well-Being and Technologies and Applications for Society priority areas, while building on institutional strengths in climate change and health, precision and data-driven health, emerging technologies, and translational research. Through interdisciplinary collaboration, the research program will help address complex health challenges and support McGill’s commitment to the responsible development and application of innovative technologies with meaningful societal impact. It will also promote connections among researchers, clinicians, healthcare systems, industry, and communities, especially communities particularly vulnerable to the impacts of the environment on health.   The research is also expected to strengthen the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences’ Strategic Research Plan, Together for Better Health , which emphasizes the continuum from foundational discovery to clinical application and real-world impact. McGill’s Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences is internationally recognized for excellence in research, education, and interdisciplinary collaboration. Together with Quebec’s vibrant health innovation ecosystem, the environment offers an exceptional opportunity for establishing a world-leading independent research program with significant scientific, clinical, and societal impact. The successful candidate will be required to begin a full-time tenure-track appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor no later than July 1, 2027 , in the Department of Biomedical Engineering. This may include a joint appointment with McGill-affiliated institutes and centres. Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders Program The Canada Impact+ Emerging Leaders program is a one-time initiative designed to support institutions in recruiting internationally based Early Career Researchers to Canada. Emerging Leaders will receive research funding and institutional support to advance transformative research in Canada’s strategic priority areas , build partnerships across sectors and borders, and translate their research into applications and impacts that benefit Canadians and the world. The successful candidate will be expected to propose an independent research program demonstrating strong alignment with the program’s strategic priority of ‘Health, including biotechnology’. The Emerging Leaders Program award is valued at $100,000 per year over six years to support the Early Career Researchers’ Research Program, with the possibility of extension for a further six years. An exceptional environment for Biosensing research and translation The candidate will join a growing research community in biosensing and wearables, strengthened by McGill’s recent recruitment of Professor Sameer Sonkusale through the $10 M Canada Eddie Goldenberg Chairs of Canada program to the Department of Biomedical Engineering within the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences. The candidate’s research program will complement the strategic research cluster in biosensors and wearables in the Department of Biomedical Engineering, as well as in the Faculty of Engineering, positioning McGill as global leader in this area. McGill offers an exceptional end-to-end pipeline for translating technologies from ideation to clinical applications and clinical trials. The candidate will benefit from on-site expertise and infrastructure spanning molecular and device design, microfabrication, rapid prototyping, systems integration, AI-enabled sensing, clinical research, and technology translation. McGill Nanotools Micro- and Nanofabrication Facility is recognized as one of the leading microfabrication facilities globally, and its suite of instrumentation and equipment will be expanded to include modern rapid prototyping equipment as part of the new Impact+ Research Chair. The candidate will also benefit from McGill’s strong innovation and commercialization ecosystem, including the Office of Innovation and Partnerships, the Technology Transfer Office, and entrepreneurship promotion through the McGill Innovation Fund, the Clinical Innovation Platform, the Dobson Centre for Entrepreneurship, and the McGill Engine that collectively provide $825K in annual nondilutive funding (the largest in Canada). These resources support the progression of promising discoveries toward intellectual property, partnerships, clinical validation, and new ventures, and provide opportunities to engage with industry and healthcare partners throughout the innovation pathway. The successful candidate will join McGill during a period of exceptional institutional investment with the $820 M Sustainability Park and the Climate Change and Health Centre . The successful candidate will also benefit from extensive opportunities for collaboration with researchers in the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences (Goodman Cancer Institute, the Dahdaleh Institute for Genomic Medicine, School of Population and Global Health), the Faculty of Engineering, the McGill-affiliated hospitals and research institutes, as well as with the Faculty of Agricultural and Environmental Sciences. McGill strongly encourages interdisciplinary collaboration across Medicine, Engineering, and Science, as well as partnerships with industry and government that accelerate translation of fundamental discoveries. Job Duties   The successful applicant will be expected to: ·        Develop an independent research program of the highest international standards in their own areas of expertise related to biosensing, aligned with the priorities of the Department of Biomedical Engineering , School of Biomedical Sciences , and Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences ·        The research program is expected to include interdisciplinary collaboration with relevant partner units within the University and its affiliates, as well as in Canada and internationally Successfully compete for research funding Supervise a diverse body of graduate students Serve in administrative roles within their units, the university, and the wider scholarly community Contribute to formal classroom teaching equivalent to at least one graduate course in their area of expertise and supporting and complementing the existing offerings Disseminate research findings through peer-reviewed publications and other knowledge translation and exchange activities Integrate equity, diversity, and inclusiveness (EDI) considerations into research design and practices and create an equitable and inclusive research environment. Qualifications and Education Requirements Only candidates who were internationally based, both working and residing outside Canada, on July 4, 2026 are eligible. Candidates must be within five years following the start date of their first research-related appointment, minus the length of any eligible delays in research. Recent graduates and postdoctoral fellows may also be eligible. Research-related appointments are defined as those where the individual has the autonomy to conduct research independently. Eligible leaves, including maternity, parental, medical, and bereavement leave, are credited as twice the amount of time taken. Professional leaves, including training, sabbatical, and administrative leave, are not credited. A PhD is required by McGill University for appointment to a tenure-track position; however, it is not a requirement of the Impact+ Emerging Leaders Program itself. Candidates should review the eligibility and selection criteria for the Impact+ Program prior to applying.     Canadians living abroad who wish to relocate to Canada are encouraged to apply. Application Process Applications must be submitted through Workday, McGill’s HR management system. Use a personal email address when creating an account in Workday . Refer to the How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates) job aid for instructions on how to apply. Required Documents The application package should include the documents listed below and must be merged into one single PDF file not exceeding 5 MB. 1-page executive summary Cover letter (maximum 2 pages) Curriculum vitae Statement of research describing current and future research, alignment with the strategic priority area, and potential for knowledge translation, mobilization, and application of research results (maximum 2 pages) Research infrastructure needs: laboratory space, equipment (maximum 1 page) Teaching statement and dossier (maximum 1 page) Contact information for three references  Job Details Job Type:            Tenure-track Rank:                   Assistant Professor Salary:                 Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Posting Period Inquiries from potential candidates are welcome and will be treated in strict confidence. Correspondence can be directed to  Sonia.nardini@mcgill.ca Commitment to Equity and Diversity Please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University offers immigration and relocation support for selected candidates . McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing.   McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities.   At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry.   McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record.   McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, Catherine Roy accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . Posting date: October 1, 2026 Closing date: November 15, 2026   McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Morrice-Hall/Grader---Arabic-Language-Courses---Winter-2027_JR0000080695-1": {
      "title": "Grader - Arabic Language Courses - Winter 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-01\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The Institute of Islamic Studies is recruiting Graders for the Arabic language courses for the winter 2027 semester.   ISLA 221/621 D2 (001) – Introductory Arabic Position Summary: The successful applicants will grade all submitted assignments before the next class meeting and submit the grades online using McGill systems.   Qualifications: Preference will be given to graduate students in the PhD program at the Institute of Islamic Studies. Applicants must be in good standing in their academic program. Demonstrable knowledge in written Arabic is required. Additional testing may be requested.   Language Requirements: English; Arabic. Hiring Unit: Institute of Islamic Studies Course Title: ISLA 221/621 D2 (001) - Introductory Arabic Estimated Number of Positions: 2 Estimated number of hours per term: 165 hours ISLA 221/621 D2 (002) – Introductory Arabic Position Summary: The successful applicants will grade all submitted assignments before the next class meeting and submit the grades online using McGill systems.   Qualifications: Preference will be given to graduate students in the PhD program at the Institute of Islamic Studies. Applicants must be in good standing in their academic program. Demonstrable knowledge in written Arabic is required. Additional testing may be requested.   Language Requirements: English; Arabic. Hiring Unit: Institute of Islamic Studies Course Title: ISLA 221/621 D2 (002) - Introductory Arabic Estimated Number of Positions: 2 Estimated number of hours per term: 165 hours ISLA 332/622 D2 (001) – Lower-Intermediate Arabic Position Summary: The successful applicants will grade all submitted assignments before the next class meeting and submit the grades online using McGill systems.   Qualifications: Preference will be given to graduate students in the PhD program at the Institute of Islamic Studies. Applicants must be in good standing in their academic program. Demonstrable intermediate knowledge in written Arabic is required. Additional testing may be requested.   Language Requirements: English; near-native proficiency in Arabic. Hiring Unit: Institute of Islamic Studies Course Title: ISLA 322/622 D2 (001) – Lower-Intermediate Arabic Estimated Number of Positions: 2 Estimated number of hours per term: 165 hours ISLA 423/623 D2 (001) – Higher-Intermediate Arabic Position Summary: The successful applicants will grade all submitted assignments before the next class meeting and submit the grades online using McGill systems.   Qualifications: Preference will be given to graduate students in the PhD program at the Institute of Islamic Studies. Applicants must be in good standing in their academic program. Demonstrable advanced knowledge in written Arabic is required. Additional testing may be requested.   Language Requirements: English; native or near-native proficiency in Arabic. Hiring Unit: Institute of Islamic Studies Course Title: ISLA 423/623 D2 (001) – Higher-Intermediate Arabic Estimated Number of Positions: 2 Estimated number of hours per term: 150 hours Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
