    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McGill-College-2001/Teaching-Assistant---FSCI-198--Climate-Crisis-and-Climate-Actions-_JR0000082318": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - FSCI 198: Climate Crisis and Climate Actions",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Office of Science Education   Course Title: Climate Crisis and Climate Actions  Course Code: FSCI 198 Estimated Number of Positions: 12 Total Hours of Work per Term: 180 Hiring Unit: Office of Science Education   Title and course number: FSCI 198 Climate Crisis and Climate Actions   Estimate of the number positions available: 12  Total hours of work per term: 180   Term: Winter 2027  Dates of classes: Thursdays: 1:05 pm – 3:25 pm  Effective dates of appointment: January 2nd, 2027, to April 16th, 2027  Hourly rates and salary per term: $38.46      Special requirements: - Must be available for in-person training on Tuesday, January 5th, 2027 (10 a.m. - 1 p.m.).   - Must be available to co-lead one weekly 85-minute workshop beginning January 11th, 2027.  ( Mon:   10:05 – 11:25 am OR 1:05 – 2:25 pm OR 2:35 – 3:55 pm OR Tues : 8:35 – 9:55 am OR 11:35 am – 12:55 pm OR 1:05 – 2:25 pm)   - Must be available to attend in-person lectures each Thursday between 1:05 p.m. and 3:25 p.m. from January 7th until April 8th.    Please submit a one-page cover letter and your CV as part of your application on Workday. This letter should describe why you are interested in becoming a TA for FSCI 198 as well as how you meet the qualifications noted below.      Position summary       This position is well-suited to graduate students who would like to gain experience leading weekly seminars to develop skills in teaching and in cross-disciplinary approaches to complex problems.      FSCI 198 will introduce undergraduate students from programs across the University to climate science and climate action. The course will focus on providing tools for climate science literacy, learning in community, and taking action to reduce impacts of the climate crisis. Lecture and tutorial activities focus on active learning, personal reflection, Indigenous knowledge, and building hope and resilience through collaborative action. More information about the course is here: https://www.mcgill.ca/ose/courses/fsci-198      FSCI 198 is scheduled for two teaching segments each week. On Thursdays from 1:05 pm – 3:25 pm there will be a 2.5 hour large-format lecture with all students, TAs, and instructors in attendance.      The second is an 85-minute segment with groups of up to 20 students in an active learning workshop led by two TAs. TAs will receive guidelines and materials for each session. TAs will be responsible for assessing and providing feedback to these students (~10 per TA) on their written individual and team assignments throughout the term. TAs will also be the first contact for responding to students’ needs and concerns throughout the course.      Summary of required duties:       Participate in weekly 1-hour course meetings with teaching team of co-instructors and TAs   Meet for office hours by request of students  Work closely with TAs and co-instructors to lead weekly workshops including facilitating discussions   Complete course readings and follow workshop instructions    Answer students’ questions (including emails) pertaining to course material and assessment.    Grade and provide feedback on student assessments in a timely fashion in collaboration with instructors.    Be accountable for professional, timely, and confidential use and accurate entry of student marks in the FSCI 198 Grade Book in myCourses.        Qualifications :    Strong candidates will have an interest in climate education and experience with grading and giving feedback on writing. Interest in Indigenous pedagogy is important. Knowledge and practice of science communication is valuable.  A willingness to learn with undergraduate students and to engage in reflective and inclusive teaching practices is key.    Completed undergraduate degree in science, arts, engineering, education, or other related field.    Must have strong organizational skills and excellent communication skills.   McGill University is committed to equity in employment.    Location: This position is located in Montreal, Quebec and the work for this position is expected to be performed in Quebec.   Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2026-10-14 Position End Date: 2027-04-16 Deadline to Apply: 2026-12-01 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Sherbrooke-680/MPP-Research-Assistant----Competition-Policy-Podcast_JR0000082179-2": {
      "title": "MPP Research Assistant -  Competition Policy Podcast",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Applicants must be an MPP student at Max Bell School of Public Policy Job Title : Research Assistant (1 position) Location: Max Bell School of Public Policy, McGill University Supervisor: Matthew Chiasson, J.W. McConnell Visiting Scholar Start Date: Late October 2026 (as soon as possible) End Date : April 30, 2027 Hours: 2–4 hours per week Rate: $25.00/hour Project Title Planning for Competition (working title) Position Summary Matthew Chiasson, J.W. McConnell Visiting Scholar at the Max Bell School of Public Policy, is seeking one Research Assistant (RA) to help develop and produce a podcast exploring competition as a lens through which to understand contemporary public policy challenges. Tentatively titled “Planning for Competition,” the podcast would feature accessible, approximately 15–20 minute conversations with competition policy researchers and thought leaders. An initial component may involve interviews with members of the five previous Max Bell Policy Lab teams that worked on Competition Bureau-sponsored projects, highlighting their research, experiences, and what they learned about applying a competition lens to policy problems. The RA will have an opportunity to contribute creatively to the podcast’s format, research, and interviews. Key Responsibilities Help develop the podcast concept, episode themes, interview formats, and production schedule. Identify prospective guests, prepare background research, and draft interview briefs and questions. Coordinate outreach, scheduling, and logistics for guest interviews. Assist with interviews and, where appropriate, take the lead in interviewing former Max Bell Policy Lab participants. Review interview recordings or transcripts, as useful, to identify key points and compelling excerpts; audio editing and production are not required. Draft episode summaries, show notes, and short promotional materials. Help ensure episodes are clear, engaging, accurate, and accessible to a non-specialist audience. Qualifications Strong oral and written communication skills in English; French is an asset. Interest in public policy, economics, competition, or communicating complex ideas to broad audiences; competition policy knowledge is an asset, not a requirement. Comfort speaking with interview guests and willingness to lead selected interviews. Excellent organization, initiative, attention to detail, and ability to work independently. Experience with interviewing, journalism, or public-facing communications is an asset, not a requirement. Must be a current MPP student at the Max Bell School of Public Policy. Application Process Please submit a CV and brief statement of interest through McGill Workday by October 21, 2026 Hourly Salary: $25.00 Hours per Week: 4 (Part time) Location: Sherbrooke 680 Supervisor: Professor Position Start Date: 2026-10-26 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Sherbrooke-680/MPP-Research-Assistant---Competition-Policy-Research_JR0000082178-1": {
      "title": "MPP Research Assistant — Competition Policy Research",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Applicants must be an MPP student at Max Bell School of Public Policy Job Title: Research Assistant (1 position) Location: Max Bell School of Public Policy, McGill University Supervisor: Matthew Chiasson, J.W. McConnell Visiting Scholar Start Date : Late October 2026 (as soon as possible) End Date : April 30, 2027 Hours: 2–4 hours per week Rate: $25.00/hour Project Title Eternal Vigilance, Principled Pragmatism: Mark Carney and Competition Policy (working title) Position Summary Matthew Chiasson, J.W. McConnell Visiting Scholar at the Max Bell School of Public Policy, is seeking one Research Assistant (RA) to support a research project examining Mark Carney’s contributions to, and thinking about, competition policy over approximately four decades. Drawing on an assembled collection of speeches, publications, interviews, testimony, and other primary sources from different stages of Carney’s career, the project aims to trace the evolution and underlying continuity of his views on competition, markets, economic institutions, and the role of government. The RA will help organize and analyze historical source material and produce research notes, source summaries, literature reviews, and other supporting materials for a research project whose ultimate format remains flexible. Key Responsibilities Review and systematically catalogue a substantial collection of primary sources, including speeches, interviews, publications, and testimony. Identify and extract passages relating to competition, market structure, market dynamism, regulation, and the role of the state. Develop a chronology and thematic framework to trace how the relevant ideas evolved across different career stages. Conduct supplementary research in academic literature, economic policy history, and relevant public records. Prepare source summaries, annotated bibliographies, timelines, and short analytical memos. Help assess themes, tensions, and continuities across the material while distinguishing direct statements from interpretation. Assist with fact-checking, citation management, and editing of research outputs, as needed. Qualifications Strong analytical research and writing skills in English; French is an asset. Interest in economic policy, intellectual history, political economy, economic thought, public institutions, or competition policy; competition policy knowledge is an asset, not a requirement. Comfort working with historical and contemporary primary sources and synthesizing large amounts of information. Careful attention to sourcing, nuance, accuracy, and competing interpretations. Ability to organize research systematically and work independently. Experience with academic databases, citation management, and responsible use of AI-supported research tools is an asset. Must be a current MPP student at the Max Bell School of Public Policy. Application Process Please submit a CV and brief statement of interest through McGill Workday by October 21, 2026. Hourly Salary: $25.00 Hours per Week: 4 (Part time) Location: Sherbrooke 680 Supervisor: Professor Position Start Date: 2026-10-26 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bronfman-Building/Office-Assistant_JR0000082384": {
      "title": "Office Assistant",
