    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Education-Building/Undergraduate-Research-Assistant--Thort-_JR0000081115": {
      "title": "Undergraduate Research Assistant (Théorêt)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Job Description: We are seeking a highly motivated undergraduate student to support a mixed-methods study on controlling behaviors in adolescent dating relationships. The successful candidate will assist across all phases of the project, from literature review through knowledge mobilization activities. This is an initial trial period, with the possibility of extension for up to two years based on performance and funding.   Responsibilities: Conduct literature reviews Assist with study preparation, including survey programming (e.g., Qualtrics) Support data collection, including administering in-class surveys Transcribe qualitative data and assist with data cleaning Contribute to preliminary data analyses Assist with other tasks as assigned by the PI   Qualifications: Currently enrolled undergraduate student in Psychology or a related field Excellent academic standing (GPA of 3.3 or above) Prior research experience (quantitative or qualitative) Availability during class hours to administer in-class surveys Prior experience working with adolescents is an asset Familiarity with Qualtrics, SPSS, or NVivo is an asset Fluency in English and French preferred Application Instructions : Please Upload the following documents in your application: Cover Letter CV Updated (unofficial) Transcript Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $19.02 Hours per Week: 5 (Part time) Location: Education Building Supervisor: Assistant Professor Position Start Date: 2026-09-27 Position End Date: 2026-12-19 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-21 This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Hosmer-House/Course-Lecturer-OCC1-545--F26-_JR0000082364-1": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer OCC1 545 (F26)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). OCC1 545 Therapeutic Strategies in OT 1 Le candidat.e sera responsable de l’enseignement associé à ce cours. Cela comprendra la prestation du contenu et l’évaluation liées aux activités d’enseignement, ainsi que la responsabilité de répondre aux besoins des étudiants dans le cadre de ce cours, conformément aux directives du coordonnateur (trice) du cours. Il ou elle pourra également contribuer au développement du cours sous la supervision de ce coordonnateur(trice). The candidate will be responsible for teaching associated with this course. This will include content delivery and grading associated with teaching assignments as well as being responsible for meeting student needs within this course, as assigned by the Course Coordinator. They may also contribute to course development under the oversight of the Course Coordinator. Teaching Qualification Requirements: Éducation / Education Maîtrise ou doctorat avec un diplôme donnant accès à l'exercice de la profession d'ergothérapeute Master or PhD degree that grants licensure as an occupational therapist Expérience / Experience Doit avoir une expérience clinique en tant qu'ergothérapeute avec une population adulte présentant des troubles musculosquelettiques avec une expérience préalable dans l'enseignement universitaire Must have clinical experience as an occupational therapist with an adult population with musculoskeletal conditions and previous teaching experience at a university level. Autre / Other Doit posséder de l’expérience dans la création de cours, l’enseignement et l’évaluation des étudiants dans le cadre du cours OCC1 545. Cela inclut les HOW, les cours magistraux, les CBAs et les activités de simulation. Doit être titulaire d’un permis de l’OEQ. Must have experience in the course creation, delivery and student assessment within OCC1 545. This includes HOWs, lectures, CBAs and simulation activities. Must be licensed with the OEQ. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hiring Unit: School of Physical and Occupational Therapy Course Title: Therapeutic Strategies in OT 1 Subject Code: OCC1 545 Location: TBD Schedule: TBD Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-16 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/3605-de-la-Montagne/Academic-Affairs-Officer_JR0000081966": {
      "title": "Academic Affairs Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: The Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences (FMHS) Academic Affairs Office provides expertise, support and guidance to academic staff, academic leaders, and administrative staff in the Faculty to ensure the recruitment, support, development, and recognition of academic staff performing at the highest standards of excellence, across all spheres of academic work and all phases of their careers.   The Academic Affairs Office serves as the liaison from the academic units in the FMHS to the offices of the Provost, Academic Personnel, and the Secretariat. The Academic Affairs Office is led by the Dean and Director, Academic Affairs.   The Academic Affairs Officer working in the Academic Affairs Office reviews the work submitted by the Academic Affairs Administrators (AAA) working in the faculty’s department and units and ensures the quality, accuracy, and timeliness of the documents before final approval by the Dean.      Reporting to the Director of the Academic Affairs office, the incumbent is responsible for the recruitment, appointments and remuneration of the ranked (both M.D. and PhD faculty) and unranked academic staff for the Faculty of Medicine.  The Academic Affairs Officer advises, supports and trains the Academic Affairs Administrators working in the faculty’s department and units in the context of the applicable University, hospital and provincial government (Ministry of Health) regulations, policies, procedures and collective agreements.  Primary Responsibilities: Guide the AAA Administrators, Deanery leadership, Chairs, Directors, faculty members and administrative staff with respect to regulations, policies and procedures governing the entire faculty lifecycle.  This includes the academic recruitment process, medical manpower, immigration, academic appointments, compensation, leaves, termination, and retirement.  Ensures consistency, equity and compliance across the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences.    Foster and maintain excellent working relationships with stakeholders. Communicate and collaborate with the Faculty AAAs and the Academic Personnel Office to ensure that standards are met, and that information and record-keeping is complete, consistent, and transparent to all.  Meets with the Academic Affairs administrators on a weekly basis to ensure alignment of activities.   Analyze and troubleshoot requests from across the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences.  Provides guidance on a wide variety of issues that involve the University’s regulations, academic policies and procedures and collective agreements that cover academic positions.    Assists with the annual academic salary policy exercise.  Provide information to administrators and faculty members regarding eligibility and the process.   Responds to all incoming academic recruitment requests from AAA’s, Chairs and Directors for any academic position type. Analyzes and troubleshoots requests. Probes for additional information. Identifies discrepancies and advises Faculty AAA’s on appropriate course of action and/or corrective measures necessary in order to resolve the situation.   Liaises with Faculty AAA’s, the Academic Personnel Office, central Human Resources, other areas of the Faculty, the McGill Research and Restricted Funds Office, and the McGill Office of Sponsored Research to resolve issues.  Ensures compliance with the appropriate collective agreement (MCLIU).   Evaluates draft job descriptions for Academic Associates positions.  Discusses position requirements and qualifications, probes for additional information to support the creation of the position.  Provides guidance and advice on the posting and selection procedures.  When required instructs AAA staff on corrective action needed to proceed.   Proofreads, analyzes and corrects all contracts of employment received from the AAA’s to ensure accuracy and compliance with applicable University, FMHS, hospital and government regulations, policies, procedures and collective agreements.  When required, instructs AAA on corrective action needed to proceed with recruitment.   Ensures that financial agreements for salary partnerships with our affiliated hospital research institutes comply with FMHS standards in order to avoid legally binding financial commitments and appointments for which the FMHS should not be held liable.   Verifies and approves all academic appointment forms received from AAA’s and ensures compliance with all relevant University, hospital, government and external agencies policies and regulations related to appointments, compensation, conditions of employment, and immigration status.  When required instructs AAA’s on corrective action needed to proceed.   Audits all financial aspects of appointment forms.  Ensures accuracy with regards to salary distribution for appointments with multiple salary sources (operating funds, restricted funds, internal and external salary awards, stipends, endowed chairs, third party chargeback arrangements).  Ensures salary sources correspond to those previously approved and budgeted.  Ensures compliance with the intended purpose of the funds being used and that the compensation is an allowable expense on the fund.    When required instructs AAA’s on corrective action needed to proceed.   Provides administrative support for the appointments and remuneration of Vice-Deans, Associate Deans, Assistant Deans, Chairs, Directors of Schools and Directors of major Centres and units within the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences:  https://www.mcgill.ca/medhealthsci/schools-departments .   Trains and mentors AAO and AAA staff on these responsibilities and duties. Identifies training needs and makes recommendations.  Participates in the development of tools for the FMHS and departmental administration.  Presents at formal training sessions for administrators that work at the unit level. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Experience working in academic affairs and or University administration.  Results-oriented with demonstrated ability to effectively plan and review work, collaborate with others, and work successfully in a group setting. Excellent interpersonal skills to establish and maintain effective working relationships with stakeholders at all levels. Ability to multi-task in a fast-paced environment and to carry on simultaneous projects.  Ability to manage and respect competing deadlines and to adapt to changing circumstances.  Client-focused with strong problem-solving, priority-setting and organizational skills.  Proven attention to detail and analytical skills to understand, interpret and apply complex academic policies and procedures.  Demonstrated ability to work in a computerized environment using word-processing, databases, spreadsheets and presentation software.  Demonstrated oral and written communication skills in both English and French.   As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 4 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 05) $73,470.00 - $91,840.00 - $110,210.00 Job Profile: MPEX-HRM2G - Academic Affairs - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Director, Academic Affairs Position End Date (If applicable): 2027-10-05 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-28 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Sherbrooke-680/Lead-Auditor_JR0000082095": {
      "title": "Lead Auditor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Reporting to the Director, Internal Audit, and working closely with the Executive Director, Internal Audit & Enterprise Risk Management, the Lead Auditor is a member of McGill’s Internal Audit function, operating as the third line and reporting functionally to the Audit and Risk Committee of the Board of Governors. In conformance with The IIA’s Global Internal Audit Standards™ and the University’s Internal Audit Charter, the function strengthens McGill’s ability to create, protect and sustain value by providing the Audit and Risk Committee and Senior Management with independent, risk-based and objective assurance, advice, insight and foresight. The Lead Auditor independently leads complex, high-exposure assurance, advisory and investigation engagements aligned with the Institutional Priorities, and contributes to the University’s Enterprise Risk Management (ERM) Program, for which Internal Audit is the designated ERM Facilitator under the ERM Policy. Primary Responsibilities: Independently leads high-exposure and complex internal audit engagements across all phases — planning, execution and reporting — exercising professional judgment on scope, approach and escalation. Oversees and reviews the work performed by the Internal Auditors assigned to the engagements to ensure quality standards are met, and coaches and mentors Internal Auditors to build their technical and professional capabilities. Prioritizes, plans, organizes and executes internal audit projects in accordance with the engagement approach and risk-based methodology. Contributes to the development of the annual risk assessment and Internal Audit Engagement Plan, aligning proposed engagements with the Institutional Risk Register, the Institutional Priorities, and bringing forward emerging trends and risks for consideration. Develops and implements methodologies, policies, programs and systems that support the unit’s operations, including the integration of data analytics, Lean Six Sigma and emerging technologies (e.g., AI-assisted audit tools) to enhance audit efficiency and impact. Assesses the adequacy and effectiveness of internal control systems by performing risk-based testing procedures and determining the appropriate scope of analysis. Performs root-cause analysis to identify systemic issues beyond individual control gaps, taking fraud risk into account in the design of engagement procedures. Recommends practical, forward-looking process improvements and engages with management as it develops action plans to address internal control deficiencies and strengthen risk management practices, while management retains ownership of the design and implementation of corrective actions. Follows up and validates the implementation status of management action plans arising from audit engagements and reports on outstanding items. Prepares and finalizes the internal engagement report for distribution to the Senior Administration and the Audit Committee. Presents engagement results and recommendations directly to senior management and, as required, to the Audit and Risk Committee, in support of the Director and Executive Director. Leads special assignments and examinations, including investigations, exercising independence, objectivity and discretion, and coordinating as appropriate with Legal Services, Human Resources and the Chair of the Audit and Risk Committee. Contributes to the Quality Assurance and Improvement Program (QAIP), including engagement quality reviews, periodic self-assessments, performance metrics and preparation for external quality assessments Maintains up-to-date professional and technical knowledge by attending workshops, reviewing relevant publications, building networks and participating in professional associations, and represents the function externally (e.g., IIA Montreal, IIA Canada) to strengthen McGill’s standing within the profession. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities More than 3 years’ experience in Internal Audit or related field. A recognized professional designation (CPA, CIA, CRMA or equivalent), in progress or completed is considered an asset. Excellent communication skills in both English and French (written and spoken). Demonstrated ability to multi-task and to organize and prioritize work to meet deadlines. Must be client-focused and service-oriented with a proven ability to deal with people at all levels. Proven ability to transmit and receive information accurately when working with clients and deal effectively and diplomatically in delicate situations. Proven ability to take initiative and work autonomously and as part of a team. Experience working with non-profit organizations, universities or other higher education institutions. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 4 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience with Bachelor’s Degree / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 05) $73,470.00 - $91,840.00 - $110,210.00 Job Profile: MPEX-FIN2C - Audit, Risk, Insurance & Investments - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Director Internal Audit Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-27 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Strathcona-AnatDent/Teaching-Assistant---INDS-426--Anatomy-for-Surgeons_JR0000082346": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - INDS 426: Anatomy for Surgeons",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Anatomy and Cell Biology Course Title: Anatomy for Surgeons Course Code: INDS 426 Estimated Number of Positions: 4 Total Hours of Work per Term: 23 Hiring Unit: Anatomy & Cell Biology Course Title and Course Number: INDS 426 - Anatomy for Surgeons Estimated Number of Positions: 2 Total Hours of Work per Term: 23 Position Description: Teaching Assistants will be responsible for guiding students through detailed cadaveric dissections of various regions of the human body. Teaching Assistants will further assist in demonstrating and discussing clinical cases in laboratory sessions. Teaching Assistants are responsible for reinforcing the laboratory rules and code of conduct in the lab, to oversee all safety precautions regarding use of all dissection tools including bone saws as well as proper use of specimen preservative solutions. Qualifications:   Must have previous cadaveric dissection-based laboratory experience and strong knowledge in all anatomy systems of the human body. Strong dissection and effective communication skills are essential. Prior dissection-based teaching experience is an asset. Effective communication skills in English (spoken and written) are essential. Communication in French is an asset. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-04 Position End Date: 2027-05-07 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-02 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Strathcona-AnatDent/Teaching-Assistant---INDS-116--Medical-Histology-Lab_JR0000082341": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - INDS 116: Medical Histology Lab",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Anatomy and Cell Biology Course Title: Medical Histology Lab Course Code: INDS 116 Estimated Number of Positions: 3 Total Hours of Work per Term: 26 Hiring Unit: Anatomy & Cell Biology Course Title and Course Number: INDS 116 - Medical Histology Labs Estimated Number of Positions: 2 Total Hours of Work per Term: 26 Position Description: Teaching Assistants will help guide and answer questions from the medical and dental students during ten Medical Histology laboratory sessions, which are part of the new Faculty of Medicine and Dentistry curriculum. Qualifications: Must have previously taken and passed ANAT 663 (Graduate Histology) or equivalent experience. Effective communication skills in English are essential. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-04 Position End Date: 2027-05-07 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-02 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Strathcona-AnatDent/Teaching-Assistant---INDS-114--Digestion---Metabolism_JR0000082317": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - INDS 114: Digestion & Metabolism",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Anatomy and Cell Biology Course Title: Digestion & Metabolism Course Code: INDS 114 Estimated Number of Positions: 5 Total Hours of Work per Term: 24 Hiring Unit: Anatomy and Cell Biology Course Title and Course Number: INDS 114: Digestion and Metabolism Estimated Number of Positions: 3 Total Hours of Work per Term: 24 Position Summary: Teaching Assistants will assist first year medical in anatomy dissections (16 hours). Teaching Assistants must demonstrate an excellent ability to teach human anatomy in the anatomy lab and effectively guide students in abdominal dissections. Teaching Assistants are responsible for reinforcing the laboratory rules and code of conduct in the lab, to oversee all safety precautions regarding use of all dissection tools. Teaching Assistants will also participate in the supervision and facilitation of anatomy review time (4 hours) as well as the preparation (2 hours), invigilation and grading (2 hours) of the anatomy lab exam. Availability is required on the following dates and times: February 1st (Monday): 12:30 - 2:30 pm February 3rd (Wednesday): 12:30 - 2:30 pm February 4th (Thursday): 1:00 - 3:00 pm February 5th (Friday): 1:30 - 3:30 pm February 8th (Monday): 12:30 - 2:30 pm February 10th (Wednesday): 1:00 - 3:00 pm February 11th (Thursday): 1:00 - 3:00 pm February 12th (Friday): 1:30 - 3:30 pm February 19th (Friday) Self-Directed: 1:00 - 3:00 pm February 19th (Friday) Self-Directed: 3:00 - 5:00 pm February 24th (Wednesday) Lab exam set-up: time TBD February 24th (Wednesday) Lab exam: 12:30-3:30 pm Qualifications Must have dissection experience and demonstrate advanced dissection skills on topics relevant to the course description. Strong knowledge of abdominal anatomy, experience with abdominal dissection and good communication skills are essential. Effective communication skills in English are essential. Communication in French is an asset. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-04 Position End Date: 2027-05-07 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-02 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/James-Admin-Bldg/Human-Resources-Advisor_JR0000082264": {
      "title": "Human Resources Advisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: As an HR Advisor, this role serves as a trusted partner to academic and administrative leaders within the VPRI. This role will be reporting to the HR manager, at first, who will be taking a maternity leave early in the contract. The incumbent will then report directly to the Director of Operations. The position provides a full range of HR services, staffing, compensation, benefits administration, organizational design and training and most importantly, labour/employee relations. It acts primarily as a resource for managers, offering guidance on policies and collective agreements, and contribute to shaping HR initiatives that align with the unit’s goals. The incumbent will be working independently, but alongside a team of HR professionals who will require their input on conflict negotiation, change management and niche recruitment opportunities/difficulties.   The Human Resources Advisor is responsible for the effective delivery of Human Resources services to employees and managers within the VPRI. They will provide professional guidance and expertise to managers and clients, regarding labour relations, dispute resolution and grievances. The incumbent will develop and implement human resources policies and programs to maximize unit effectiveness; integrate human resources strategy into policy and planning frameworks; promote organizational effectiveness and cultural change; implement effective recruitment strategies to attract top talent. Undertake special projects as needed. Primary Responsibilities: Provide guidance to the HR Administrator and Talent Acquisition Administrator on files (e.g. employee disputes, establishing internal protocols, interpreting the Collective Agreement, etc). Represent the unit at Human Resources-related meetings, including Labour Relations or working committees, as required, and contribute to HR projects, working groups, and initiatives aimed at improving HR practices. Provide professional expertise and advice to managers and directors on the application and interpretation of human resources policies and procedures, collective agreements, the handling of grievances, and institutional procedures, referring complex matters to Labour and Employee Relations as appropriate. Work alongside Labor Advisor and Employee Relations throughout the grievance and arbitration processes. Responsible for reviewing new position requests, re-matching, requests for hiring salaries, salary adjustments for assigned groups of employees/clients. Ensure consistent application of the various policies and internal equity within area. Conduct job audits. Manage the performance dialogue process by providing guidance to managers and directors on documenting objectives, conducting performance discussions, addressing performance concerns, and identifying training and development needs often related to performance improvement plans. Advise on performance issues and performance improvement plans. Develop training, succession planning, talent management programs, and other organizational development workshops, to motivate and develop staff to their full potential. Collaborate with the HR Benefits and Disability Management Office, and managers to lead the effective administration of disability and leave cases; assist with coordinating short- and long-term disability processes and providing information on leave entitlements. Oversee a varied selection of HR responsibilities from the accurate implementation of performance dialogue; succession planning; as well as performance improvement plans and job abolitions. Provide support to direct supervisor, in the preparation of budgetary planning and recommend budget allocations as it relates to staffing. Support operational recruitment activities for the VPRI, including preparing job postings and advising management on recruitment needs, and liaising with HR central HR. Develop objectives and make recommendations for optimization of office space. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Experience working as a Human Resources Advisor: Manage complex employee relations matters, including workplace conflicts, grievances, investigations, and internal dispute resolution; Lead organizational change initiatives, including position abolitions, workforce restructuring, ensuring compliance with employment legislation and organizational policies. Fluently bilingual, English and French, spoken and written. Ability to communicate with all levels of staff in an effective and respectful manner. Holds a recognized qualification in HR or industrial relations Must be a CRHA member. Ability to multitask and work on several projects at the same time, under strict deadlines while ensuring strong attention to detail. Strong problem solving, diplomacy, and organizational skills. Comfortable working with Microsoft Office 365 (Excel, Outlook, PowerPoint, Teams, SharePoint). Experience with HR Information Systems like Workday is an asset. Proven experience in working in a unionized environment Demonstrated ability to prioritize tasks and meet deadlines while managing multiple, often conflicting, demands. Prior experience working as an HR Advisor (3 year+), or within McGill University (1year+), is considered a strong asset. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4(qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 05) $73,470.00 - $91,840.00 - $110,210.00 Job Profile: MPEX-HRM2D - Human Resources Generalist - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Human Resources & Talent Management Manager Position End Date (If applicable): 2027-10-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-28 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Strathcona-AnatDent/Teaching-Assistant--INDS-114-PROSEC---Digestion---Metabolism_JR0000082335": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant: INDS 114 PROSEC - Digestion & Metabolism",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Anatomy and Cell Biology Course Title: PROSEC - Digestion & Metabolism Course Code: INDS 114 Estimated Number of Positions: 5 Total Hours of Work per Term: 0 Hiring Unit: Anatomy and Cell Biology Course Title and Course Number: INDS 114 PROSEC - Digestion & Metabolism Estimated Number of Positions: 2 Total Hours of Work per Term: 9   Position Summary: Teaching Assistants will assist first year dental students in prosection-based anatomy labs (4 hours). Teaching Assistants must demonstrate an excellent ability to teach human anatomy in the anatomy lab and effectively guide students in learning the abdominal region using prosections. Teaching Assistants are responsible for reinforcing the laboratory rules and code of conduct in the lab, to oversee all safety precautions in the anatomy lab as well as proper use of specimen preservative solutions and ensuring proper set-up and clean-up in the lab. Teaching Assistants will also participate in the supervision and facilitation of anatomy review time (2 hours) as well as the preparation (2 hours), invigilation and grading (1 hour) of the anatomy lab exam. Availability is required on the following dates and times: February 2nd (Tuesday): 12:30 - 2:30 pm February 9th (Tuesday): 12:30 - 2:30 pm February 17th (Wednesday): 12:30 - 2:30 pm February 25th (Thursday) Exam setup: time TBD February 26th (Friday) Exam: 1:00 - 2:00 pm Qualifications: Must have previous laboratory experience in human anatomy on topics relevant to the course description. Strong knowledge of abdominal anatomy, and good communication skills are essential. Effective communication skills in English are essential. Communication in French is an asset. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing.   Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-04 Position End Date: 2027-05-07 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-02 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Strathcona-AnatDent/Teaching-Assistant--ANAT-316---Clinical-Human-Visceral-Anatomy_JR0000082017": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant: ANAT 316 - Clinical Human Visceral Anatomy",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Anatomy & Cell Biology Course Title: Clinical Human Visceral Anatomy Course Code: ANAT 316 001 Estimated Number of Positions: 2 Total Hours of Work per Term: 60 Hiring Unit: Anatomy and Cell Biology Course Title and Course Number: ANAT 316 - Clinical Human Visceral Anatomy Estimated Number of Positions: 2 Total Hours of Work per Term: 60   Position Summary: TA Responsibilities: Attend all laboratory sessions fully prepared on weekly topics. Work with large groups of students to illustrate and explain clinical visceral human anatomy using prosections and models. Retrieve, present, and return specimens; ensure proper clean-up and organization of lab stations. Reinforce laboratory rules, code of conduct, and safety procedures related to specimen handling and preservation. Coordinate and assign duties to Undergraduate Course Assistants (UCAs) in collaboration with the course instructor and lab staff. Collect and compile weekly practice questions prepared by UCAs. Support UCAs in teaching activities (models, medical images, cadaveric specimens, etc.) and lead small-group teaching in labs. Assist with lab set-up, grading of laboratory quizzes, and support the midterm review. Contribute to the setup and grading of the final lab exam and help with proctoring the midterm if needed. Lectures will take place Wednesdays and Fridays from 8:35 am - 9:25 am. Labs are scheduled for Thursdays 9:35 am - 11:25 am and Fridays 10:35 am - 12:25 pm.  Qualifications: Must have experience working with human cadaveric samples, with past experience with cadaveric dissection strongly preferred. Must have previously studied gross anatomy related to the head and neck, thoracic, abdominal and pelvic anatomy, for example through successful completion of ANAT 316, ANAT 214, or another course equivalent. Holding a past course assistant position in ANAT 316/214 is a strong asset. Must have demonstrated leadership skills in supervising and guiding undergraduate students to foster a productive and inclusive learning environment. Must have proven organizational skills, with meticulous attention to detail, and a commitment to maintaining accuracy, safety, and inclusivity in all aspects of teaching and administrative responsibilities.   Effective communication skills in English (spoken and written) are essential.   Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-04 Position End Date: 2027-05-07 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-02 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Rutherford-Physics/Teaching-Assistants---Physics-Winter-2027_JR0000082108": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistants - Physics Winter 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Job Title: Physics Teaching Assistants Winter 2027 Hiring Unit: Department of Physics Course Title and Course Number: PHYS 102 - Introductory Physics – Electromagnetism PHYS 142 -Electromagnetism & Optics PHYS 181 - Everyday Physics                                                                                       PHYS 183 -The Milky Way Inside and Out PHYS 186- Astrobiology PHYS 228 - Energy & the Environment                                                                   PHYS 232 - Heat and Waves PHYS 241 - Signal Processing PHYS 242- Electricity and Magnetism PHYS 258 -Experimental Methods 2 PHYS 319 - Introduction to Biophysics PHYS 321 - Data Science & Observational Astrophysics   PHYS 331 + 351 - Topics in Classical Mechanics PHYS 333 + 362 - Thermal and Statistical Physics PHYS 339 - Measurements Laboratory in General Physics PHYS 342 - Electromagnetic Waves PHYS 359 - Advanced Physics Laboratory 1. PHYS 396 – Undergraduate Research Project PHYS 432 - Physics of Fluids PHYS 447 - Applications of Quantum Mechanics PHYS 449 - Majors Research Project PHYS 457 - Honour's Quantum Physics 2 PHYS 459D2 - Research Thesis PHYS 479/489 - Physics Research Project PHYS 514 - General Relativity PHYS 519 - Advanced Biophysics PHYS 559 - Advanced Statistical Mechanics PHYS 562 - Electromagnetic Theory PHYS 567 - Particle Physics PHYS 602 - Introduction to Graduate Studies in Physics 2 PHYS 617 - Selected Topics: Contemporary Physics 2 PHYS 641 - Observational Techniques of Modern Astrophysics PHYS 642- Radiative Processes in Astrophysics PHYS 645 - High Energy Astrophysics PHYS 657 - Classical Condensed Matter PHYS 659 - Experimental Condensed Matter PHYS 660 - Quantum Condensed Matter PHYS 673 – Quantum field Theory 2 PHYS 743 - Very Early Universe Estimated number of positions: 120 Total number of hours per term: 90h   Hourly Salary: According to the collective agreement   Position Start Date: 2027-01-05   Position End Date: 2027-04-19   Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-28     Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University: You must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Must be registered in a graduate program at McGill University. Priority will be given to students enrolled in a graduate program in Geography.   McGill University is committed to equity in employment.   McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing.   Use a personal email when creating an account to submit your application. Do not use @mail.mcgill.ca or @mcgill.ca email accounts when you apply. Position Summary: Teaching assistants (TAs) will perform some or all of the following tasks, in consultation with the course instructor: grading, holding tutorials and possibly office hours for consultation, supervising laboratory sessions, assist with exams, attending lectures, and assisting instructors in other aspects of the course development and delivery. Qualifications: Prior graduate/undergraduate study in the subject of the course. Suitable teaching skills. Must be registered in a graduate program at McGill University. Priority will be given to students enrolled in a graduate program in physics at McGill University. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Strathcona-AnatDent/Teaching-Assistant---ANAT-262_JR0000082011": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - ANAT 262",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Anatomy & Cell Biology Course Title: Intro to Molecular and Cell Biology Course Code: ANAT 262 001 Estimated Number of Positions: 2 Total Hours of Work per Term: 60 Hiring Unit: Anatomy and Cell Biology Course Title and Course Number: ANAT 262 - Intro to Molecular and Cell Biology Estimated Number of Positions: 2 Total Hours of Work per Term: 60 Position Summary: Teaching Assistant should attend classes and provide weekly office hours to students. Help with exam/assignment organization and invigilation including marking of the midterm and final exam. Assist professors with lecture-related tasks. Qualifications: Must have previously taken and passed ANAT 262 or equivalent course. Previous knowledge of cell biology appropriate for this course is required. Good communication skills. Effective communication skills in English are essential. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-04 Position End Date: 2027-05-07 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-02 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
