    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Strathcona-AnatDent/Teaching-Assistant--ANAT-416---Disease--Development-and-Regeneration_JR0000082028": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant: ANAT 416 - Disease, Development and Regeneration",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Anatomy & Cell Biology Course Title: Development, Disease and Regeneration Course Code: ANAT 416 001 Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 130 Hiring Unit: Anatomy and Cell Biology Course Title and Course Number: ANAT 416 - Disease, Development and Regeneration Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 130 Position Summary: Teaching Assistant must attend all lectures and manage Slido to collect questions and comments. In conjunction with the Professor, Teaching Assistant must help to mark exams and assignment. Assist professors with lecture-related tasks, including hosting zoom sessions, if required. Qualifications: Must have previously taken and successfully passed ANAT 416, or do graduate study in developmental biology. Undergraduate degree in related field. Prior Teaching Assistant experience is an asset. Effective communication skills in English are essential. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-04 Position End Date: 2027-05-07 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-02 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Macdonald-Eng-Bldg/Research-Assistant-2_JR0000081988": {
      "title": "Research Assistant 2",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: We currently have an opening position as a Research Assistant 2 (RA2) working with Prof. Fangzhou Albert Liu in the Geomechanics and System Resilience Laboratory (GeoSRL). The Research Assistant will support experimental and numerical research on tailings and geotextile-based systems, including their interaction with geomembranes and geosynthetic clay liners. The work combines material characterization, constitutive modelling and finite-element analysis to assess drainage, consolidation, differential settlement and long-term performance of tailings containment and closure systems. Responsibilities: Reviews scientific literature on tailings consolidation, geotextile filtration and drainage, and geosynthetic barrier and cover performance. Supports laboratory testing of tailings and geotextile-tailings interfaces, and compiles laboratory and available field datasets for material characterization and model calibration. Develops, calibrates and validates finite-element models of tailings-geosynthetic systems, including large deformation, contact and interface slip, and seepage. Implements and evaluates constitutive relationships for tailings and geosynthetics, including stress-dependent behaviour, creep and strain-rate effects. Quantifies the effects of consolidation and differential settlement on geotextile strains, interface stresses, drainage performance and leakage through associated barriers. Conducts sensitivity analyses of tailings variability, hydraulic properties, interface resistance and geosynthetic stiffness to identify controls on system performance. Prepares reproducible datasets, model documentation, technical reports and manuscript drafts, and collaborates with students and research partners Other Qualifying Skills and Abilities: Experience with finite-element modelling of geotechnical or geosynthetic systems using Abaqus or equivalent software, including nonlinear material behaviour and contact/interface analysis. Knowledge of the hydraulic and mechanical behaviour of geosynthetics, including geotextiles, geomembranes or geosynthetic clay liners. Ability to analyse laboratory and field measurements, verify numerical models, and document data, assumptions and uncertainty with attention to accuracy and reproducibility. Strong technical writing, independent problem-solving and collaborative research skills; publications in a relevant research area are an asset. Experience implementing and calibrating constitutive models; familiarity with user-defined material subroutines and Fortran or equivalent programming is an asset. Familiarity with tailings consolidation, seepage, effective-stress behaviour or critical-state soil mechanics is an asset. Minimum requirement: MSc degree; Masters degree in civil, geotechnical or geoenvironmental engineering, or a closely related discipline, with research experience in geosynthetics, soil-structure interaction or computational geomechanics. Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4  on a scale of 0-4. Posting expiry date: October 23, 2026 Supervisor: Prof. Fangzhou Albert Liu Schedule: Full time, 35 hours, schedule to be determined with supervisor. Pay: $32.15/hour Dates: November 1, 2026 - April 30, 2027 This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. Hourly Salary: $32.15 Hours per Week: 35 (Full time) Location: Macdonald Eng. Bldg. Supervisor: Associate Professor Position Start Date: 2026-11-01 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-23 This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Strathcona-AnatDent/Teaching-Assistant--ANAT-514---Advanced-Human-Anatomy-Laboratory_JR0000082029": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant: ANAT 514 - Advanced Human Anatomy Laboratory",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Anatomy & Cell Biology Course Title: Advanced Human Anatomy Laboratory Course Code: ANAT 514 001 Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 60 Hiring Unit: Anatomy and Cell Biology Course Title and Course Number: ANAT 514 - Advanced Human Anatomy Laboratory Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 60 Position Summary: Teaching Assistant will be responsible for guiding and assisting students in developing anatomy laboratory-based teaching resources, using dissection, videography, photography, 3D printing, and other techniques. Advanced dissection skills are essential. Teaching Assistant will actively participate in assessing students' progress and evaluating their final projects. Qualifications: Must have dissection experience and demonstrate advanced dissection skills. Must have previously taken and successfully passed other anatomy courses at McGill (ANAT 214, 314, 315 or 316). Prior Teaching Assistant experience is an asset. Effective communication skills in English are essential. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-04 Position End Date: 2027-05-07 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-02 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Strathcona-AnatDent/Teaching-Assistant--ANAT-541---Cell-and-Molecular-Biology-of-Aging_JR0000082030": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant: ANAT 541 - Cell and Molecular Biology of Aging",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Anatomy & Cell Biology Course Title: Cell and Molecular Biology of Aging Course Code: ANAT 541 001 Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 90 Hiring Unit: Anatomy and Cell Biology Course Title and Course Number: ANAT 541 - Cell and Molecular Biology of Aging Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 90 Position Summary: Prepare and give 30-minute lecture on grant writing/techniques (2.5 hours).  Answer student inquires with respect to assignments and be available for office hours or online discussion (7.5 hours). Teaching Assistant will be preparing and grading 5 quizzes (20 hours). Teaching Assistant will perform grading of 30 grant proposals (60 hours). Qualifications: Have taken and excelled in ANAT 541 or have significant research experience in one or more topics covered in ANAT 541. Effective communication skills in English are essential. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-04 Position End Date: 2027-05-07 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-02 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Wong-Building/Research-Assistant-2_JR0000082311": {
      "title": "Research Assistant 2",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). This part-time Research Assistant 2 position is in the research lab of Prof Kirk Bevan, in the Department of Mining and Materials Engineering Position Summary: The Research Assistant 2 will support an NSERC-funded project on quantum point defect design from first principles. The incumbent will carry out data collection and analysis using established protocols on quantum point defects in nitride semiconductors, help prepare reports and manuscripts, and provide technical guidance to student research assistants. Responsibilities: Conduct research activities using established protocols.  This includes quantum point defect calculations using first-principles methods. Clean, manage and analyze data using Python scripts. Maintain research documentation records. Prepare summaries, figures and draft sections of reports and manuscripts. Coordinate day-to-day project tasks and train or guide student research assistants. Participate in team meetings and present progress updates. Qualifications: Master's degree in Materials Engineering. Extensive experience with quantum point defect calculations from first principles, knowledge of crystal field theory for point defects, ability to perform excited state point defect calculations, ability to train machine learning interatomic potentials from first principles, detailed knowledge of point defects in aluminum and gallium nitride semiconductors. Proficiency in the Vienna Ab initio Simulation Package (VASP). Strong organization, attention to detail and written communication skills. Have published at least one scientific manuscript using first-principles calculations. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $32.15 Hours per Week: 18 (Part time) Location: Wong Building Supervisor: Associate Professor Position Start Date: 2026-10-14 Position End Date: 2027-02-15 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-21 This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Montreal-Neuro/Research-Associate_JR0000082208": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The candidate will work independently on basic and applied research projects in the laboratory of Dr. Fellows at the Montreal Neurological Institute (MNI), Faculty of Medicine, McGill University. This work will focus on investigating the brain basis of decision-making and related executive processes, using multiple measurement techniques including studies of humans with focal brain lesions and neuroimaging in healthy and clinical populations (e.g. Parkinson Disease, people living with HIV). The candidate will also manage the McGill Cognitive Neuroscience Research Registry, a crucial support for focal lesion research across the University. They will work collaboratively with other investigators, students and research staff. The candidate is expected to develop local expertise in advanced structure-function imaging analysis methods suited for use in clinical populations, notably including those with focal brain lesions. He/she will work with collaborative teams on the multidisciplinary aspects of the research projects and will analyze data, write drafts of manuscripts, and contribute to the writing of grants and protocols. He/she will facilitate research meetings and other local events aiming to strengthen multi-disciplinary cognitive neuroscience research. The candidate will also participate in the supervision of graduate, undergraduate and summer trainees and contribute to laboratory management, helping to assure the efficient, ethical and responsible day-to-day conduct of research within the Fellows lab. EDUCATION/EXPERIENCE PhD in Neuroscience or Psychology. Post-doctoral training or work experience in a relevant field will be an asset. Previous experience working with clinical or healthy elderly participants also would be an asset. Applicants should be: Experts in human neuroscience methods: experimental neuropsychology, behavioral methods for human subjects’ research in healthy and clinical populations, eye-tracking, structural and/or functional MRI analysis, brain anatomy and connectivity. Very good knowledge of programming in Matlab, Python, and R or similar environments; databasing; and statistical methods; computational modeling or health outcomes research experience would be an asset. Other Qualifying Skills/Abilities Excellent written and verbal communication skills (English essential, French an asset). Ability to guide graduate students and summer trainees. Ability to work in a multidisciplinary team environment. Strong organization skills, attention to detail and priority setting skills. Ability to work autonomously in a complex, changing, deadline-oriented environment. Advanced knowledge of cognitive neuroscience. Very good knowledge of MRI analysis techniques. Knowledge of good research/clinical practice. A strong publication record in cognitive neuroscience or a closely related field. This position is covered by the AMURE collective agreement: http://www.aerum-amure.ca/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/AMURE-2023-2027.pdf Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 4 on a scale of 0-4. Hourly Salary: $35.37 Hours per Week: 40 (Full time) Location: Montreal Neuro. Supervisor: Professor Position Start Date: 2026-11-01 Position End Date: 2027-03-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-21 This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/MacDonald-Harrington/Teaching-Assistant-ARCH-517---Winter2027_JR0000082315": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant ARCH 517 - Winter2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: The Peter Guo-hua Fu School of Architecture Course Title: Sustainable Residential Development Course Code: ARCH 517 Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 100 Responsibilities: Grading papers Attending all lectures and presentations Interaction with students Recording attendance and participation Qualifications: Background in housing and architecture. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-04 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-05 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McGill-College-2001/Research-Assistant-1_JR0000082190": {
      "title": "Research Assistant 1",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). POSITION SUMMARY The Department of Equity, Ethics and Policy is seeking a Research Assistant 1 to support the research project exploring how clinical investigators critically assess and interpret evidence for early-phase clinical trials. The project aims to identify the key factors that inform risk-benefit analyses in early-phase trials and contribute to the development of evidence-based frameworks for decision-making. The successful candidate will join a collaborative research team and assist with qualitative research activities, data analysis, manuscript preparation, and research coordination.   RESPONSIBILITIES Under the supervision of the Research Supervisor and in collaboration with the research team, the Research Assistant 1 will: Execute research activities using established qualitative research methodologies and protocols. Coordinate, conduct, transcribe, and code qualitative interviews with clinical investigators and other stakeholders involved in early-phase clinical trials. Assist with participant recruitment, scheduling, data management, and the compilation of research documentation. Analyze and interpret qualitative research data and assist in the preparation of reports, summaries, presentations, and manuscripts. Contribute to the drafting and submission of manuscripts and assist with responses to reviewer comments. Support the development and implementation of new research projects examining evidence assessment and risk-benefit decision-making in clinical research. Recommend improvements to data collection, coding, and analytical processes as appropriate. Provide technical guidance and training to students and new research personnel on study procedures and qualitative research methods. Present research findings and participate in journal clubs, laboratory meetings, and other academic activities. Perform administrative tasks related to the project, including organizing group meetings, maintaining research records, and supporting day-to-day laboratory operations. Be responsible for the quality and integrity of data collection, coding, and research outputs associated with the project.   QUALIFICATIONS Possess a Bachelor's degree in Health Sciences, Public Health, Epidemiology, Psychology, Sociology, Bioethics, Clinical Research, or a related discipline. Minimum of two years of relevant experience in a research project or academic research environment. An equivalent combination of education and experience may be considered. Experience conducting qualitative research, including interviews, transcription, coding, and analysis. Experience preparing research reports, manuscripts, presentations, or other scholarly outputs is considered an asset. Knowledge of qualitative research methodologies and data analysis techniques. Strong analytical, organizational, and problem-solving skills. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Ability to work independently and collaboratively within a multidisciplinary research team. Proficiency with Microsoft Office applications; experience with qualitative analysis software is an asset. Hiring Unit: Department of Equity, Ethics and Policy   Number of Positions: 1   Supervisor: Professor Jonathan Kimmelman   Total Hours Per Week: 30 hours per week (part-time)   Hourly Salary: $31.31   Location: 2001 McGill College « Connaissance du français et de l'anglais: L'Université McGill est une université de langue anglaise où les tâches quotidiennes peuvent nécessiter une communication verbale et écrite en anglais.  Le niveau d’anglais requis pour ce poste a été déterminé comme étant de niveau #4 (qualificatif) sur une échelle de 0-4.» “Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4 (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4.” In accordance with Canadian and Quebec law, the present offer is conditional upon your obtaining the appropriate work authorization from any or all of the following as applicable: Citizenship & Immigration Canada, Immigration Quebec and Human Resources & Skills Development Canada (HRSDC).   Hourly Salary: $31.31 Hours per Week: 30 (Part time) Location: McGill College 2001 Supervisor: Professor Position Start Date: 2026-10-13 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-20 This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Strathcona-AnatDent/Teaching-Assistant--ANAT-542---TEM-of-Biological-Samples_JR0000082031": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant: ANAT 542 - TEM of Biological Samples",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Anatomy & Cell Biology Course Title: Transmission Electron Microscopy of Biological Samples Course Code: ANAT 542 001 Estimated Number of Positions: 2 Total Hours of Work per Term: 55 Hiring Unit: Anatomy and Cell Biology Course Title and Course Number: ANAT 542 - TEM of Biological Samples Estimated Number of Positions: 2 Total Hours of Work per Term: 55 Position Summary: Run EM labs including microscope time or image processing, answer student e mails and questions, prepare labs, correct lab reports, help organize and invigilate mid-term exam and final exams. Qualifications: Priority will be given to the graduate students who took and passed ANAT 542 successfully. Electron microscopy experience and demonstrated knowledge of the software image processing (IMOD, XMIPP), Linux. Effective communication skills in English are essential. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-04 Position End Date: 2027-05-07 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-02 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
