    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Various/Course-monitors---Dialogue-McGill-s-Language-Training-Program--Anglais-Sant-_JR0000082053": {
      "title": "Course monitors - Dialogue McGill's Language Training Program \"Anglais Santé\"",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nPart time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The Dialogue McGill project, funded by Health Canada since 2004 under the Official Languages Health Program, is focused on initiatives to build and maintain the capacity of bilingual health and social services professionals in Quebec to improve health care access for Canadian official language minority communities. Course monitors provide guidance and support to a group of second language learners enrolled in the English workshops for healthcare and social services professionals and intake staff. The position requires a commitment of 50 to 100 hours over the course of a 10-week session and must be available for at least one evening per week to answer student calls and emails. Main responsibilities : Monitors learners’ progress and encourage student participation by creating a dynamic, positive and memorable experience Answers emails from learners with a delay of no more than 24 hours Adjusts language usage to suit learners’ level of understanding Refers learners to appropriate resources for technical support, language development, etc. Sends friendly module reminder emails Helps learners with access to workshop components (e.g., finding documents, understanding instructions, etc.) Highlights important due dates, events, etc. Motivates learners to complete lessons, modules, quizzes Provides feedback (simple suggestions and guidance) on the proper way to say something Redirects learners to ask for more elaborate feedback & practice from instructor in the English Conversation workshop Communicates with the instructor as required about student progress Clarifies the differences between the English Online workshop & the English Conversation workshop if needed Creates a no-show list of students who have not accessed the online platform Checks grade book for completed and uncompleted quizzes and follow up as needed Enters final letter grade in grade book in consultation with the academic leadership team Exports final grades at the end of the session and send to the academic leadership team Reports cases of learners’ requesting extensions to the academic leadership team Attends a training session as well as briefing/debriefing meetings with the project’s academic leadership team. Performs various administrative duties as and when needed. Qualifications : At least one year completed in a university program leading to an Education degree Excellent oral and written communication skills in English and French Students currently enrolled in TESL programs preferred            Hourly rate : 20.00$/h Time commitment : 50 -100 hours for a 10-week workshop session. Schedule : Spring/Summer semester: 4 January 2027 – 19 March 2027 The following supporting documents are required : A cover letter & curriculum vitae the names and contact information of three references McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing We thank all applicants for their interest, however, only applicants selected for an interview will be contacted. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Wong-Building/Winter-2027-Teaching-Assistants--Chemical-Engineering_JR0000081349": {
      "title": "Winter 2027 Teaching Assistants: Chemical Engineering",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). These positions are open to McGill University Graduate Students only. Use this application form to apply to any of the positions currently being posted (see the list below). You will have the opportunity to list your ranked top choices of courses for the Winter 2027 semester by uploading the completed TA application form to your application in Workday: https://www.mcgill.ca/hr/files/hr/1_-_graduate_student_application_for_ta_-_english_-_jan_2021_-_fillable.pdf   Upload the application form before you upload your CV. NOTE (Important): 1. Teaching Assistants are required to be on campus to perform their tasks in person.  2. Course Instructors are responsible for assigning tasks to Teaching Assistants. 3. Teaching Assistant tasks include: assisting with undergraduate labs and lab safety training. Hiring Unit: Department of Chemical Engineering Duration of each position: 4-Jan-2027 to 30-Apr-2027 Rate per hour: $38.46 Course Title, Course Number and number of positions and hours: Chemical Engineering Principles II (CHEE204) // 2 x 100 hrs Chemical Engineering Thermodynamics (CHEE220) // 4 x 100 hrs & 1 x 50 hrs Resource Recovery and Circular Use (CHEE301) // 1 x 100 hrs & 1 x 50 hrs Physical Chemistry for Engineers (CHEE310) // 4 x 100 hrs Heat and Mass Transfer (CHEE315) // 4 x 100 hrs & 1 x 50 hrs Separation Processes (CHEE351) // 4 x 100 hrs Biochemical Engineering (CHEE374) // 4 x 100 hrs Materials Engineering (CHEE384)// 4 x 100 hrs Energy Systems Engineering (CHEE401) // 2 x 100 hrs & 1 x 50 hrs Process Modelling (CHEE440) // 3 x 100 hrs Design Project (456D1/D2) // 1 x 100 hrs Instrumentation & Measurement II (CHEE491) // 5 x 100 hrs & 1 x 50 hrs Foundations of Soft Matter (CHEE585) // 1 x 75 hrs Computational Methods (CHEE662) // 1 x 50 hrs Process Dynamics and Control (CHEE672) // 1 x 75 hrs Laboratory Safety 2 (CHEE682) // 1 x 100 hrs Research Skills (CHEE687) // 1 x 50 hrs Complete Course descriptions and Lecturers may be found here: Undergraduate courses: Program/Courses | Chemical Engineering - McGill University Graduate courses: https://www.mcgill.ca/chemeng/grad/gradcourses   *********************************** Qualifications: For all courses listed : having been a TA for the course previously, is a plus having taken the course as a student, is a plus Chemical Engineering or equivalent degree WHMIS certification is a must Additional Qualifications: Chemical Engineering Principles II (CHEE 204): Must have taken CHEE204 or equivalent and must have experience working in UG lab (i.e., gas cylinder, high pressure vessel, calorimeter, safety) and process design; Responsible for mini design project and calorimetry lab Resource Recovery and Circular Use (CHEE 301): Must have taken CHEE301 and/or have advanced knowledge of waste recovery processes (e.g., municipal solid waste, CO2 capture & reutilization, wastewater treatment etc.) Physical Chemistry for Engineers (CHEE 310): Solid background in chemical kinetics and/or reactions engineering. Background in electrochemistry preferred Separation processes (CHEE 351): Must have taken CHEE 351 and/or have advanced knowledge of distillation, absorption, carbon capture, and extraction. Ability to conduct tutorials and office hours on course topics involving separation processes. Ability to do a combination of: grading quizzes and midterms; leading in-person lab sessions; grading reports; invigilating in-person exams; evaluating in-person presentations; and delivering in-person tutorials. Must have up-to-date WHMIS. Biochemical Engineering (CHEE 374): Experience operating a microbial stirred tank bioreactor. Having taken CHEE 474, CHEE 651 or an equivalent course focused on bioprocess engineering. Having fundamental knowledge of both chemical engineering as well as biology (e.g. university-level courses in biochemical engineering, biotechnology, biomanufacturing)   Materials Engineering (CHEE 384): Have taken the course or have previously worked with ANSYS EduPack and EcoAudit. Process Modelling (CHEE 440): Having taken an undergraduate or graduate course equivalent to CHEE440. Proficient in applied mathematics for chemical engineering. Proficient in chemical engineering process modeling: discrete models and continuous models. Mathematical skills: working knowledge of linear and nonlinear algebra, vector and tensor algebra and calculus solution methods for coupled algebraic equations, partial and ordinary differential equations, integro-differential equations, difference equations optimization with constraints,  calculus of variation, scaling methods, approximation methods. Ability to conduct tutorials and office hours on course topics involving chemical engineering applied mathematics. Ability to grade quizzes and midterms in chemical engineering applied mathematics. Problem solving teaching skills in applied mathematics. Design Project (CHEE 456D1/D2): Advanced knowledge of HYSYS software. Instrumentation & Measurement II (CHEE491): ​Experience with data acquisition and control. Knowledge of LabView and instrumentation necessary. Must have up-to-date WHMIS. Foundations of Soft Matter (CHEE585): Beneficial to be conducting research in soft matter and/or a closely related area, e.g., biomaterials, interfaces, colloids, gels, liquid crystals. Beneficial to have experience using Matlab. Beneficial to have previously taken CHEE688 (Advanced Materials) and/or CHEE585  (Foundations of Soft Matter), or equivalents at other institutions. Computational Methods (CHEE662): Having taken CHEE662 or equivalent course in Computational Methods in Chemical Engineering . Working knowledge of theory and computation of: Partial Differential Equations;  Discretization Methods; Finite Elements;Integration; Interpolation and Mapping Functions;Nonlinear Algebraic equations; Time integration techniques for PDEs and MD; Nonuniqueness and stability in nonlinear systems; Bifurcations and Continuation Methods;Mesh Refinement Strategies; Convection dominated transport and Hyperbolic PDEs;Inverse Problems in Chemical Engineering; Computational Geometry of Surfaces. Process Dynamics and Control (CHEE672): Having taken CHEE672 or equivalent graduate core course in Process Dynamics and Control in Chemical Engineering. Proficient with MATLAB and its SIMULINK add-on Committed to rapid grading and feedback to students, excellent communication skills. Laboratory Safety 2 (CHEE682): Experience as a TA with lab safety. ***** HOW TO APPLY ***** Please apply through Workday, providing the necessary documents and info as per the AGSEM Collective Agreement, article 12.02. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Strathcona-Music/Assistant-Professor-of-Brass_JR0000082162": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Brass",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The Schulich School of Music of McGill University is Canada’s leading institution for the professional training of performers and composers, and the humanities-based and scientific research in music and sound. We seek individuals with an international profile in music performance and research who will maintain a high level of professional activity and are able to teach and supervise students in all university degree and diploma programs through to the doctoral level. Montreal offers a vibrant multicultural living environment and is consistently rated among the best student cities in the world. The Department of Performance invites applications from accomplished brass instrumentalists for a tenure-track position in Brass at the rank of Assistant Professor, beginning August 1, 202 7. We seek an accomplished brass instrumentalist with a specialization in trumpet, although outstanding candidates whose primary expertise is in trombone, horn, or tuba are also encouraged to apply. The appointment will be for an initial three-year term. Appointment at the rank of Associate Professor may be considered for an exceptional candidate. Job Duties: Academic responsibilities include teaching, research, and service to the University. The successful candidate will be expected to maintain an active international profile with a high level of professional activity, while teaching and supervising students at the undergraduate, graduate, and diploma levels, including those preparing for professional careers. The position also involves supervision of doctoral students in brass performance as a member of doctoral advisory committees. Teaching responsibilities will include applied instruction and chamber music coaching and may also encompass courses and seminars related to the candidate’s area of expertise, such as brass literature, orchestral excerpts, performance practice, pedagogy, or other specialized topics. Service responsibilities will include leadership and coordination of the Brass Area and may involve participation in departmental and School committees, as well as contributions to curriculum development and program enhancement. The successful candidate must demonstrate the ability to develop and sustain a level of artistic activity and/or research consistent with the University's expectations for tenure. Qualifications and Education Requirements: A doctorate or equivalent professional experience, together with a distinguished international reputation as a performer, is required. The successful candidate will demonstrate excellence in teaching students at the highest levels of performance and scholarship, broad knowledge of the brass repertoire, and a strong commitment to fostering an inclusive, equitable, and supportive learning environment. The candidate will also contribute to student recruitment and outreach initiatives that engage and inspire emerging musicians. Exceptional leadership, organizational, communication, and interpersonal skills are essential, as is the ability to collaborate effectively with colleagues, students, and the broader musical community. Faculty/Department/Unit: Schulich School of Music / Department of Music Performance Job Type: Tenure-track Rank: Assistant Professor Salary: Salary and rank commensurate with qualifications and experience. The position is subject to final budgetary approval. Required Documents: Applicants should submit the following items using McGill’s platform, Workday (see https://www.mcgill.ca/music/about-us/employment for instructions and links to apply). Further inquiries may be addressed to Prof. Danielle Gaudry, Chair, Department of Performance search.musicperformance@mcgill.ca . a cover letter outlining the candidate’s qualifications and suitability for the position a detailed curriculum vitae a link to a video recording of a performance demonstrating artistic excellence and containing at least two contrasting selections the names and contact information of three referees Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. To ensure full consideration, applications should be received by November 20, 2026 . Please note that applicants are not required to submit a teaching video with the initial application. Selected candidates may be invited to participate in a virtual interview. Finalists will be invited to demonstrate their teaching and artistic expertise by teaching a lesson and/or masterclass, coaching a chamber ensemble, presenting a short recital, and meeting with the Dean, students, and members of the Search Committee. All internal applicants (current, active employees only), please find McGill’s application manual found here: * English: https://general-knowledgebase.mcgill.ca/spaces/SHRKB/pages/54632206/How+to+Apply+for+a+Job+for+Internal+Candidates * French: https://general-knowledgebase.mcgill.ca/spaces/SHRKB/pages/54633556/Comment+postuler+%C3%A0+un+emploi+pour+les+candidats+internes All external applicants (including former employees), please find McGill’s application manual found here (English only): https://www.mcgill.ca/hr/files/hr/english_applying_as_an_external_candidate_one-pager_1.pdf Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, this  email . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Duff-Medical-Bldg/Teaching-Assistant---BMDE-660---Advanced-Magnetic-Resonance-Imaging-and-Spectroscopy-of-the-Brain_JR0000082139": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - BMDE 660:  Advanced Magnetic Resonance Imaging and Spectroscopy of the Brain",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Biomedical Engineering Course Title: Advanced Magnetic Resonance Imaging and Spectroscopy of the Brain Course Code: BMDE 660 Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 80 Position Summary: Update selected course material, Mark assignments, Respond to students' questions, monitor the myCourses discussion board and ensure that students get a timely response either from the TA or the instructor. Hiring Unit: Biomedical Engineering Course Title and Course Number: BMDE 660 - Advanced Magnetic Resonance Imaging and Spectroscopy of the Brain Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 80 Qualifications: Graduate training in Biomedical Engineering or Medical Physics with experience in magnetic resonance imaging and/or spectroscopy. Previously completed this or an equivalent course. Good oral and written communication skills. Effective communication skills in English are essential Only registered graduate students at McGill can be hired as TAs. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-04 Position End Date: 2027-05-07 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-28 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Duff-Medical-Bldg/Teaching-Assistant---BMDE-653---Patents-in-Biomedical-Engineering_JR0000082136": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - BMDE 653 - Patents in Biomedical Engineering",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Biomedical Engineering Course Title: Patents in Biomedical Engineering Course Code: BMDE 653 Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 90 Position Summary: Meet with the course instructor, and then correct the midterm exam, the project and the final exam. Hiring Unit: Biomedical Engineering Course Title and Course Number: BMDE 653 - Patents in Biomedical Engineering Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 90 Qualifications: - Having successfully taken course BMDE 653 - Effective communication skills in English are essential. - Only registered graduate students at McGill can be hired as TAs . Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-04 Position End Date: 2027-05-07 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-28 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
