    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Currie-Gymnasium/Assistant-Manager--Varsity-Sports---League-Games-Logistics---Operations_JR0000080274": {
      "title": "Assistant Manager, Varsity Sports – League Games Logistics & Operations",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Under the direction of the Manager, Varsity Sports, the Assistant Manager provides operational oversight for varsity league games, scheduling, staffing, and client experience. The incumbent supports league sports logistics and operations across all varsity sport groups, contributing to the effective delivery of competitions, compliance with league standards, and a positive experience for student‑athletes, officials, staff, and spectators.   Major Duties and Responsibilities Support the planning, and delivery of league sports varsity games scheduling across all four varsity sport groups. Oversee practice scheduling for all four varsity sport groups, ensuring equitable access, operational efficiency, and alignment with competition demands. Oversee game day supervision including amuse staff.   Support community outreach initiatives, including group ticket sales and engagement strategies designed to increase varsity game attendance and community connection. Manage and continuously improve the client experience for varsity league games, including spectators, visiting teams, officials, and stakeholders. Oversee operations of games and competitions, ensuring adherence to league regulations, safety standards, and McGill operational protocols. Identify operational risks and implement solutions to ensure seamless execution of varsity league games. Supervise a full-time varsity manager. Serve as the primary contact for assigned varsity league sports across Groups 1, 2, 3, and 4. Oversee ticketing operations for league‑run sports, including pricing structures, access management, reporting, and coordination with internal partners. Oversee ticketing budget. Supervise games for league‑run sports, including themed games and special league events. Function as primary liaison with league representatives, officials, and internal stakeholders for assigned sports. Provide direction to on‑site supervisors, sport officials, and event staff during league games and competitions. Ensure consistent application of service standards, branding, and league requirements across all supervised events. Leadership, Collaboration & Continuous Improvement Collaborate closely with Varsity Sports management, coaches, the A&R areas and external partners. Contribute to the planning for varsity league operations, attendance growth, and service excellence. Analyze operational data and post‑event feedback to inform improvements in scheduling, staffing, ticketing, and spectator experience. Mentor and support staff to promote professional development and operational consistency. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Strong working knowledge of the Microsoft 365 required Strong technological aptitude, with the ability to rapidly learn new business platforms while producing accurate work. Strong sport-event management, partner-coordination and problem-solving experience. Knowledge of university sport associations, including the RSEQ and U SPORTS is required. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills. Strong organizational, planning and time-management skills. High attention to detail and ability to multi-task. Demonstrated ability to work independently and collaboratively as part of a team. Availability to work evenings and weekends, as required. Bilingual in French and English required. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 04) $66,190.00 - $82,740.00 - $99,290.00 Job Profile: MPEX-ADM2A - General Administration - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Director Sport Programs Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-22 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Maass-Chemistry-Bldg/Invigilator_JR0000082109": {
      "title": "Invigilator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Chemistry Exam Period: October 22, 2026 - 5:30-8:00 pm (includes setup and takedown) -CHEM 552 midterm Position Summary: Responsible for setting up the examination room according to specific requirements as well as ensuring that equipment and facilities are functioning properly. Administers the delivery of the exams and documentation. Handles students’ problems. Ensures that students comply with regulations with respect to exams. Alerts the professor when students have a question. At the end of the examination period, collects all exam materials and ensures nothing is left behind in the examination room(s). Responsible for reporting back to the professor and ensuring delivery of the exams. Qualifications: Must be client-focused and service-oriented. Punctual. Demonstrated excellent communication and organizational skills, and an ability to transmit and receive information accurately. Demonstrates ability to listen and efficiently assist clients with problems. Ability to follow instructions and to work independently and as part of a team. Ability to act in an acceptable manner so as not to disrupt students writing the exams. English, spoken and read. French an asset. Experience and Education: High-School Diploma is the minimum requirement. Priority will be given to candidates who are already in McGill's priority pool. Location: This position is located in Montreal, Quebec and the work for this position is expected to be performed in Quebec. Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 3 on a scale of 0-4. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: (TSUM Invigilator) $18.00 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-19 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Montreal-Neuro/Research-Assistant-2_JR0000080701": {
      "title": "Research Assistant 2",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: This position leads the independent design and execution of in vitro studies using human iPSC-derived neuronal models to support translational research in neurodegenerative disease. This role requires advanced expertise in cell and molecular biology, assay development, and therapeutic screening. The successful candidate will be responsible for driving ASO therapeutic development projects, including candidate design, screening, and delivery system evaluation, and will contribute to experimental strategy, data interpretation, and laboratory mentorship. Key Responsibilities Independently design, optimize, and execute cell-based and molecular assays to support translational research programs Develop and characterize human iPSC-derived neuronal models for disease modeling Lead the design, screening, and efficacy evaluation of antisense oligonucleotide (ASO) candidates for patient-specific applications Develop and test lipid nanoparticle (LNP) and other delivery approaches for nucleic acid therapeutics Conduct protein expression and gene analysis (Western blot, qPCR, immunofluorescence) to characterize cellular phenotypes and therapeutic responses Establish robust, reproducible assay conditions; troubleshoot experimental challenges and optimize protocols Analyze and interpret complex datasets; prepare figures and written summaries for publications and grant proposals Train and mentor junior researchers, graduate students, and technical staff Contribute to the development of standard operating procedures (SOPs) and ensure quality documentation Minimum Qualifications: Master's in cell biology, molecular biology, neuroscience, or a related discipline Minimum 5 years of research experience (postdoctoral training included) in an academic or industry environment Demonstrated expertise with human iPSC culture, neuronal differentiation, and in vitro disease modeling Strong publication record demonstrating independent research capability Extensive experience with molecular and protein analysis techniques (qPCR, Western blot, immunofluorescence, co-immunoprecipitation) Proven ability to lead projects, analyze data, and contribute to experimental design Experience supervising or training students and junior researchers French and English language skills (spoken and written) Preferred Qualifications: Experience with ASO therapeutic design, screening, and in vitro efficacy evaluation Familiarity with lipid nanoparticle (LNP) formulation and testing Experience processing patient-derived primary samples for downstream cellular and molecular applications Knowledge of neurodegenerative disease biology This position is covered by the AMURE Collective Agreement: https://www.mcgill.ca/hr/files/hr/2023-2027_collective_agreement_amure_ras_final_-_with_signatures_-_for_printing_cover_page_reviewed.pdf Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 4 on a scale of 0-4. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $32.15 Hours per Week: 35 (Full time) Location: Montreal Neuro. Supervisor: Associate Professor Position Start Date: 2026-09-23 Position End Date: 2027-09-22 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-16 This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Brown-Student-Serv/Associate-Director-Operations-and-Systems_JR0000081732": {
      "title": "Associate Director Operations and Systems",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Scholarship and Student Aid Office McGill’s Scholarship and Student Aid Ofﬁce (SSAO) advises McGill students on funding options, budgeting, and debt management. They disburse all provincial, federal, and US student aid funding, and help with government aid information and documentation. They offer an Entrance Bursary Program, Entrance Scholarships, an In-Course Financial Aid Program (loans and bursaries) and a Work Study Program based on ﬁnancial need.   Position Summary: Reporting to the Director of the Scholarships & Student Aid Office, the Associate Director provides strategic leadership in operations, systems, and service delivery. The role oversees operational budgets, funding development and stewardship, Work Study employer relations, oversees delivery for student aid and award processes as well as internal and external communications. Leading a team of professional and administrative staff, the Associate Director ensures high-quality service for students and employers, while overseeing financial, human, and physical resources, system infrastructure and security, records management, and policy compliance.   Major Responsibilities Operations Develop short- and long-range planning for human and physical resources of the unit, financial capacity in support of student financial aid/award programs, space development and usage, and quality control within the unit’s full scope in partnership with the Director. Define and manage administrative operations, including operating budgets and related financial, contractual and procurement administrative tasks. Provide senior professional expertise and support to teams with diverse expertise, ensures high-quality staff recruitment and professional development standards. Participates in the hiring of key staff, and ensures University Human Resources’ policies and procedures, including collective agreements, are respected. Plan, develop and oversee processes, procedures, protocols and unit policies in accordance with the unit’s goals and objectives, for the integrated functioning of the various services and programs provided by the Scholarships & Student Aid Office. Plan and develop financial controls and segregation of duties within the finance portfolio; coach the Finance Officer on the implementation of these controls. Provide direction, leadership, management and supervision to a team of administrative and student staff: Guide and coach staff to achieve unit goals and outcomes Oversee professional development planning and all HR-related matters for team under their supervision Promote compliance with McGill policies in an environment of excellence Design and implement protocols for internal Knowledge Base, production schedule and user manuals for all positions across Scholarships and Student Aid. Provide senior professional expertise in response to team or stakeholder concerns regarding policies, programs or service, and controls, resolving any difficulties, as needed. Primary stakeholders include APB (Analysis, Planning and Budget), University Advancement, Enrolment Services, Student Accounts and the Offices of the Associate  Provost, Student Life and Learning and Senior Director of Student Services. Ensure all records are digitized, stored, retained and disposed of in accordance with University archive policies. Work on projects, carries out any other related duties as necessary in keeping with the requirements and qualifications of the job. Act as a designate for the Director, as required. Student Aid/Awards Fund Management Strategic oversight of 800+ individually-named funds from donors and grants for bursaries, loans and scholarships, as well as related endowments. Influence University Advancement central compliance and faculty-based Development Officers to negotiate meaningful and administrable award terms. Support APB and government relations with regard to how to implement new government grants for student aid/awards. Negotiate partnership agreements whereby third-party organizations share contributions and/or selections of full-ticket scholarships. Support the cycle of new student awards from the Committee on Enrolment and Student Affairs approval to the timely opening of Finance Funds and Aid Fund Codes.   Plan and control over-offers for Entrance Scholarships and Entrance Bursaries to meet predicted registration. In partnership with Associate Director Financial Aid, final authority on wording of financial commitment (offer) letters used at entrance and for immigration purposes. Establish protocols to optimize the assignment of specific funding sources based on award term restrictions/preferences matched with student candidates, as well as after-the-fact verification and rescindments. Develop and implement tactics for student engagement in donor thank you campaigns Review all stewardship and grant reporting. Oversee multi-year trend analysis to project needs and demands of programs.     Work Study Subsidy Management Oversee awareness and engagement of employers in support of the Work Study Program, that yields maximum use of subsidy funds and diverse part-time jobs available for students in financial need. Business owner of Work Study Subsidy Management platform used by employers and team to receive and approve subsidy requests as well as monitor usage. Oversee funding related to Work Study subsidy provision and expenditures .Plan and monitor financial capacity. Manage reporting to all Program funders, including the provincial government, per regulations and agreements. Create partnerships with key employers to ensure students with vulnerable profiles (refugees, youth in/from care, BIPOC, etc.) are placed in first job. Collaborate with student associations as both benefactors and supporters of the Work Study Program. Liaise with counterparts in Quebec Universities to advocate for the growth and protection of work study as a key resource for needy students to gain financial resources, skills and critical work experience.   External and Internal Communications Cultivate relationships with key McGill stakeholders and partners including: administrative units, departments / faculties, alumni etc. Guide and lead the development of strategic communications for the Scholarships & Student Aid Office for students, Work Study employers, Funders and community partners, aligning with Student Services and McGill communications processes. Working with content experts across Scholarships and Student Aid, oversee the delivery of mass communication and design of promotional materials and social media campaigns. Participate on University and departmental committees and workgroups   Systems & Infrastructure Responsible for the oversight, development, maintenance and integration of the IT application portfolio which supports the Scholarships & Student Aid Office, working closely with central IT staff to ensure applications are effective in managing work processes. Develop an in-depth understanding of the Banner Student Aid (BSA) product capabilities and ensure optimal use of its functionality in supporting the delivery and administration of the University’s undergraduate merit-based and need-based student awards programs including scholarships, bursaries, loans, work study, faculty awards, athletic awards, etc. Develop and maintain regulatory compliance functionality to support government aid programs for Canada, provincial and US Department of Education including PowerFAIDs and SAMS applications. Analyze business/user/system requirements using interviews, document analysis, business process descriptions, use and test cases, task and workflow analysis, etc. Provide direction to professional staff in the implementation of new technology and functionality, and recommend efficient business processes to optimize the analysis of IT requests for business needs. Develop framework for student funding reporting by working closely with Financial Services, Student Accounts, Enrolment Services, Human Resources, Academic Planning & Budget and corresponding central IT professionals to coordinate sound controls and exchange of data. Responsible for information retrieval, data control, reporting strategies and tools, as well as data warehouse requirements and ensure integrity of data to enable the University to measure the effectiveness of our student support programs, trends and benchmarking. Responsible for documentation of user manuals, logic and process capture for team scope. Manage system security, user access requests, and annual security review. Acts as security designate for Financial Aid/Awards (FAA) Administration, data warehouse, enrolment verification applications, business intelligence tools, knowledge base, SharePoint and shared mailbox administration. Works on special projects, as required.   Awards Administration Services Responsible for the strategic oversight, process improvement and support delivery for campus-wide student award processors (500+ users) from system interface, training, security set-up, to help-desk support. Approx. 30,000 interactions annually. Oversee back-office functions in the creation of student awards based on Committee on Enrolment and Student Affairs (CESA) approval, adherence to award types and University policies therein, as well as for publication in the Awards Calendar. Implements service level agreement with Graduate & Postdoctoral Studies regarding graduate student awards infrastructure and support, including fellowships, external granting agency awards, research stipends, etc. Oversight to ensure timely student award controls in central system repository including security set-up, adherence to award types and University policies therein, Aid Fund codification, NSF checking, student eligibility verification and exceptional reporting. Approx. 420,000 award disbursements annually.   Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Demonstrated leadership and managerial skills, with the ability to provide strategic direction, exercise sound judgement, establish priorities, and lead teams through complex and evolving operational environments. Demonstrated ability to supervise, mentor, coach and develop professional and administrative staff across diverse functions, including identifying performance, competency and professional development needs. Strong financial acumen, with demonstrated experience managing significant budgets, financial controls, reporting and resource allocation. Demonstrated ability to analyze complex operational, financial and/or systems-related issues, anticipate risks, and develop and implement effective solutions and process improvements. Strong organizational, planning and project management skills, with the ability to manage multiple priorities and coordinate initiatives involving diverse stakeholders. Demonstrated ability to develop and maintain effective collaborative relationships and partnerships with internal and external stakeholders at various organizational levels. Strong understanding of business processes, information systems and data management, with the ability to work effectively with technical and functional experts to support system development, integration, reporting, security and data integrity. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills, with demonstrated ability to communicate complex information clearly and effectively to diverse audiences and provide strategic advice to senior leadership and stakeholders. Tact, diplomacy, discretion and sound judgement when managing sensitive, confidential or complex matters. Strong service orientation; approachable, collaborative and resourceful, with the ability to remain effective in a high-volume and occasionally high-pressure environment. Demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion and to fostering an inclusive and respectful work and service environment. Knowledge of student financial aid, scholarships and awards programs, including relevant trends and practices in Quebec and North America, is a strong asset. Experience in higher education and/or in a complex, highly regulated, financial, government-funded or donor-supported environment is a strong asset. Advanced proficiency with computerized applications including spreadsheets, databases, word-processing software, Banner FIS, SIS, Banner Student Aid (BSA), Minerva, Crystal reports, Workday. English and French, Spoken & Written.   Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day-to-day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4-Fluent on a scale of 0-4.    Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 5 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 07) $92,150.00 - $115,190.00 - $138,230.00 Job Profile: MPEX-ADM3A - General Administration - Senior Professional/Manager Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Director Scholarships & Student Aid Office Position End Date (If applicable): 2029-01-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-22 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Currie-Gymnasium/Varsity-Sports--Competitions-Logistics---Operations-Administrator_JR0000080270": {
      "title": "Varsity Sports, Competitions Logistics & Operations Administrator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Under the direction of the Assistant Manager, League Games and Operations and in close collaboration with Athletics & Recreation (A&R) leadership, the incumbent provides operational support for varsity sports competitions, logistics, staffing, and special events. The position supports the delivery, supervision, and continuous improvement of varsity competitions and league games, ensuring safe, compliant, and efficient operations. Primary Responsibilities: Overall Program Lead Responsibilities Support competition sports supervision and varsity scheduling for assigned sport groupings (Groups 2, 3, and 4). Oversee home varsity games and competitions, including accurate statistical data entry, records management, and post‑event reporting. Ensure that platforms such as S1, Spordle, PrestoStats post event deadlines are met and the data is accurate Responsible for ambulance booking in collaboration with the sports medicine clinic. Coordinate training initiatives and staffing needs for sport officials and varsity competition sport officials, including onboarding, scheduling, operational readiness (training) and emergency protocols. Prepare game protocols as per league standards. Ensure operational standards, safety protocols, and league requirements are met during competitions. Manage in game promotions including Marty the mascot. Approve Workday time entries. Supervise home competitions, including but not limited to: Cheerleading (CHE) Flag Football Cross‑Country (CC) Rowing (ROW) Artistic Swimming (ART) Swimming (SWM) Assist Program Lead Responsibilities Provide operational and administrative support for ticketing and event access for both league‑run and competition‑run sports. Assist on scheduling of varsity league games and competitions. Assist with ticketing budget Contribute to funding development and government support initiatives, particularly for varsity competitions and special events. Support continuous improvement by identifying process efficiencies and recommending procedural enhancements. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Strong knowledge of varsity sport operations, competition protocols and league requirements, with demonstrated ability to safely and efficiently deliver and oversee sport program competitions. Excellent organizational, scheduling, communication, problem-solving skills, with the ability to oversees staff officials in a fast-paced environment. Ability to quickly master new systems and processes while maintaining quality and meeting deadlines. Strong technological aptitude and attention to detail in data entry, records management and reporting. Ability to manage priorities, implement process improvements and work collaboratively with internal and external partners. Bilingual in French and English is required Available and able to work evenings and weekends depending on game day. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: DEC III 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 03) $63,350.00 - $74,530.00 - $85,710.00 Job Profile: MPEX-ADM1A - General Administration - Professional 1 (Excluded) Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday Supervisor: Manager Intercollegiate Sports Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-22 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Sherbrooke-1010/Service-Person-C_JR0000030753-1": {
      "title": "Service Person C",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Facilities Management and Ancillary Services - Building Services Position Summary: Under the general supervision of the Service Person “A” and the District Supervisor, sees that the building to which he/she is assigned is fully functional, clean, secure and in good operational order. Qualifications: English and French functional spoken and read. English or French functional written. Additional Details: - Status: Temporary Full-Time - Hours of Work: 38.75 hrs/week; various hours – must be able to work all required shifts 24 /7 - Current Assignment: Downtown Campus               - Premium: Evening shift $0.97 / Night shift $1.44 McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Duff-Medical-Bldg/Student-Affairs-Administrator_JR0000082059": {
      "title": "Student Affairs Administrator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Under the guidance of the Graduate Program Director, the Student Affairs Administrator will be responsible for student admissions, enrolment services, and helping with communications and marketing. The Student Affairs Administrator will help administer and operate graduate programs in translational biomedical engineering and medical technologies; through applied coursework and internships, these programs help prepare students to transition from academia to work in the biomedical industry. The incumbent will participate in student recruitment fairs, student internship programs, convocation and graduation activities; they will offer a variety of student services including guidance for career planning, campus life and engagement, scholarships and student aid, counseling and tutorials, health and mental health, international student services, immigration, services for students with disabilities, and other programs. They will be responsible for offering student affairs services including advising, student records, registration and exam activities, as well as provide the integration of McGill's values, standards, and goals with respect to student life and learning into University policies, operations and procedures. The incumbent’s role is to ease students’ integration into University life, support students’ learning and guide them towards a rewarding student life experience by offering a broad range of services in a supportive environment. Primary Responsibilities: Oversee the day-to-day operations of the graduate programs and make recommendations to improve efficiency. Review departmental procedures and suggests improvements focusing on productivity and efficiency. Suggest new initiatives for continuous improvement; Provide administrative support for the admission process. Support professors, course coordinators, chairs, graduate program directors on academic programs, departmental policies and procedures, rules and regulations, course and seminar management; Act as the point of contact for prospective and graduate students for general information and queries; Review applications for completeness, ensure alignment with eligibility criteria and admissions requirements, calculate cGPA equivalencies, and prepare documentation for submission to the Graduate Program Director for admission decisions. Liaise with applicants and other stakeholders to explain eligibility and admission outcomes, issue offers and ensure appropriate follow-up; Provide guidance and support and refer the students to the appropriate channels to seek help on academic issues, personal difficulties, student-supervisor collaboration issues, internship issues, academic path issues, and financial issues. Handle confidential information with a high degree of sensitivity, respect, and diplomacy; Assist the Graduate Program Director with matters such as course equivalencies, recognition of courses from other institutions, transfer credits, and probationary status; Review records of all graduating BME students with regard to requirements for graduation; select and recommend candidates for graduation; Coordinate promotional material for the program and prospective students (flyers, digital content, and outreach materials) Review all requirements for internships have been met and organize internship evaluations; Coordinate and submit applications for internship funding (stipends and grants) with relevant government agencies to support student placements and industry partners; Support the development of workshops, seminars and information sessions for students and department staff. Revise budget and prepare post-event reports, when required; Network with student organizations and organize orientation and recruiting events across campus; Coordinate all course-related activities for the department, including timetabling (DCU), enrolment, student registration permits, course quotas, course evaluations and coordination of exams; Process grade submissions, coordinate course set-up, prepare and submit new course proposals and revisions for the department; Review and update the graduate calendar, the e-catalogue andthe website, In conjunction with the SBMS AEC team, collaborate on monitoring and processing graduate stipends; In conjunction with the departmentand the SBMS AEC team, prepare Teaching Assistant budgets and ensure timely posting of positions; Offer professional support and expertise, contribute to complex problem resolution and assist the Graduate Program Director and the Chair in handling of difficult situations.  Participate in GPD, Chair, Graduate Committee discussions and ensure the decisions to resolve complex issues are applied in day-to-day operations of the graduate programs. Prepare guidelines, forms and documents, and provide support to Program Officer in establishing processes; Prepare official letters for students regarding their CAQ, Study Permits, Academic Standing, Program Completion, Proof of Enrolment, etc.; Review and process Graduate Research Trainee (GRT) and Undergraduate Research Trainee (uGRT) requests for the department. Create and update departmental documents and forms (Student Handbook, Advisory Committee Meeting Report forms, Letter of Understanding, reports concerning student enrolment, evaluations, Annual reports,); Scheduling of the Graduate Student Seminars in the Winter and Fall terms. Process rescheduling requests, resolve issues, etc.; In conjunction with the BME Operations team, perform tasks related to internal and external networking and communications; post to departmental social media channels, lead the assembly and dissemination of a periodic newsletter; Attend network groups within the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences and externally. Attend meetings. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Experience working in student affairs management is an asset; Proven ability to work in a computerized environment using word processing, spreadsheets, and presentation software, including MS Office applications, Banner SIS and Minerva, databases, HTML, Workday, DCU, graduate application software (Slate/uApply) and website maintenance; Proven organizational skills and ability to prioritize and meet regular and on-going University and departmental academic deadlines; Excellent customer service and listening skills, including the ability to advise students in difficult situations, critical thinking, decision-making, attention to detail, leadership, as well as strong communication, influencing and collaboration skills; Demonstrated sense of diplomacy and experience in dealing with highly sensitive and confidential information; English spoken and written, French spoken. Event planning, graphic design, and/or digital marketing experience are assets. Testing may be administered This is a one-year temporary position As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: DEC III 3 Years Related Experience with DEC III / Hourly Salary: (MUNACA Level H) $33.05 - $40.97 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Professor Position End Date (If applicable): 2027-11-01 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-20 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/James-Admin-Bldg/Technology-Transfer-Manager_JR0000082107": {
      "title": "Technology Transfer Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: This position is co-funded by Axelys, a non-profit organization whose mission is to contribute to the economic and social prosperity of Québec by accelerating the development and transfer of high potential innovations stemming from public research. The Technology Transfer Manager will provide progress reports to Axelys and McGill University Office of the Vice-President, Research and Innovation. The technology transfer manager works closely with the Research and Innovation teams and has reporting obligations to the Associate Director, Technology Transfer.   The Technology Transfer Manager is responsible for managing a portfolio of reported inventions, its intellectual property assets and supporting the commercialization of discoveries arising from university research activities. The Technology Transfer Manager will work closely with faculty members, researchers, industry partners, investors, and entrepreneurs to maximize the impact and value of university-generated innovations through intellectual property protection, licensing, industry partnerships, and start-up creation.   The Technology Transfer Manager serves as a key liaison between the research community and external stakeholders, providing strategic guidance on commercialization opportunities and facilitating the translation of research discoveries into products, services, and companies that generate societal and economic impact. Primary Responsibilities: Serve as primary contact for Axelys to ensure funding opportunities managed by Axelys, their calls for proposal and business intelligence specifics are appropriately disseminated in the university research community. Meet with researchers on a regular basis to provide general information and advice on the commercialization process, in order to encourage and ensure reporting of inventions and foster their success in a commercial environment. Identify technologies with commercial potential. Provide early assessment of an invention’s commercial potential and the opportunity to pursue intellectual property protection. Monitor and manage a portfolio of on-going reports of invention, patents, licenses and other commercialization-related activities. Perform market and competitive landscape assessments and determine patentability and commercialization pathways.  Manage patent filings with external patent agents. Identify potential licensees for the inventions through networking, database searches, and partnership activities and actively promote inventions to external stakeholders, including pharmaceutical and biotech companies, venture capital funds, and others. Facilitate the creation of spinoffs by researchers, graduate students, and post-doctoral fellows by connecting them to mentors, coaches, and other resources available or through Axelys and McGill. Draft, review and negotiate commercialisation related agreements and legal documents. Provide senior professional expertise to the various Vice-President Research and Innovation teams through the review, interpretation, and negotiation of IP-related terms in research agreements and grants. Provide activity and other reports to the Office of the Vice-President Research and Innovation and Axelys as required. Attend and participate in meetings, workshops and events organized by Axelys (entirely in French). Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Master's degree, PhD, MD, or equivalent advanced training in a scientific, technical, or health-sciences related discipline. Minimum of five years of experience in technology transfer, intellectual property management, licensing, business development, commercialization, or a related field. Experience working within a university, research institution, hospital, or innovation ecosystem is preferred. Strong knowledge of intellectual property principles, including patents and copyrights. Proven experience in reviewing and negotiating IP related agreements. A strong understanding of the university technology transfer process and policies is an asset. Expertise in life sciences and medicine related fields. Strong interpersonal and relationship-management abilities. Fully bilingual in English and French, both verbal and written (language testing could be required). Proven ability to work within a complex policy and procedure framework. Proven ability to manage multiple priorities in a fast-paced environment. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 5 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 07) $92,150.00 - $115,190.00 - $138,230.00 Job Profile: MPEX-ADM3A - General Administration - Senior Professional/Manager Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Assoc Dir Technology Transfer Position End Date (If applicable): 2027-10-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-23 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Brown-Student-Serv/French-Outreach-Program-Assistant_JR0000082103-1": {
      "title": "French Outreach Program Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Campus Life & Engagement (CL&E) supports all McGill students by connecting them to resources and creating meaningful opportunities for involvement. They guide students in navigating the McGill student experience through orientation and transition programming and by providing student life opportunities across campus for students to develop their interests and skills. Position Summary: Under the supervision of the Associate Director, Student Involvement or his designate, the French Outreach Program Assistant supports initiatives that encourage students to engage in French both on campus and in the broader community. The role focuses on developing partnerships and identifying new opportunities for students to connect with Francophone communities on campus and throughout Montreal. This includes building relationships with community organizations, supporting outreach efforts, and helping create new French-language activities and experiences that promote engagement, belonging, and community connection. Through this work, the French Outreach Program Assistant helps build lasting partnerships and expand opportunities for students to engage in French both on campus and in the broader community. MAJOR DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Duties include, but are not limited to, the following: Conduct research and identify opportunities for collaboration with Francophone organizations and community partners. Develop and maintain relationships with campus partners, student groups, and community organizations that support French-language engagement and programming. Support outreach initiatives that increase awareness of opportunities to engage in French at McGill and within the broader Montreal community. Assist in the planning, coordination, and delivery of French-language events, workshops, and activities that promote student engagement on campus and within the broader community. Promote French-language activities and opportunities through presentations, information kiosks, social media content, and other communication channels. Assist with participant recruitment and engagement efforts for French-language programs and initiatives. Support the creation and distribution of promotional and educational materials in both English and French, as required. Collect participant feedback and assist in evaluating the effectiveness of French-language programs and outreach initiatives. Maintain outreach records, participation statistics, and program documentation. Assist with administrative tasks related to program planning, communications, outreach, and event logistics. Perform other duties and tasks as required.   EMPLOYMENT LEARNING OUTCOMES As a Program Assistant, you will have the opportunity to learn and/or develop skills in the following areas: Community Engagement Community Outreach, Partnerships & Stakeholder Relations Event Planning & Coordination Marketing & Communications Program Development Student Affairs/Student Life E DUCATION & EXPERIENCE Currently registered as a full-time student in any degree program at McGill University Will be a returning student in Winter 2027 Excellent French, spoken and written, with the ability to communicate effectively with diverse student and community audiences. Experience in outreach, event planning, communications, community engagement, or related areas, an asset. Work Study qualified, an asset Leadership experience, an asset Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #3 (advanced) for both written and oral communication on a scale of 0-4 .   OTHER QUALIFYING SKILLS AND/OR ABILITIES Excellent oral and written communication skills. Demonstrated interpersonal, networking, and relationship-building skills. Strong organizational, time management, and attention-to-detail skills. Ability to take initiative, work independently, and manage multiple priorities. Reliable, professional, and dependable. Ability to work effectively with campus and community partners. Proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite (Word, Excel, Outlook, PowerPoint). SUPERVISION RECEIVED Candidate receives regular review of work. Employee works within the framework of established procedures and receives direction as to objectives and policies. Work supervised may be of a routine or variable nature.   HOW TO APPLY : Please submit your cover letter , C.V. and Work Study approval letter (if applicable)   N.B . Only applicants selected for an interview will be contacted (usually within two weeks). Work Study applicants preferred - but not limited to them. Some weekend and evening work may be required for special events Anticipated Start Date: November 9, 2026 Application Deadline: October 26, 2026 Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 2 Years Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE C) $18.00 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 15 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-10-08 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Brown-Student-Serv/French-Programming-Assistant_JR0000082098-1": {
      "title": "French Programming Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-08\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Campus Life & Engagement (CL&E) supports all McGill students by connecting them to resources and creating meaningful opportunities for involvement. They guide students in navigating the McGill student experience through orientation and transition programming and by providing student life opportunities across campus for students to develop their interests and skills. Position Summary: Under the supervision of the Associate Director, New Student Experience or her designate, the French Programming Program Assistant coordinates peer-led French Language learning opportunities activities during the fall and winter semesters, allowing students to practice their French while building connections on-campus and in Montreal. This will include planning and carrying out different activities including cultural visits, French-language volunteer placements, seasonal and community events and French Lunch conversation Events while promoting social connection and a sense of community.   MAJOR DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES Duties include, but are not limited to, the following: Develop and plan French language programming in collaboration with the team, scheduling events at opportune times and liaising with on- and off-campus partners in the planning process. Reach out to community organizations to organize activities during which students will be able to practice their French conversational skills. Act as a facilitator during events, encouraging and prompting students as needed to promote active participation and French language learning, and guiding them where necessary. Track participation. Reach out to other partners to collaborate on promotion and sharing best (better) practices. Collaborate with CL&E’s Communications team on the promotion of French-language events, determining effective channels of promotion and collaborative promotional opportunities with partners, including McGill LearnFrench website. Liaise with students and university organizations for outreach, information exchange and collaboration; diplomatic communications and relationship-building. Track results and prepare a final activity report, detailing number of participants and number of students hours implemented during the period. Perform other duties and tasks, as required. EMPLOYMENT LEARNING OUTCOMES As a Program Assistant, you will have the opportunity to learn and/or develop skills in the following areas: Program Development Student support Adult Education/Learning Principles Peer Programs & Peer Support Student Affairs/Student Life EDUCATION & EXPERIENCE Currently registered as a full-time student in any degree program at McGill University Excellent French, spoken and written, with knowledge of grammatical terms and ability to provide some linguistic explanations Group facilitation experience, preferred Background in education, tutoring, or pedagogy, an asset. Work Study qualified, an asset Leadership experience, an asset Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #3 (advanced) for both written and oral communication on a scale of 0-4 . OTHER QUALIFYING SKILLS AND/OR ABILITIES Excellent communication skills, both oral and written Demonstrated attention to detail and time management skills Reliable, Ability to be focused on results, yet flexible to improve the student experience. Organized and professional Excellent knowledge of Microsoft Suite (Word, Excel, Outlook, PowerPoint) SUPERVISION RECEIVED Candidate receives regular review of work. Employee works within the framework of established procedures and receives direction as to objectives and policies. Work supervised may be of a routine or variable nature. HOW TO APPLY : Please submit your cover letter , C.V. and Work Study approval letter (if applicable) N.B . Only applicants selected for an interview will be contacted (usually within two weeks). Some weekend and evening work will be required for special events Application Deadline: October 26 Anticipated Start Date: November 9, 2026 Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 2 Years Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE C) $18.00 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 15 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-10-08 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
