    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Strathcona-AnatDent/Undergraduate-Course-Assistant--ANAT-316---Clinical-Human-Visceral-Anatomy_JR0000081751": {
      "title": "Undergraduate Course Assistant: ANAT 316 - Clinical Human Visceral Anatomy",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Anatomy and Cell Biology Course Title and Course Number: ANAT 316: Clinical Human Visceral Anatomy Estimated Number of Positions: 23 Total Hours of Work per Term: 39   Position Summary: The Undergraduate Course Assistant in ANAT 316 - Clinical Visceral Human Anatomy is integral in enriching the educational experience by providing hands-on support in the anatomy laboratory. Undergraduate Course Assistants are responsible for delivering weekly in-person laboratory demonstrations to groups of 8-10 students in the anatomy laboratory. Demonstrations and discussions will involve using cadaveric prosections and anatomical models to enhance students' understanding of visceral anatomy. Undergraduate Course Assistants are expected to help design and carry out laboratory activities, fostering discussions on specific regional topics related to human visceral anatomy in parallel with the course curriculum. Undergraduate Course Assistants are expected to actively participate in group meetings, and to uphold the Anatomy Lab Code of Conduct at all times. Undergraduate Course Assistants are required to assist with weekly lab setup, specimen selection and retrieval for presentation to assigned student groups, and, after lab sessions, the proper return of specimens to their designated storage spaces. Undergraduate Course Assistants will also be required to develop and submit practice 'bellringer' questions for the students according to a predetermined schedule (3-4 questions per semester).  There are currently openings for the Thursday lab. Labs are scheduled for Thursdays 8:35 am - 12:25 pm and Fridays 10:35 am - 12:25 pm.  Students need to be available 30 minutes before the lab starts for setup, and they may need to stay briefly after the lab ends. Qualifications: Must have experience working with human cadaveric samples. Must have previously studied gross anatomy related to the head and neck, thoracic, abdominal and pelvic anatomy, for example through successful completion of ANAT 316, ANAT 214, or another course equivalent. Must have demonstrated leadership skills in guiding undergraduate students to foster a productive and inclusive learning environment. Must have proven organizational skills, with meticulous attention to detail, and a commitment to maintaining accuracy, safety, and inclusivity in all aspects of teaching. Effective communication skills in English (spoken and written) are essential. Hourly Salary: $16.60 Position Start Date: 2027-01-04 Position End Date: 2027-05-07 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-23 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Strathcona-AnatDent/Undergraduate-Course-Assistant--ANAT-314---Human-Musculoskeletal-Anatomy_JR0000081744": {
      "title": "Undergraduate Course Assistant: ANAT 314 - Human Musculoskeletal Anatomy",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Anatomy and Cell Biology Course Title and Course Number: ANAT 314 - Human Musculoskeletal Anatomy Estimated Number of Positions: 12 Total Hours of Work per Term: 39 ​ Position Summary: UCA will be required to provide laboratory demonstrations in-person in the anatomy laboratory using prosection, anatomical models, medical images and other media. UCA will also be asked to define and create new laboratory activities and facilitate small group discussions for a given topic on the human anatomy related to the course. UCA will be expected to participate in group meetings to prepare the labs and to reinforce the Anatomy Lab Code of Conduct during all sessions. Other duties will be discussed as required. UCA will be responsible for helping with the lab set up, retrieving specimens to present to assigned student groups, and upon lab completion, to return specimens to their assigned storage space as well as fully cleaning up the different stations area. Depending on the number of applications received, a select number of applicants may be asked to participate in an informal interview process. Qualifications: Must have previously taken and passed ANAT 314.Demonstrated ability to teach human anatomy. Great knowledge of the gross anatomy of the musculoskeletal system (bone, joints, muscles and neurovascular structures) of the head, neck, trunk, upper and lower limbs regions of the human body is required. Must be able to lead lab demonstrations emphasizing on functional and clinical anatomy; must have a good understanding of surface anatomy, prosection and medical imaging. Proven organizational skills with attention to detail and accuracy. Effective communication skills in English are essential. Hourly Salary: $16.60 Position Start Date: 2027-01-04 Position End Date: 2027-05-07 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-23 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Laird-Hall/Student-Recruitment-Associate_JR0000081910": {
      "title": "Student Recruitment Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary:    Under the direction of the Student Recruitment Supervisor, plans, coordinates, and participates in individual and/or team virtual and in-person visits representing McGill’s Macdonald Campus programs primarily to Quebec CEGEPs, Canadian, and International High Schools. May include some overnight travel. Participates in undergraduate recruitment events, targeting prospective students, applicants, admitted and confirmed students, parents and counselors, including on and off campus events, newly admitted student activities, Open House, virtual events, and more.  Presents information, including undergraduate admissions criteria, academic programs offered at Macdonald Campus, and student life and services to groups of potential students, families, counsellors, and industry professionals.   Duties and Responsibilities:   Assists with the coordination and logistics of recruitment events, including virtual information sessions, campus visits, school fairs, open houses, and other student recruitment activities. Represents the Faculty at select recruitment events and information fairs by providing prospective students and families with general information regarding programs, admission requirements, student services, and campus life in both English and French. Responds to routine inquiries from prospective students via email, phone, social media, and other communication channels. Refers complex admission or academic questions to senior recruitment staff. Maintains and updates recruitment materials, presentations, contact lists, and event databases. Assists in creating and scheduling content for social media platforms and recruitment communications. Supports the collection and compilation of recruitment statistics, event attendance data, survey results, and other reporting information. Prepares recruitment packages, promotional materials, and supplies for events and campus visits. Assists with organizing campus tours, student-for-a-day programs, open houses, orientation activities, and other prospective student events. Coordinates schedules for student ambassadors and tour guides, assists with training logistics, and monitors completion of administrative documentation. Communicate with prospective students, applicants, parents, guidance counsellors and other stakeholders regarding admissions, enrolment procedures and applications and other inquiries.  Provides follow-up communications with applicants. Processes purchase requisitions, tracks recruitment-related expenditures, reconciles routine transactions, and maintains financial records for recruitment activities. Maintains filing systems, databases, and shared resources to support the efficient operation of the unit. Create material and handouts to bring to site visits or post on website.  Participates in special projects and performs other related duties in support of recruitment, admissions, and student engagement activities, including but not limited to CEGEP and high school visits, job fairs and Open House. Establish and foster effective working relationships by identifying needs and providing support in the delivery of services. When appropriate, inform supervisor and formulate recommendations.  Attends meetings.    Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities: Demonstrated ability to provide professional, responsive, and client-focused service to prospective students, applicants, parents, and other stakeholders. Strong verbal and written communication skills, with the ability to convey information clearly and adapt messaging to diverse audiences in both English and French. Proven organizational and time management skills, with the ability to coordinate multiple events, schedules, deadlines, and competing priorities simultaneously. Ability to represent the Faculty professionally at recruitment events, information sessions, campus tours, and community outreach activities. Experience creating, editing, and maintaining promotional materials, presentations, communications, and online content. Proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite and experience using databases, customer relationship management (CRM) systems, shared drives, or other administrative software. Demonstrated ability to collect, compile, and maintain accurate data, records, reports, and financial documentation while ensuring attention to detail. Ability to work collaboratively with colleagues, students, faculty members, and external partners to support recruitment and student engagement initiatives. undefined As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 4 (qualifier) on a scale of 4-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: DEC III 3 Years Related Experience with DEC III / Hourly Salary: (MUNACA Level H) $33.05 - $40.97 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Student Recruitment Supervisor Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-20 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Dawson-Hall/Research-and-Grants-Officer_JR0000081985-1": {
      "title": "Research and Grants Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-07\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Under the supervisor of Director of Administration, the Research and Grants Officer will collaborate with the Associate Dean, Research (ADR), to support grant and research administration in the Faculty of Arts and advance the direction of the ADR portfolio. The incumbent will work closely with Faculty of Arts academic staff throughout the full grant lifecycle (writing, submission, budget review) with the overall aim of increasing the number and quality of grant proposals. The incumbent is responsible for identifying and communicating research funding opportunities for academics, as well as preparing and maintaining grantsmanship resources. The incumbent will also develop and maintain quantitative dashboards to track submissions, success rates, financial commitments, and dollar allocations to help optimize existing research resources. Primary Responsibilities: Advises on and provides grant proposal development support to faculty, particularly junior and early-career faculty, by reviewing draft applications, offering guidance and feedback, recommending revisions, and validating the overall coherence, feasibility, quality and readiness of proposals prior to submission.  Develop a faculty support framework (structure, delivery method, and evaluation criteria) for grant preparation; evaluate and maintain effectiveness of framework against outcomes (e.g., grant success rates) and revise it accordingly.  Develop a repository of successful grant proposals; interpret and statistically analyze the data and provide recommendations to academics submitting grants and provide guidance to the ADR  Oversee internal peer review processes for grant applications; establish review criteria with the Associate Dean and assess proposal readiness for submission.  Analyze research performance data, identify meaningful benchmarks and comparators, and prepare annual reports on research impact, application and funding trends, and benchmarking outcomes.  Develop, maintain and improve quantitative dashboards and data visualizations  Advise faculty on award nominations by assessing alignment with award criteria, evaluating competitiveness and fit, and providing guidance on complex or ambiguous cases.  Identify and communicate funding opportunities to faculty; review and edit nomination packages to align with funder and donor requirements; draft memos, announcements, and communications on research opportunities.  Organize and facilitate faculty peer-support networks for grant writers, leveraging expertise in social sciences and humanities research methodologies to foster effective proposal development and knowledge sharing.  Develop and produce original faculty capacity-building resources, including best-practices guides and support materials, informed by analysis of successful grant applications to enhance research funding success.  Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities PhD in the field of Humanities or Quantitative and Qualitative Social Sciences preferred  A minimum of 2 years of experience with grant writing and submission  Proficiency in using Excel, PowerBI, MS Office365  Demonstrated ability to build-relationships with a diverse group of stakeholders  Demonstrated ability to be adaptable and flexible, with the capacity to respond effectively to evolving priorities and dynamic research environment  Strong client-service-orientation, with excellent interpersonal and stakeholder engagement skills  Knowledge in statistical programming (STATA and or R) and Python is an asset  As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 4 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 05) $73,470.00 - $91,840.00 - $110,210.00 Job Profile: MPEX-ADM2B - Research Administration - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Interim Director Administration Position End Date (If applicable): 2027-12-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-22 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
