    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Strathcona-AnatDent/Undergraduate-Course-Assistant---INDS-114-PROSEC--Digestion-and-Metabolism_JR0000081927": {
      "title": "Undergraduate Course Assistant - INDS 114 PROSEC: Digestion and Metabolism",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Anatomy and Cell Biology Course Title and Course Number: INDS 114 PROSEC: Digestion and Metabolism Estimated Number of Positions: 3 Total Hours of Work per Term: 6 Position Summary: Undergraduate Course Assistants will assist first year medical and dental students in anatomy dissections (4 hours). Undergraduate Course Assistants should demonstrate an ability to teach human anatomy in the anatomy lab and be able to facilitate student dissections of the abdomen. Undergraduate Course Assistants will also participate in the supervision and facilitation of anatomy review time (2 hours). Availability is required on the following dates and times: February 2nd (Tuesday): 12:30 - 2:30 pm February 9th (Tuesday): 12:30 - 2:30 pm February 17th (Wednesday): 12:30 - 2:30 pm Qualifications: Must have previous laboratory experience in human anatomy on topics relevant to the course description. Strong knowledge of abdominal anatomy, and good communication skills are essential. Effective communication skills in English are essential. Communication in French is an asset. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $16.60 Position Start Date: 2027-01-04 Position End Date: 2027-05-07 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-23 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Ferrier-Building/Program-Manager_JR0000081918": {
      "title": "Program Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Reporting to the Director of the French Language Centre (FLC), the Program Manager will focus on developing, delivering, planning, and organizing structured programming that supports language acquisition, student engagement, and academic success. It will also provide a liaison function with other units and external organizations. Primary Responsibilities: Develop and oversee learning and cultural opportunities that complement and enhance the student academic experience outside of specific credit courses; Develop and deliver workshops focused on conversation practice outside the classroom Design and implement co-curricular learning activities and events, and initiatives that engage with French language and culture, and career readiness Create resources that support these programs (eg workshop guides, activity materials, learning resources, orientation content, and supplementary academic support tools) Deliver and facilitate structured workshops (eg Jasette, French at Work Program ) focused on oral communication, professional skills, and intercultural competencies Monitor student participation, engagement, and program outcomes to inform retention and student success activities Conduct student outreach and provide individualized support and guidance to enhance engagement and persistence Develop, implement, and assess initiatives that foster belonging, linguistic confidence, and sustained participation in French-language programming Manage program activity registrations, reminders, and student communications Prepare detailed reports evaluating program effectiveness Oversee recruitment and training of a team of Francophone language assistants Prepare job postings and interview candidates Supervise casual employees. Assign work. Provide ongoing guidance and support. Maintain team’s work schedule Promote programs and maintain digital presence on FLC’s social media platforms Contribute to website redesign and ensure compliance with university branding and accessibility standards Liaise with FLC stakeholders to plan McGill’s participation in institutional academic collaborations (eg Jumelage linguistique , Parcours Francoville ) Develop intercultural partnerships (e.g., Université de Montréal, Université de Lorraine, UQAM)  Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Fluency in spoken and written French, required to support pedagogical activities and all forms of professional communication, and advanced proficiency in spoken and written English. Bachelor’s degree in International Studies and Languages, or a related field. Minimum of three years of relevant professional experience in program coordination, student engagement, and the development and delivery of educational, language-learning, and intercultural programs and initiatives. Minimum of two years of experience coordinating programs, projects, or student initiatives in a post-secondary environment. Minimum of two years of experience designing and facilitating workshops, training sessions, or learning activities for students in a post-secondary environment. Demonstrated experience developing, implementing, and assessing programs or initiatives that support student engagement, retention, and success. Demonstrated ability to develop and maintain partnerships with internal and external stakeholders. Demonstrated interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build productive relationships, exercise sound judgment, and address challenges through a collaborative and solution-oriented approach. Demonstrated organizational skills and ability to manage multiple priorities simultaneously. Assets Understanding of the Quebec context related to supporting French language learning in an English-language higher education environment. Experience developing co-curricular and/or extracurricular French as a Second Language (FSL) programming in higher education. Experience managing digital communications, including website development or redesign and social media platforms. Experience supervising, training, and mentoring language assistants or peer facilitators. Experience supporting and contributing to academic committees and working groups in a higher education environment. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 3 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 04) $66,190.00 - $82,740.00 - $99,290.00 Job Profile: MPEX-ADM2A - General Administration - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Senior Faculty Lecturer Position End Date (If applicable): 2028-12-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-21 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Penfield-740/Web-Application-Specialist_JR0000075458": {
      "title": "Web Application Specialist",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position summary: At Canadian Centre for Computational Genomics (C3G) at McGill University, we build open-source Research Data Management (RDM) solutions that support every stage of the genomics and health data lifecycle. Our portals, APIs, databases and tools are the infrastructure behind national genomics and health data sharing in Canada. If you want your work to matter beyond the sprint, this is that role. Projects include: The Pan-Canadian Genome Library (PCGL) The Terry Fox Marathon of Hope Cancer Centre Network (MoHCCN) The International Human Epigenome Consortium (IHEC) The Quebec COVID-19 Biobank (BQC19) We also provide bioinformatics analysis software and high-performance computing services to the life sciences research community, including widely used analysis pipelines. A Web Application Specialist will engage in the development of online data sharing solutions, notably the Bento Platform ( https://github.com/bento-platform ), which supports the Pan-Canadian Genome Library ( https://genomelibrary.ca/ ), and many other projects in the fields of genomics, epigenomics, and life science. Under the supervision of the Data Team Lead, the Web Application Specialist will implement and maintain client-side and server-side applications, extending the tools and technologies already in place using JavaScript (React/TypeScript), Python, PostgreSQL, Docker and more. Short development cycles will involve small but frequent releases, and constant interactions with the bioinformatics platform team members. Primary responsibilities: Design, build, test, deploy, monitor and maintain high-quality interactive web applications using TypeScript with React Develop server-side applications (APIs) and scripts, such as back-end data services and quality control pipelines, using Python Focus on user experience, intuitive interfaces and interaction layers to create user-friendly web pages and applications Ensure implementation is done using the best coding practices, including documentation, unit testing, etc. Contribute in technological research and the prototyping of new clinical/genomics data services Setup and maintain automation tools for testing, continuous integration & delivery Optimise code when needed while making use of good software practices Use an issue tracking system to document tasks, issues and bugs, and their resolution status throughout sprints Ensure that all finished solutions work within a variety of browser types and screen settings along with the constraints set by development and design Contribute to the code review process Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities: Hard skills: Experience with a JavaScript framework (e.g., React, Vue) in developing full-scale web applications is mandatory. Providing links to examples of web applications the applicant has worked on, either live or on GitHub, is highly recommended. Experience with server-side programming/scripting, such as with Python or Node.js. Experience with relational databases (RDBMS), such as PostgreSQL or MySQL. Experience with applications containerization technologies (e.g. Docker, Podman, Kubernetes). Undergraduate degree in computer science, software engineering or related field. Soft skills: Demonstrated sound intuition in making efficient and simple-to-use browser-based interactive tools and interfaces. Interest in developing fully open-source solutions. Having an open mind towards new technologies, with at least basic knowledge of the various layers involved in web application development (full-stack). Attention to detail, good communication and organisational skills and ability to work in a highly interactive group. Capable of getting multiple tasks assigned at once, and making them all progress steadily. Strong Assets: Experience with Python frameworks like Flask, Django, or FastAPI. Experience with the Kubernetes container orchestration system. Experience with Extract-Transform-Load (ETL) or Extract-Load-Transform (ELT) pipelines. Experience with CI/CD workflow orchestration systems (e.g. GitHub Actions, GitLab Workflows) Interest in the field of genetics and bioinformatics. English and French (spoken and written). As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 5 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 06) $82,280.00 - $102,850.00 - $123,420.00 Job Profile: MPEX-IST3S - Research Software Development - Senior Professional Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Bioinformatics Manager Position End Date (If applicable): 2027-06-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-01 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Arts-Building/Administrative-Supervisor_JR0000081939": {
      "title": "Administrative Supervisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Under the supervision of the Administrative Manager, Administrative Service Centre 3 Arts-Ferrier, the Administrative Supervisor provides efficient and effective administration to the ASC Arts-Ferrier in the Faculty of Arts, in support of teaching, research and administration, in accordance with the unit's goals and objectives, while ensuring coherence with the University's mission and culture. The position primarily supports activities related to student affairs, human resources and operations in the French Language Centre. Directly supervises the Student Affairs Coordinator in ASC Arts-Ferrier, who also works primarily with the French Language Centre. Primary Responsibilities: Organize activities to support the daily operations of the French Language Centre by ensuring that administrative functions are carried out in accordance with established goals and objectives  Receive and resolve complex student cases escalated from the Student Affairs Coordinator  Recommend process improvements to prevent or minimize recurring problems  Provide high-level customer service on matters relating to the French Language Centre, including the handling of complaints and resolving issues within the scope of responsibility  Liaise with other units, including GPS, Enrolment Services, Student Accounts, School of Continuing Studies, and foster good relationships with those colleagues and units  Administer office procedures; assess existing procedures; implement new procedures when necessary  Design and implement communication strategy to staff and the student community on behalf of the French Language Centre  Create content for the French Language Centre website  Compile statistics and data on academic and administrative activities for inclusion in reports  Act as back-up to the Student Affairs Coordinator during absences  Process recruitments and appointments in Workday for Course Lecturers for FRPC course sections  Maintain MCLIU priority pool for FRPC  Act as a resource on issues related to MCLIU and liaise with the union, McGill Labour Relations and Faculty HR to resolve disputes related to collective agreement  Hire Teaching Assistants, Lab Attendants and any other roles needed to support related course sections  Directly supervise the Student Affairs Coordinator. Organize and verify work, set goals and deadlines. Monitor performance. Approve vacation and monitor absences.  Act as back-up to the Administrative Manager during absences.  Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Candidate must be fluently bilingual in both French and English, providing primary support for the French Language Centre.  Demonstrated organizational skills and ability to multi-task.   Demonstrated ability to prioritize and to meet weekly and monthly deadlines.   Proven ability to compose correspondences and to proofread documents using appropriate spelling, grammar, punctuation, and formatting.   Ability to adapt to change and to foster a collaborative environment.   Strong accuracy and attention to detail.   Must be client focused and service oriented.   Demonstrated ability to clearly transmit and receive information.   Demonstrated ability to work in a computerized environment using word-processing, databases, spreadsheets, presentation software and knowledge of web maintenance.   Knowledge of programs such as MMP, CMMS, Banner and applications, Microsoft office suite particularly Excel (level: intermediate) and Powerpoint, and Workday.  As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 4 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: DEC III 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 03) $63,350.00 - $74,530.00 - $85,710.00 Job Profile: MPEX-ADM1A - General Administration - Professional 1 (Excluded) Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Administrative Manager Position End Date (If applicable): 2028-12-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-21 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McLennan-Library/Student-Project-Assistant--Book-Arts-Lab-_JR0000081989-1": {
      "title": "Student Project Assistant (Book Arts Lab)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). *** The position is open to both undergraduate and graduate McGill students.   Hiring Unit: Rare Books and Special Collections   Job Title: Student Project Assistant (Book Arts Lab) – Office Assistant A   Reports to: Jennifer Garland, Interim Director, Rare Books and Special Collections   Hour of work: 4-5 hours per week   Hourly Rate: $16.70   Classification: AMUSE A    Duration: October 18, 2026 – December 23, 2026 with possibility of renewal   Job Summary:   Would you like to learn about historical letterpress printing? Are you ready to roll up your sleeves, get your hands dirty, and work with ink and type? This opportunity may be for you!    The apprenticeship positions: We’re looking for two student printing apprentices to work with us for 4-5 hours every Friday in the fall semester, to help with the general operation and maintenance of the Book Arts Lab. Apprentices will be taught the basics of print shop safety, equipment, tools, techniques, and the printing process, from setting type to inking the presses. Our current focus is the wood type in our lab: student apprentices will assist in cleaning, organizing, identifying, and printing specimen sheets of wood type, as well as assisting in the printing of one of our wood type projects.   Please submit a brief statement of interest to tell us why you are interested in this position, along with any previous printing, design, or book arts experience, and a current resume. Please also confirm you Friday availability.   Training and orientation will be provided. All duties to be completed on-site, in Rare Books and Special Collections, McLennan Library Building.    About the Book Arts Lab , McGill University Libraries:    The Book Arts Lab was established in 2020, to preserve and teach with remarkable historical printing presses held at the McGill Libraries, and to introduce students to the materials, processes, design, and labour involved in letterpress printing.    Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities:   •                An enthusiasm for books, printing, crafts, working with your hands •                Attention to detail  •                Aptitude for intricate, careful work with your hands •                Experience with printing or graphic arts in any form is an asset, but not required •                Comfortable lifting somewhat heavy objects; working in a sometimes dusty environment; working with letterpress ink and related cleaning supplies •                Ability to stand for 3-4 hours at a time •                McGill University is an English-language university where day-to-day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (3) on a scale of 0-4 Other languages an asset. For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma Minimal or No Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE A) $16.70 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 5 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-10-06 Position End Date: 2026-12-23 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-16 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McLennan-Library/Graduate-Research-Project-Assistant_JR0000081357": {
      "title": "Graduate Research Project Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nPart time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). AI-Assisted Translation and Pedagogical Resource Development Teaching and Academic Programs (TAP) at McGill University is seeking graduate students to contribute to a research and development project exploring the use of generative AI to translate francophone scholarly work and transform it into accessible, anglophone instructor-facing teaching resources. A central goal of the project is to broaden the perspectives available to English-speaking instructors and students in Quebec (and in Canada more broadly) by making scholarship about AI from outside the established Anglo-American sphere of knowledge production more accessible, particularly the work of francophone scholars which may offer alternative perspectives on artificial intelligence and its social, cultural, and educational implications. The project will develop a collection of translated scholarly articles accompanied by study guides, teaching activities, handouts, presentation slides, and other resources designed to help instructors integrate the materials into their courses. Candidates will also contribute to documenting and evaluating the AI-assisted translation and pedagogical adaptation workflow. Position Details Duration: January 4, 2026 to April 30, 2027 Hours: Up to 15 hours per week Location: Primarily remote, with occasional meetings or activities on campus Hourly rate: Master’s student: approximately $20/hour + 4% vacation indemnity; PhD student: approximately $23/hour + 4% vacation indemnity Hiring unit: Teaching and Academic Programs MANDATE Working with the team lead and using an established prototype and production workflow, the Candidate will identify suitable articles, conduct AI-assisted translation, and produce pedagogical adaptations of scholarly articles for practical use in the classroom. The work will involve using generative AI tools critically and systematically, reviewing and improving translations, and developing supporting instructional resources that help instructors engage students with the ideas presented in the articles. Project assistants will also document their workflow and provide feedback that will help refine the project's methodology, templates, and quality standards. RESPONSIBILITIES Identify suitable articles from among francophone scholarship, using mostly, but not restricted to, sources from Quebec; Use generative AI tools to produce French/English translations of selected scholarly articles, then review, edit, and improve texts for accuracy, readability, terminology, tone, and fidelity to the original text; Maintain documentation of prompts, workflows, revisions, and approximate production time; Develop concise instructor-facing study guides based on established project templates; Apply project guidelines and quality criteria consistently across assigned materials; Participate in regular project meetings and internal review checkpoints; Review materials produced by other members of the project team and contribute to quality assurance; Provide feedback on the effectiveness and limitations of the project's AI-assisted workflow. PROFILE The ideal candidate will combine strong academic reading and writing abilities with an interest in teaching, learning, translation, and emerging technologies. Candidates should have: Advanced proficiency in both English and French , including strong written communication skills; Strong academic reading, writing, editing, and synthesis skills; Ability to understand scholarly texts and communicate complex ideas clearly to non-specialist audiences; Knowledge of instructional design, pedagogy, educational technology, or teaching and learning in higher education; Familiarity with generative AI tools such as ChatGPT, Copilot, Gemini, Claude, or similar systems; Particularly, familiarity with their shortcomings, redundancies, and tendency for circular regurgitation. Strong attention to detail and ability to identify problems of meaning, terminology, tone, and accuracy; Proficiency with Microsoft Word and PowerPoint or comparable applications; Strong organizational and time-management skills; Ability to manage several related deliverables and meet agreed-upon deadlines. Ability to work independently while also collaborating effectively with a project team; Considered an asset Academic background in Educational Technology, Education, Instructional Design, Translation, Linguistics, Communication, Information Studies, or a related field; Experience developing instructional materials for university-level learners; Experience working with instructors or subject-matter experts; Experience with translation, editing, localization, or terminology work; Experience designing presentation materials, learning activities, handouts, or study guides; Familiarity with research on generative AI, AI-assisted translation, or AI in education; Experience with qualitative research, evaluation, or documenting research processes. EDUCATION Currently enrolled in a Master’s or PhD program in Educational Technology, Education, Instructional Design, Translation, Linguistics, Communication, Information Studies, or another field relevant to the project. Candidates from related disciplines who can demonstrate strong bilingual academic writing and pedagogical skills are also encouraged to apply. HOW TO APPLY Applicants should submit one .pdf file combining the following three documents: A current CV or résumé; A brief cover letter describing their relevant experience and interest in the project; An unofficial transcript confirming current graduate enrolment; Shortlisted candidates may be asked to complete a brief practical exercise in which they identify and justify the choice of a couple of scholarly articles that would be suitable for inclusion in the project. The exercise will assess familiarity with relevant fields of scholarship, ability to search efficiently across academic sources, skill in scanning and evaluating abstracts, and judgment in identifying work that contributes perspectives not already well represented in dominant discussions of AI. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Currie-Gymnasium/Technical-Specialist-Assistant-Coach---Artistic-Swimming_JR0000081907": {
      "title": "Technical Specialist Assistant Coach - Artistic Swimming",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Athletics and Recreation Position Summary: Under the direction of the Artistic Swimming - Head Coach, the Technical Specialist - Assistant Coach will be expected to assist the head coach in the implementation of performance and development initiatives that will help ensure the success of the Artistic student-athletes. Expected to assist head coach with other related functions. Primary Responsibilities: Maintain and distribute on-field team equipment. Prepare practice and training equipment ahead of start times Record activities and keep track of statistics during competitions and performances. Recruitment of prospective student-athlete Artistic players Adhere to the administrative rules, regulations and policies of the university and the league (CUASL) regulations   Activate fundraising initiatives that will support the Artistic Swimming operational needs. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Excellent interpersonal, analytical, evaluation and planning skills Strong desire and dedication to the development of the student athletes Willingness to work flexible hours including evenings and weekends Working hours will depend on the   time and resources needed during different parts of the season May require CPR and First Aid certifications Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 2 Years Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE C) $17.17 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 10 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-10-05 Position End Date: 2027-04-27 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-22 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McGill-College-2001/Research-Assistant-2_JR0000081898": {
      "title": "Research Assistant 2",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The Faculty of Dental Medicine and Oral Health Sciences at McGill University invites applications for a part-time Research Assistant 2 position to join the interdisciplinary research program led by Dr. Pascaline Kengne Talla . Key Responsibilities The successful candidate will: Coordinate research projects and support day-to-day project operations; Contribute to research protocols, ethics submissions, grant applications, and progress reports; Coordinate meetings, project timelines, deliverables, and communications with collaborators and partners; Support quantitative and qualitative data collection, management, analysis, and interpretation; Assist with literature reviews and knowledge syntheses; Contribute to the preparation of peer-reviewed manuscripts, conference presentations, reports, and knowledge mobilization products; Support graduate and undergraduate trainees involved in research projects; and Participate actively in research team meetings and collaborative activities. Qualifications Applicants should have: A master’s degree in public health, epidemiology, health services research, population health, social sciences, implementation science, dentistry, or a related field; Experience coordinating research projects; Excellent organizational, communication, and project management skills; Strong scientific writing skills; Ability to work independently and collaboratively in interdisciplinary teams; and Experience with quantitative research methods and statistical software (e.g., R, Stata, SAS, SPSS); Experience with qualitative or mixed-methods research; Experience with grant applications and ethics submissions; Proficiency in English. Assets The following would be considered assets: Proficiency in French; Knowledge of health equity, population health, oral health, health services research, or implementation science; and Experience working with community, healthcare, or policy partners. Application Applicants should submit: A cover letter; A curriculum vitae; and Contact information for two or three references. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $32.15 Hours per Week: 32 (Part time) Location: McGill College 2001 Supervisor: Assistant Professor Position Start Date: 2026-10-20 Position End Date: 2027-10-20 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-19 This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/5858-Cote-des-Neiges/Teaching-Assistant---FMED-527_JR0000081941": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - FMED 527",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Family Medicine Course Title: Inuit Health in the Circumpolar World Course Code: FMED 527 Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 0 Hiring Unit: Department of Family Medicine Course Number and Title: FMED 527 Inuit Health in the Circumpolar World Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 40 Position Summary: Family Medicine : The course will explore the histories, perspectives and contemporary realities of Inuit health in the four regions of Inuit Nunangat (the Inuit homeland) with a particular focus on the Nunavik region of northern Quebec. The Inuit of Nunavik are the second-largest Inuit community in Canada, with a population of 11,000 living in 14 communities. Nunavik is part of the McGill Réseau universitaire intégré de santé et services sociaux. That gives McGill’s Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences a unique rationale, and opportunity, to offer, under the sponsorship of Family Medicine, a course on Inuit health in the Canadian context. Duties and Responsibilities: Assist with course management Assist with course delivery Assist with student inquiries Assist with assessing student assignments Qualifications: Candidate must be a Ph.D. or M.Sc. student in the Family Medicine Program. Students who have completed FMED 506 Indigenous Perspectives are preferred. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-05 Position End Date: 2027-04-14 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-23 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/5858-Cote-des-Neiges/Teaching-Assistant---FMED-614_JR0000081944": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - FMED 614",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-06\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Family Medicine Course Title: Foundations of Mixed Methods Research Course Code: FMED 614 Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 0 Hiring Unit: Department of Family Medicine Course Number and Title: FMED 614   Foundations of Mixed Methods Research Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 30 Position Summary: Family Medicine: Training in how to conduct mixed methods research (the most common types of mixed methods in primary research and literature review).     Duties and Responsibilities: Assist with course development Assist with course delivery Assist with student inquiries Assist with assessing student assignments Qualifications: Candidate must be an M.Sc. student year 2+ or Ph.D. student in the Family Medicine program with mixed methods experience and/or training. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-05 Position End Date: 2027-04-14 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-23 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
