    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Leacock-Building/Jewish-Studies-334-Teaching-Assistant-Winter-2027_JR0000082904": {
      "title": "Jewish Studies 334 Teaching Assistant Winter 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Jewish Studies Course Title: Jews and Muslims: A Modern History. Course Code: JWST 334 001 Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 100 The Department of Jewish Studies is searching for eligible candidates as a Teaching Assistant for the Winter 2027 term.   Applicants must be students registered in a McGill MA or PhD graduate program in the Winter 2027 term. ·         Verify Minerva that any TA positions you plan to apply for in Winter 2027 don’t conflict with your own courses.   Teaching Assistants will be required on campus to perform their tasks in person and attend all the lectures.   Postings are considered tentative pending the final determination of course offerings and enrolments. Due to insufficient enrolment in courses, not all courses posted may require a TA.   Position Summary: Responsibilities include hosting and facilitating discussions on Zoom with small groups of students; grading exams and term papers; attending lectures and reading course materials; communicating with students regarding myCourses; submitting assignments, etc. Qualifications: Positions are only available to McGill graduate students with an appropriate academic background. Previous study in the subject of the course.   Legend: JWST = Jewish Studies   Positions available at 100 hours: JWST 334 Jews and Muslims: A Modern History, Christopher Silver (1 position)   Hourly Salary: $38.46 Total Hours of Work per Term: 100 Position Start Date: 2027/01/05 Position End Date:   2027/05/07 Deadline to Apply: 2026/11/08   HOW TO APPLY: If you have an active job at McGill, you can apply as an INTERNAL APPLICANT : log in and apply through   Workday , using your McGill username and password. IMPORTANT: You MUST first connect to McGill's VPN before you log in to Workday ( instructions on how to download VPN) . If you do not have an active job at McGill, you must apply as an EXTERNAL APPLICANT : navigate to the McGill Career website . First, sign in to your candidate account or create one, as applicable. IMPORTANT: To create your account, make sure to use a personal email address (and NOT an @mail.mcgill.ca or @mcgill.ca email address). Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2026-10-26 Position End Date: 2027-05-07 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-08 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Montreal-Neuro/Post-Doctoral-Researcher_JR0000082354": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Researcher",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Postdoctoral Fellow, Multimodal Machine Learning Role Overview The Postdoctoral Fellow will develop techniques to build a foundation model of human neurotypes by integrating cognitive task performance, conversational text, and biometric data. The core challenge is utilizing self-supervised representation learning to construct a continuous latent manifold of individual differences. Responsibilities ● Develop tokenization techniques and self-supervised learning methods to integrate diverse data streams (cognitive results, text, creative choices, psychometrics). ● Train models utilizing novel multi-modal architectures to project qualitative and quantitative data into a unified latent space. ● Work closely with a psychometrics postdoc to distinguish genuine cognitive variation from measurement error, device effects, and language confounds. ● Design rigorous evaluations focusing on participant-level data separation, model robustness, missing modalities, and generalization. ● Scrutinize models for bias, unsupported sensitive inferences, and stability, ensuring responsible data usage in collaboration with governance bodies. ● Collaborate with software engineers on data preprocessing pipelines and model integration within the game's cloud infrastructure. Qualifications ● PhD in machine learning, computer science, computational neuroscience, statistics, or a related discipline. ● Proficiency in Python and modern deep learning frameworks. ● Experience with High-performance Computing Infrastructure and GPU clusters. ● Strong research background in multimodal modeling, representation learning, and statistical evaluation of machine learning models. ● Preferred: Experience with self-supervised learning, natural language processing (NLP), cognitive modeling, and privacy-aware methods. This is a one-year contract, with the possibility of extension. Compensation to be determined based on experience and qualifications. To apply for this position, please submit the following: A curriculum vitae (CV) detailing your educational background, research experience, and publications. Contact information for at least two academic or professional references. This position is covered by the AMURE collective agreement: https://www.mcgill.ca/hr/employee-relations/assocs-unions Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Annual Salary: $60,000.00 Hours per Week: 35 (Full time) Location: Montreal Neuro. Supervisor: Associate Professor Position Start Date: 2027-01-01 Position End Date: 2027-12-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-30 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Montreal-Neuro/Post-Doctoral-Researcher_JR0000082153-1": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Researcher",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: The Citadel of the Mind Project: The Citadel of the Mind is an ambitious research initiative supported by a Schmidt Sciences Polymath Award . The project aims to replace traditional psychiatric diagnostic \"buckets\" with a continuous, high-dimensional AI foundation model of human neurotypes. To achieve the massive global scale required, data collection will be driven by a mobile and browser-based puzzle video game, which will serve as a dual data engine, capturing quantitative metrics through cognitive tests and qualitative data through conversational AI interactions with non-playable characters (NPCs). Postdoctoral Fellow, Cognitive Assessment and Psychometrics Role Overview Working with Professor Blake Richards, the postdoctoral fellow will adapt standard cognitive assessments into engaging mechanics within a research-focused video game. The goal is to capture reliable, interpretable measures of working memory, response inhibition, cognitive flexibility, time perception, and other cognitive metrics. Responsibilities Adapt established cognitive tasks for the game, specifying intended constructs, scoring methods, and interpretation limits. Collaborate with a software engineer to translate task designs into implementation specifications, including stimuli, response timing, and calibration. Design and execute pilot studies, initially collecting baseline multi-modal data across a cohort of over 1,000 participants. Evaluate how device variations, gameplay experience, and cultural context affect measurement comparability and performance. Develop reproducible analysis pipelines to test measurement quality, reliability, and validity. Partner with the machine learning team to define interpretable features and target evaluations for multimodal models. Qualifications: PhD in cognitive neuroscience, psychology, or a closely related discipline. Demonstrated expertise in psychometrics, experimental design, and evaluating measurement reliability. Proficiency in statistical analysis and reproducible research. Experience with coding in Python. Preferred: Experience with latent variable models, item response theory (IRT), adaptive testing, or gamified assessments. This is a one-year contract, with the possibility of extension. Compensation to be determined based on experience and qualifications. To apply for this position, please submit the following: A curriculum vitae (CV) detailing your educational background, research experience, and publications. Contact information for at least two academic or professional references. This position is covered by the AMURE collective agreement: https://www.mcgill.ca/hr/employee-relations/assocs-unions Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Annual Salary: $60,000.00 Hours per Week: 35 (Full time) Location: Montreal Neuro. Supervisor: Associate Professor Position Start Date: 2027-01-01 Position End Date: 2027-12-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-30 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McGill-College-2001/Invigilator--Dentistry-_JR0000082892": {
      "title": "Invigilator (Dentistry)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Job Description Helps administer the delivery of the exams and documentation Ensures that students comply with regulations with respect to exams. Alerts professor when students have a question or a concern, if arises. At the end of the examination period, collects all exam materials and ensures nothing is left behind in the examination room(s). Responsible for reporting back to the administrator/program director and ensure delivery of the exams.    EDUCATION/EXPERIENCE REQUIRED Understanding and commitment to maintaining appropriate standards of confidentiality and security of materials. Adhering to McGill University policies and procedures. Ability to work independently. Dependability, reliability, punctuality. Please note that all exams are scheduled in Room 102 and Room 222 1. DENT 437 (CDM) – Monday December 7th (1-4 PM) 2. DENT 240 (Dental Ana & Occl) – Wednesday, December 16th (9-12 PM) 3. DENT 415 (Ortho) – Wednesday, December 16th (1-4 PM) 4. DENT 322 (Radiology) – Thursday, December 17th (9-12 PM) 5. DENT 222 (Intro to Radiology) – Thursday, December 17th (1-4 PM) If applying, please note that you must be available to invigilate all above mentioned exams. Exam period Start Date: Monday December 7th, 2026 Exam period End Date: Thursday, December 17th Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: (TSUM Invigilator) $18.00 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-04 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/MacdonaldStewart-Bld/Research-Assistant-2_JR0000082873": {
      "title": "Research Assistant 2",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Supervisor: Dr. J. Ronholm, Department of Food Science and Agricultural Chemistry Position Summary: The candidate will have expertise in polar microbiology, astrobiology, and cell culture. The work will involve measuring the toxicity field samples and bacterial isolates from Martian analogue sites using cell cultures. Qualifications: Master's Degree required.  M.Sc. candidate or M.Sc. degree in microbiology with experience using samples and bacterial cell cultures from Martian analogue sites. The candidate will be required to split their time between Macdonald Campus and the MUHC. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $32.15 Hours per Week: 35 (Full time) Location: MacdonaldStewart Bld Supervisor: Associate Professor Position Start Date: 2026-10-23 Position End Date: 2027-07-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-30 This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Various/Course-Lecturer-EDEE-253-782-OFNIE-W27_JR0000082848": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer EDEE 253 782 OFNIE W27",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Office of First Nations and Inuit Education (OFNIE) Course Number-Section and Title: EDEE 253 782 - Kindergarten Classroom Pedagogy Course Description: Elementary Education: An orientation to the Kindergarten curriculum. Integration of the school subject areas (language arts, second language, mathematics, social sciences, science, expressive arts, moral and religious education, and physical education) in a manner appropriate to the developmental level of the pre-school child. Posting Dates : October 26 - November 9, 2026 Application Deadline: November 9, 2026; 11:59 p.m. CL Credits: 3 (This course is not team-taught) Number of Positions Available: 1 Term: Winter 2027 Course Dates : January 4 - 28, 2027 Contract dates : December 21, 2026 - February 12, 2027 Schedule: Monday, Tuesday, Thursday from 5:30-8:45pm Location: Gesgapegiag Travel, taxi/mileage, accommodation and per diems are covered from Montreal by the Office for instructors who require it. Teaching Qualification Requirements (TQRs): Education A Master’s degree (in exceptional circumstances applicants without a Master’s degree but with significant research or professional experience related to the course content may be considered). Experience Expert knowledge of course content as outlined in the course description. Knowledge of the Quebec Education program (QEP) and the curriculum of the partnering school board with whom this course is being given in collaboration. Familiarity with the 13 professional competencies specified in the Ministère de l’Éducation Knowledge of the impact of colonialism on Indigenous communities and proven record integrating pedagogical approaches anchored to equity, diversity and inclusion. Experience as a teacher,  administrator, and/or consultant in the partnering school board for which this course is being delivered. Experience teaching in the Quebec elementary school system. Previous successful experience teaching similar course(s) to pre-service teachers. Previous successful experience teaching similar courses in Indigenous communities. Experience developing curriculum, instructional approaches and/or policy grounded in Indigenous knowledge and pedagogies. Other Adherence to evolving program requirements concerning course content and program-developed competencies; course content and references must reﬂect the most current advances in the ﬁeld. The course instructor is expected to demonstrate capacity and willingness to modify the course to the educational, social, and cultural context of the community in which it is being delivered. Availability to participate in course coordination and planning activities prior to the course start date (approximately two weeks in advance; online format). Demonstrated knowledge and/or experience with Indigenous issues in First Nations and Inuit contexts, for example through prior teaching, community engagement, or relevant academic or professional work.   Information supplémentaires/Additional Information:   Effective dates of the appointment are December 21, 2026 - February 12, 2027 . Course dates are January 4 - 28, 2027 . This course will be offered in person in Gesgapegiag following the schedule above. You are covered by the working conditions specified in the McGill Course Lecturers and Instructors Union collective agreement; https://www.mcgill.ca/hr/files/hr/mcliu_collective_agreement_-_final_version_for_printing_0.pdf . McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English Language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Please contact ofnie.education@mcgill.ca  with any questions you may have. Hiring Unit: Bureau de l'éducation des Premières Nations et des Inuits (OFNIE) Course Title: Pédagogie de l’enseignement à la maternelle Subject Code: EDEE 253 782 Location: Gesgapegiag Schedule: Les lundis, mardis et jeudis, de 17 h 30 à 20 h 45. Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-09 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Sherbrooke-680/Ingram-School-of-Nursing--Nurse-Practitioner-Program-Winter-2027-Postings_JR0000081151": {
      "title": "Ingram School of Nursing: Nurse Practitioner Program Winter 2027 Postings",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). MCLIU WINTER 2027 NP PROGRAM POSTINGS The Ingram School of Nursing is searching for Supervisor & Student Support Professionals and Course Lecturers for their Nurse Practitioner Program courses for the Winter 2027 semester. AVAILABLE POSITIONS FOR: SUPPLEMENTAL NUR3 647: Course Lecturer   DEADLINE TO APPLY IS OCTOBER 27, 2026, INCLUSIVELY.   For details job descriptions and information regarding TQRs, please visit: https://www.mcgill.ca/nursing/about/employment-opportunities HOW TO APPLY: Internal Applicants: Internal applicants are McGill employees who currently hold an ACTIVE contract at McGill University and are working during the current semester. Knowledge Base Guide on how to apply: https://www.mcgill.ca/hr/files/hr/workday_guide_for_candidates_internal_eng.pdf Current McGill employees must apply via internal postings. These are accessed using the ‘Find Jobs’ report in Workday. External Applicants : External applicants are any applicant who do not currently hold an active contract at McGill University. This applies for employees who may have had contracts in the past , but do not currently hold an active contract. They cannot apply internally. Knowledge Base Guide on how to apply: https://www.mcgill.ca/hr/files/hr/workday_guide_for_candidates_extenal_eng.pdf The website will direct you to create a Workday account. McGill Careers Website: https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers Please note, applicants must use a personal email address when creating this account . . Applicants must use their legal names when creating their Workday candidate home accounts. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Laird-Hall/Sales-Assistant-A_JR0000082861-1": {
      "title": "Sales Assistant A",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Campus Store Macdonald Campus (Centennial Center) Student Housing & Hospitality Services Position Summary: The Campus Store is committed to offering robust experiential learning opportunities to all casual employees. As a Sales Assistant A, you will gain real life experience in a retail environment. This will include learning to work as a team to offer quality service and products to the McGill Community.  Description:   The Sales Assistant A position, involves performing many tasks, such as:  • Customer Service (which includes, but is not limited to):  • Greeting customers in a friendly manner  • Ensuring customer and staff safety  • Ensuring customers leave with a lasting positive impression  • Assisting in the many facets of order fulfillment process which include, but are not limited to:  • Finding and gathering all items on the web orders assigned to you  • Verifying that all items picked are correct  • Preparing & packing, in a timely manner, all orders that need to be shipped or picked up  • Handing out web orders and modifying the status of web orders  • Shelving books, folding/hanging clothes, restocking and merchandising  • Pricing products, ensuring that all product codes are correct, lifting 30lbs boxes and bins  • Processing sales transactions, looking up products in the database  • Other duties assigned  • Shifts assigned are expected to range between 3 and 8 hours per week. Requirements:   • Must be a McGill student  • Must be available for 2 days of training  • Must be a self-starter, punctual, organized, efficient and detail oriented  • Must be fluent in English; the ability to speak French is an asset  • Must be able to lift boxes of up to 30lbs  • Must wear pants and sturdy, closed-toed shoes  • Prior experience as a cashier, in customer service, and in shipping is an asset  Supporting cross-departmental efforts and initiatives, specifically working alongside SHHS teams, like Residence Life (Thrift McGill) to achieve shared goals.  • Knowledge of French and English. McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English.  This position is covered by the AMUSE Collective Agreement     Minimum Education and Experience:   High School Diploma     Hourly Salary:   (AMUSE A) $16.70  Period of contract: October 25, 2026 - May 29, 2027 Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma Minimal or No Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE A) $16.70 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 8 (Part time) Sunday, Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday Position Start Date: 2026-10-25 Position End Date: 2027-05-29 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-04 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Currie-Gymnasium/Assistant-Professor-Kinesiology-Science_JR0000082292": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor Kinesiology Science",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Tenure-track Assistant Professor Position in Kinesiology Science    The Department of Kinesiology and Physical Education (DKPE), one of the three academic units of the Faculty of Education at McGill University, invites applications for a tenure-stream appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor. Applicants must hold a Ph.D. degree in kinesiology or a closely related field; postdoctoral experience is considered an asset. Applications are encouraged from candidates across the full breadth of kinesiology, with priority given to those whose research focuses on (a) motor control (b) biomechanics, or (c) the epidemiology of physical activity, sport, and exercise.  The successful candidate will demonstrate research excellence through high-quality peer-reviewed publications, national and international conference presentations, and a strong record or potential for securing external funding. They will establish an innovative, internationally recognized research program, foster interdisciplinary and transdisciplinary collaborations, supervise master’s and doctoral students in Kinesiology Science, and teach undergraduate and graduate courses in their area of expertise. Through excellence in research, teaching, mentorship, and service, they will contribute to DKPE, McGill University, and the broader scholarly and professional communities. We welcome candidates whose research advances understanding of human movement, physical activity, exercise, health promotion, chronic disease prevention, rehabilitation, mobility, functional capacity, and healthy aging, and who can contribute to addressing current and emerging challenges in health and human performance. In the context of the evolving professional landscape of kinesiology in Québec, candidates who can strengthen links between research, professional practice, and interprofessional collaboration are particularly encouraged to apply.  This position will play a strategic role in advancing the complementary missions of the DKPE and the Sylvan Adams Sports Science Institute (SASSI) at McGill University (https://www.mcgill.ca/sassi/) . The successful candidate will contribute to the advancement and translation of knowledge in sport, physical activity and/or human health across the lifespan, while strengthening SASSI’s mandate to generate and apply innovative research in applied sport science, particularly research aimed at optimizing elite athlete health and performance. We seek a collaborative and forward-thinking scholar who will enrich the DKPE’s collegial academic environment, build sustainable partnerships with academic, clinical, community, public health, and sport organizations, and connect fundamental discovery with meaningful applications in health, sport, and human performance. The ideal candidate will demonstrate the ability to develop productive local, national, and international collaborations. The ability to work in both English and French is an asset.   McGill University is consistently ranked among the world’s leading universities and is located in the heart of Montreal, a vibrant, cosmopolitan, and culturally diverse city where both French and English are widely spoken. With a history of physical and health education dating to 1912, the DKPE offers a B.Ed. in Physical and Health Education, a B.Sc. in Kinesiology, and graduate programs leading to M.Sc., M.A., and Ph.D. degrees across six areas of specialization. The successful candidate will join a dynamic academic community with extensive opportunities to complement and expand existing research strengths in the DKPE, SASSI, the Faculty of Education, and the broader University.    Job Duties: The successful candidate will be expected to establish an innovative and externally funded research program; supervise master’s and doctoral students in Kinesiology Science; and teach undergraduate and graduate courses in their area of expertise. Through excellence in research, teaching, mentorship and service, the successful candidate will contribute to the advancement of the DKPE, SASSI, McGill University, and the broader scholarly and professional communities while helping to prepare the next generation of leaders in Kinesiology Science.     Faculty/Department/Unit: Faculty of Education / Department of Kinesiology and Physical Education Job Type: Tenure-track Rank: Assistant Professor Salary: Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Required Documents: Please address your application to: Dr. Gordon Bloom, Chair, Department of Kinesiology and Physical Education, McGill University. Application materials from each applicant must be submitted via Workday in one portable document file (PDF). Applicants should submit: Letter of application/cover letter; Curriculum vitae; Contact information for three academic references 1-page statement of teaching philosophy and teaching interests 1-page statement of current and future research activities and plans, respectively.   The deadline for all application materials is December 8, 2026 .   Successful candidates will be expected to begin a full-time tenure-track appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor effective August 1, 2027 .   Inquiries may be directed to Dr. Jenna Gibbs, Chair of the Kinesiology Science Search Committee, via the following e-mail address: jenna.gibbs@mcgill.ca Land Acknowledgement  McGill University is on land which has long served as a site of meeting and exchange amongst Indigenous peoples, including the Haudenosaunee and Anishinabeg nations. We acknowledge and thank the diverse Indigenous peoples whose presence marks this territory on which peoples of the world now gather. To learn more about the land and peoples of Tiohtià:ke/ Montreal, please follow this link . McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities.  At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record.  McGill University implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, this email or phone at 514-398-2477.  All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority.  Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Music-527-Sherbrooke/Audiovisual-Assistant-B--CIRMMT-_JR0000082725-1": {
      "title": "Audiovisual Assistant B (CIRMMT)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: CIRMMT   Position Summary: Under the direction of the immediate supervisor, the incumbent may perform some or all of the listed typical functions in the context of the installation and operation of equipment and infrastructure necessary for CIRMMT productions and events (concerts, lectures, seminars, workshops, special events).   Primary Duties: Greets users and answers specific inquiries with a positive attitude. This includes detailed information regarding use of equipment. Sets up props and/or A/V equipment and records events such as lectures, concerts, workshops, and meetings. Schedule audio/video equipment and rooms to record events. Maintains work area and spaces. The list of functions outlined above is representative and not a complete and detailed list of tasks which may be performed by an incumbent. For example, incumbents may be asked to perform some elements of Audio/Video Assistant level “A” tasks in addition to the above listed functions.     Education / Experience: High School Diploma with one year of relevant experience in the field of work.     Other Qualifying Skills and Abilities: High School Diploma and / or equivalent professional experience. Availability for necessary events. Bilingual in French and English Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 1 Year Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE B) $21.00 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 6 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-11-02 Position End Date: 2027-09-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-30 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bronfman-Building/Event-Assistant_JR0000082876": {
      "title": "Event Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-25\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Sustainable Growth Initiative Position Summary: The assistants will help with all aspects of on-site event logistics, including but not limited to registration table, catering logistics, helping in-person participants, helping with room set up and take down. The assistants will participate in training prior to the event and help the organizing team on event-day logistics. The assistants will also ensure that all aspects of the event will conform to sustainability guidelines as described in the sustainability event certification form. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities McGill student in good standing, preferably with experience in hospitality. Must be detail-oriented with strong organizational skills and the ability to manage and follow up on multiple tasks efficiently. Fluent French is an asset. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 1 Year Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE B) $16.85 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 12 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-11-06 Position End Date: 2026-11-07 Deadline to Apply: This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Les-Cours-Mont-Royal/Phonathon-Callers-Fall-2026---Appelant-du-phonathon-automne-2026_JR0000078653": {
      "title": "Phonathon Callers Fall 2026 / Appelant du phonathon automne 2026",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-25\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). POSITION COVERED BY AMUSE-PSAC BARGAINING UNIT OPEN TO BOTH WORK-STUDY AND NON WORK-STUDY STUDENTS Hiring Unit : McGill University Advancement, Annual Giving, Phonathon Job Title : Phonathon Caller Classification: B Salary : $16.85 per hour. 4% vacation pay and compensation for Legal Holidays will be included on each pay Duration : November 8 – December 22, 2026 Schedule : Monday - Thursday 5:30pm - 9:30pm, Sunday 12:00pm - 4:00pm Estimated Number of Hours Per Week: 8 Job Summary : Under the direction of the immediate supervisor, the incumbent may perform some or all of the listed general administration functions. These functions are of moderate complexity and the incumbent performs them using established instructions. Typical Functions : Using established scripts, contacts alumni and parents of McGill students with the aim of securing donations for the McGill Fund and meeting established performance goals. Provides information and answers questions regarding the University’s fundraising and academic initiatives. Some inquiries may require further research into the topic and escalation to the Phonathon Calling Team Lead or Phonathon Manager. Collects and enters donor data and responses into the internal database. The list of functions outlined above is representative and not a complete and detailed list of tasks which may be performed by an incumbent. Minimum Education, Certification and Experience High School Diploma with one (1) year relevant experience in the field of work. Other Qualifying Skills: Must be a student currently enrolled at McGill. Must have excellent verbal communication skills (English and/or French). Experience in sales or call center environment. Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 3 on a scale of 0-4.” Important: please refer to the Candidate Guides when applying as an internal employee or as an external candidate . If you are eligible for the McGill Work Study Program, please ensure to indicate it clearly in your cover letter. POSTE COUVERT PAR LES DISPOSITIONS DE LA CONVENTION COLLECTIVE D’AMUSE OUVERT POUR WORK-STUDY ET POUR LES ETUDIANTS SANS WORK-STUDY Unité d’embauche  : Avancement universitaire - Relations avec les diplômés et Dons annuels - Phonathon Titre du poste : Téléphoniste appelant (Phonathon) Classification  :  B Taux horaire  : $16.85 l’heure. L’indemnité de vacances de 4 % et la compensation pour les jours fériés seront comprises dans chaque paye Durée : Du 8 novembre au 22 décembre, 2026 Horaire  : lundi - jeudi 17h30 - 21h30, dimanche 12h - 16h Heures estimées par semaine : 8 Sommaire du poste  : Relevant du superviseur immédiat, le titulaire du poste effectue certaines (ou la totalité) des tâches administratives de nature générale énumérées. Ces tâches sont d’une complexité modérée et le titulaire du poste les exécute en suivant les instructions établies. Fonctions types : À l’aide de scénarios établis, communiquer avec les anciens étudiants et les parents d’étudiants de l’Université dans le but d’obtenir des dons pour le Fonds McGill et d’atteindre les objectifs de rendement établis. Donner de l’information et répondre aux questions concernant les activités de financement et les initiatives universitaires de McGill. Certaines demandes de renseignements peuvent nécessiter des recherches plus poussées sur le sujet et être transmises au chef d’équipe, Téléphonistes appelants, (Phonathon) ou au gestionnaire de ce dernier. Recueillir et saisir les données et les réponses des donateurs dans la base de données interne. Les tâches énumérées ci-dessus sont représentatives, mais ne constituent pas une liste complète et détaillée des tâches qui pourraient être exécutées par le titulaire du poste. Formation et expérience minimales requises : Diplôme d’études secondaires. Un (1) an d’expérience connexe. Autres compétences et/ou aptitudes: Doit être inscrit en tant qu'étudiant à l’Université McGill. Excellentes compétences en communication (anglais et/ou français). Expérience en vente ou dans un centre d’appel. Connaissance du français et de l'anglais: L'Université McGill est une université de langue anglaise où les tâches quotidiennes peuvent nécessiter une communication verbale et écrite en anglais.  Le niveau d’anglais requis pour ce poste a été déterminé comme étant de niveau # 3 sur une échelle de 0-4. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Arts-Building/Office-Assistant-C-1_JR0000082830": {
      "title": "Office Assistant C-1",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Under the direction of the Associate Dean (Graduate Studies) and the Director of the Office of Arts Education, the incumbent will maintain and update the Faculty’s Handbook for TAs and related orientation materials, liaise with departmental administrators and recent graduate instructors to identify resource updates and professional development needs, and assist in the development, organization, and delivery of workshops to support graduate students in developing their teaching skills and portfolios. Key Responsibilities Review, update, and maintain TA training, orientation, and onboarding materials in consultation with Faculty and departmental stakeholders. Liaise with departmental administrators, Chairs, and Graduate Program Directors (GPDs) to gather information and identify required updates to TA resources and training materials. Consult with graduate instructors and recent graduates to collect feedback on teaching experiences, identify professional development needs, and assess areas for program enhancement. Assist in the development, organization, and coordination of workshops focused on teaching practices, lesson planning, classroom engagement, assessment, and teaching portfolio development. Support the preparation and delivery of workshops, including the creation, revision, and distribution of instructional materials and resources. Coordinate meetings, consultations, and communications with Faculty and departmental partners. Maintain accurate records of feedback, resource updates, and project activities, ensuring timely completion and dissemination of revised materials. Research teaching and learning practices and assist with the development of resources that support graduate student instructors. Provide general administrative and project coordination support to the Office of Arts Education, as required. The responsibilities outlined above are representative of the position and are not exhaustive. Additional related duties may be assigned in accordance with the Office Assistant C classification, including functions associated with Office Assistant levels A and B. Qualifications and Skills Strong knowledge of the university environment, including academic policies, administrative processes, and issues related to teaching, learning, and student assessment. Excellent research, writing, editing, and information synthesis skills, with the ability to translate pedagogical concepts into accessible and practical resources. Strong organizational skills, attention to detail, and demonstrated ability to manage multiple priorities and meet deadlines. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build effective working relationships with academic, administrative, and student stakeholders. Experience coordinating meetings, workshops, training sessions, or other collaborative initiatives. Ability to work independently while also contributing effectively within a team environment. Experience with academic committees, student governance, university service, or policy-related work is considered an asset. Graduate-level experience is required; doctoral-level studies are preferred. Experience as a Teaching Assistant, instructor, or facilitator in a post-secondary environment is a strong asset. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #3 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 2 Years Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE C) $32.00 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 2 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-11-02 Position End Date: 2027-05-17 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-31 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Arts-Building/Office-Assistant-C-1_JR0000082832-1": {
      "title": "Office Assistant C-1",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Additional Job Description Under the direction of the Associate Dean (Graduate Studies) and the Director of the Office of Arts Education, the incumbent will maintain and update the Faculty’s Handbook for TAs and related orientation materials, liaise with departmental administrators and recent graduate instructors to identify resource updates and professional development needs, and assist in the development, organization, and delivery of workshops to support graduate students in developing their teaching skills and portfolios. Key Responsibilities Review, update, and maintain TA training, orientation, and onboarding materials in consultation with Faculty and departmental stakeholders. Liaise with departmental administrators, Chairs, and Graduate Program Directors (GPDs) to gather information and identify required updates to TA resources and training materials. Consult with graduate instructors and recent graduates to collect feedback on teaching experiences, identify professional development needs, and assess areas for program enhancement. Assist in the development, organization, and coordination of workshops focused on teaching practices, lesson planning, classroom engagement, assessment, and teaching portfolio development. Support the preparation and delivery of workshops, including the creation, revision, and distribution of instructional materials and resources. Coordinate meetings, consultations, and communications with Faculty and departmental partners. Maintain accurate records of feedback, resource updates, and project activities, ensuring timely completion and dissemination of revised materials. Research teaching and learning practices and assist with the development of resources that support graduate student instructors. Provide general administrative and project coordination support to the Office of Arts Education, as required. The responsibilities outlined above are representative of the position and are not exhaustive. Additional related duties may be assigned in accordance with the Office Assistant C classification, including functions associated with Office Assistant levels A and B. Qualifications and Skills Strong knowledge of the university environment, including academic policies, administrative processes, and issues related to teaching, learning, and student assessment. Excellent research, writing, editing, and information synthesis skills, with the ability to translate pedagogical concepts into accessible and practical resources. Strong organizational skills, attention to detail, and demonstrated ability to manage multiple priorities and meet deadlines. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to build effective working relationships with academic, administrative, and student stakeholders. Experience coordinating meetings, workshops, training sessions, or other collaborative initiatives. Ability to work independently while also contributing effectively within a team environment. Experience with academic committees, student governance, university service, or policy-related work is considered an asset. Graduate-level experience is required; doctoral-level studies are preferred. Experience as a Teaching Assistant, instructor, or facilitator in a post-secondary environment is a strong asset. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #3 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 2 Years Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE C) $32.00 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 2 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-11-02 Position End Date: 2027-05-17 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-31 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Les-Cours-Mont-Royal/Advancement-Analyst_JR0000082666": {
      "title": "Advancement Analyst",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). University Advancement (UA) promotes McGill to its global community and to the public, engaging alumni, volunteers and friends of the University, and building on McGill’s record of fundraising success in support of University priorities. By joining UA, you can be part of a dynamic and exciting team of professionals that is making an extraordinary impact on the world by advancing the philanthropic and engagement ambitions of McGill University. To find out more about UA, please visit us at www.mcgill.ca/advancement/ua-careers . Position Summary:   The Advancement Analyst provides support to University Advancement (UA), namely in areas related to performance tracking, value for money analyses, benchmarking, trend monitoring cross-departmental analytics initiatives and governance reporting.  They collaborate with team members to support strategic planning and decision-making for UA's Senior Management Team.   Primary Responsibilities:   Collaborate with team members to conduct and present high-level trend analyses, financial projections, value-for-money assessments, and performance reporting, including data mining, forecasting, benchmarking, and analysis of trends and patterns to support strategic planning and decision-making for UA's Senior Management Team. Actively seek and collect industry and benchmarking data that support the business processes/outcomes of McGill University’s fundraising efforts.  Play an active role in Key Performance Indicators development and monitoring. Participate in the drafting of comprehensive review reporting of UA key results including related management discussion and analysis. Write, design, and present project reports, executive summaries on range of philanthropic issues, with particular focus on analytics, key performance indicators (KPIs) and emerging trends. Ensure timely delivery and provide accompanying analyses. Collaborate cross-functionally to enhance reporting tools and data structures for improved insight generation.  Assist with the development of self-service analytics tools, using namely Power BI and Salesforce reporting features. Use analytical techniques to process large volume of data from multiple sources into actionable opportunities, including data analysis and interpretation. Utilize best-in-class data visualization tools to assist other departments in understanding and representing key information. Participate in statistical modeling, analysis and reporting to determine and optimize alignment of resources with the strategic plan, including, data mining, predictive modeling, forecasting and statistical analysis to identify trends and patterns. Effectively translate business questions into analytical solutions,  Ensure data integrity by investigating anomalies in reported numbers from a variety of sources. Assist in the development of data visualization, dashboard and reporting capabilities to enable more effective strategic, tactical, and operational insights and decision-making. Conduct sophisticated ad hoc query data retrievals; provide analysis of data extracts as required. Ensure data integrity by investigating anomalies in reported numbers from a variety of sources throughout Advancement Participate in the development of standards, as led by various Units, in regards to data collection both from internal and external sources. Participate in University Advancement’s Analytics Centre of Excellence with the aim of creating synergies and best-practices in analytics across University Advancement. Keep abreast of technology trends and new BI tools.   Other Qualifying Skills and Abilities: Bachelor’s degree in a pertinent field, Bachelor of Commerce an asset.  Strategic and proactive business minded thinker, with proven expertise in business analysis. Ability to understand business needs formulated by senior management and other internal clients and translate them into actionable analysis and information. Proven experience translating business requirements into reporting specifications, data requirements and actionable insights. Ability to write and design reports and produce and deliver informative, well organized presentations. Strong interpersonal skills and the ability to work collaboratively across all levels of the organization.  Must be able to communicate effectively and confidently with internal clients. Demonstrated effective communication, writing and presentation skills, with the ability to present findings. Experience facilitating meetings and proven experience at driving consensus across multiple stakeholders. Creative, well organized and self-motivated, with strong analytical, planning and problem solving skills.  Able to collect, interpret and synthesize data sets from various sources. High-level of accuracy and attention to detail.  Conscientious and thorough, with a concern for the quality of deliverables.  Proven experience performing detailed work. Demonstrated ability to effectively manage multiple analyses / projects in a timely manner and prioritize work.    Is organized, autonomous, and able to meet strict deadlines. Demonstrated understanding of a variety of different modelling techniques with knowledge of data extraction and manipulation. Knowledge of and experience in building various statistical and machine learning models is an asset. Knowledge of cutting-edge/emerging technologies as they relate to business needs is an asset. Strong SQL skills and experience analyzing data from multiple sources. Experience developing reports and dashboards using enterprise analytics and visualization tools (PowerBi, Python, R, Excel, Salesforce reporting). Demonstrated ability to understand data models and enterprise data environments. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 3 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 05) $73,470.00 - $91,840.00 - $110,210.00 Job Profile: MPEX-COM2C - Development & Alumni Relations - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Executive Director (Advancement Operations) Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-05 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Ferrier-Building/Communications-and-Events-Associate_JR0000082800": {
      "title": "Communications and Events Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Under the direction of the Administrative Manager, Human Resources and Operations, the incumbent coordinates and supports a broad range of programs, events, communications, partnerships, and administrative activities that advance the mandate and strategic priorities of the Administrative Service Centre 6 . The role is responsible for facilitating the planning and delivery of academic, community, and cultural initiatives; supporting stakeholder engagement and outreach; coordinating communications and promotional activities; administering program-related processes and resources; and ensuring the effective day-to-day operation of Institute activities and special projects. Primary Responsibilities: Perform and organize activities that support’s the Faculty of Arts Institute for Indigenous Research and Knowledge (FAIIRK) mandate by coordinating programs, symposiums, student events, and recurring residencies including Writer in Residence, Artist in Residence, Knowledge Holder in Residence, and community engaged initiatives. Drive efforts related to the primary priorities within FAIIRK, ensuring that all activities align with strategic objectives. Administer fellowships (Rathlyn Fellowship). Collaborate with relevant departments and Indigenous/community partners for collaborations and partnerships. Assist in the expansion of FAIIRK activities, supporting operations, outreach, and the growth Plan events related to symposia and guest speakers, including venue booking, scheduling, and organizing travel, honoraria, and logistics for guest speakers. Prepare reports for donors and to increase support for Institute activities. Prepare program communications and materials, including website updates, announcements, briefing notes, agendas, minutes, and post-event summaries. Work with the Finance Manager to coordinate budgets, including requisitions, expense reports, vendor payments, and expenses for events and miscellaneous programming Perform other administrative tasks, general office duties and aiding other projects as needed (including document preparation, scheduling meetings, and managing communication between faculty, students, and external partners). Prepares and disseminates information about FAIIRK’s (courses, research, funding, awards, events, community initiatives) via web articles, ads, periodicals, brochures, posters, slides, and audio/visual media. Ensures web presence follows best practices; performs site maintenance and updates. Administer social channels (e.g., Instagram, Facebook, LinkedIn). Identifies opportunities to expand online presence and engagement, developing content that centers Indigenous voices and stories from students/staff/faculty/guests etc. Stays abreast of communications needs for Indigenous Studies, FAIIRK Collaborates with campus communications teams Builds partnerships and collaborations with Faculty of Arts Communications, Communications and External Relations, First Peoples’ House, the Office of Indigenous Initiatives, the Office of First Nations and Inuit Reviews and approves copy and visuals to ensure accurate and respectful Indigenous representation across web, social, print, and media. Coordinates production of communications and promotional materials Assists in preparing briefings and speaking notes for the Program Director and any other persons involved in events. Drafts official correspondence, letters, applications on behalf of FAIIRK. Prepares and disseminates FAIIRK’s newsletter including writing articles on events, student highlights, Fellowships, jobs etc. Administer mailing lists and contact databases for students, faculty, alumni, and community partners. Responsible for the organization and the smooth functioning of FAIIRK’s annual events (Artist in Residence, Writer in Residence, Knowledge holder, symposiums, book launches, guest speakers). Prepares programs, reserves facilities, liaises with vendors and guests, and provides on-site support to ensure smooth delivery and timely issue resolution. Promotes events and ensures high-quality electronic/print materials (invitations, programs, flyers, etc.). Tracks event/communications budgets; processes invoices, honoraria, and reimbursements. Compiles metrics and outcomes; produces year-end PDF reports for the Dean and funders. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities: Experience working with Indigenous communities and supporting Indigenous faculty, staff, and students, including building respectful relationships with Knowledge Holders, Elders, artists, writers, and community partners. Strong organizational, project management, and event coordination skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and meet deadlines. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including report writing, digital content creation, marketing, and outreach. Experience in event logistics, website and social media management, financial administration, budgeting, and donor or grant reporting. Strong analytical, problem-solving, and administrative skills, with sound judgment, discretion, and confidentiality. Proficiency in Microsoft Office, web content management systems, social media, and design tools such as Canva and Adobe Creative Suite. Ability to work independently and collaboratively in fast-paced environments, with experience in post-secondary, non-profit, research, cultural, or public-sector settings.   Note: Preference will be given to candidates of Indigenous identity in filling this position. Included in this category are First Nations (status or non-status), Inuit and Métis people, as well as Native Americans and Alaskan Natives from the USA. Candidates with existing relationships to local Indigenous communities are especially encouraged to apply. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 4 (fluent) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: DEC III 3 Years Related Experience / Hourly Salary: (MUNACA Level G) $31.19 - $38.65 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Administrative Manager Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-06 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Burnside-Hall/Multimedia-Assistant_JR0000081839": {
      "title": "Multimedia Assistant",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary Reporting to the Multimedia Services Manager, Live Events, the Multimedia Specialist performs a variety of technical duties related to the production, setup, and distribution of audiovisual materials and equipment as well as the editing of communication material that promote McGill’s research, teaching, innovation, and accomplishments.  The incumbent assists with video projects, providing creative input as needed.  They provide technical guidance to clients on equipment use and perform routine equipment maintenance. Major Duties and Responsibilities: Provides technical audio-visual support. Receives client requests, verifies equipment availability, arranges for unit services, and maintains media assets and project-related information. Provides clients with information on unit policies and procedures, service availability, and the proper use of equipment. Assists with shoot logistics, media assets and other project particulars with clients.  Ensure that projects meet established timelines and specifications. Prepares, installs, sets up, and demonstrates audio-visual equipment, including motion picture, slides, and video/data projectors, public address systems, audio/video players, laptop computers and peripherals. Edits a range of video types. Integrates graphics in accordance with McGill’s visual identity guidelines. Works with faculty members to adapt PowerPoint presentations for use in video productions. Creates relevant metadata records and upload videos to social media platforms. Provides professional expertise on post-production software solutions and best-practice industry trends. Maintains the unit’s archive and help organize digital media assets. Maintains the equipment storage area and ensures the work area is clean, organized, and secure. Picks up and delivers equipment and assists with the construction and modification of audiovisual facilities, as required.   The duties and responsibilities outlined above are representative of the position and are not intended to be exhaustive. The incumbent may be assigned other related duties consistent with the nature and classification of the position. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities: Knowledge of video-editing and motion-graphics tools and techniques. Organizational skills and ability to work within established timelines and procedures . Valid Quebec Class 5 driver's license As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 4 (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 6 Months Related Experience / Hourly Salary: (MUNACA Level A) $21.99 - $27.24 Hours per Week: 35 (Full time) Supervisor: Associate Director, Creative Services Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-04 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/James-Admin-Bldg/Project-Manager-Transformation-and-Process-Improvement_JR0000080104-1": {
      "title": "Project Manager Transformation and Process Improvement",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Reporting to the Director, Process Improvement and Client Orientation in the Office of the Vice-President, Administration and Finance, the Project Manager plays an important role in advancing organizational transformation and business process optimization initiatives. Under general direction, the incumbent develops and implements assigned projects; serving as a single point of contact throughout the project lifecycle. The Project Manager develops detailed work plans, schedules, project estimates, resource plans, and status reports and works collaboratively with stakeholders across a matrix organization. The incumbent conducts project meetings and follows up on project issues, schedule and risks. They also ensure adherence to quality standards and review project deliverables. Primary Responsibilities: Plan and oversee a portfolio of organizational transformations (operating model, culture, service redesign, etc.) and business process optimization projects. Ensure projects are coherent and aligned with optimal sequencing to maximize operational efficiency, enable effective change adoption, and achieve the University’s objectives of transformation. Manage and implement transformation and business process improvement projects using established project management methodologies, including project planning, scheduling, monitoring and execution Provide senior professional expertise; communicate goals, expectations, roles and status updates; follow up on outstanding action items and collaborate with stakeholders to identify and resolve issues as needed. Identify risks and develop risk mitigation plans. Develop measurable project goals, objectives and benefits and monitor progress. Ensure consistency and quality of implementation through the execution of project management best practices. Identify and manage project dependencies and critical path activities to ensure projects remain on schedule and meet established goals/objectives. Develop and manage project plans covering documentation, design, testing, and implementation phases. Monitor budgets, schedules, and key performance indicators to deliver expected results and support effective change management. Coach and guide teams on best practices in organizational transformation, continuous improvement and change management. Identify and realize opportunities for value creation such as cost reduction, improved client experience and operational efficiency. Establish and maintain effective relationships, communication tools and systems with stakeholders to ensure that required information, resources and expertise are available to support the achievement of project milestones and schedules. Obtain direction and decisions from the Project Steering Committee or the Project Sponsor, in a timely manner, on any issues that impede the completion of the deliverables within the project schedule and budget. Develop and implement effective project communication plans, including planning, monitoring and adapting communications to address any arising needs throughout projects. Liaise with stakeholders, partners and other interested parties; collaborate with multidisciplinary teams and provide cross-functional guidance. Contribute to the development of continuous improvement and client orientation standards and methodologies for the Office of the Vice-President, Administration and Finance Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Undergraduate degree in business affairs, project management or a related field. Desired: 5-10 years’ experience managing similar types of organizational transformation and business process optimization projects Experience managing projects portfolios and programs PMP certification highly preferred Knowledge and proficiency in process improvement methodology and tools, operating model review, service redesign and culture changes Ability to work with multiple stakeholders and prioritize requests. Mastery of PMBOK principles or other recognized project management methodologies. Fluent in English and French, skilled communicator, both in writing and speaking. Ability to work in a team, responsible, quality oriented and able to meet deadlines, aptitude for clear and concise thinking, flexible and able to adapt to change Lean Six Sigma and/or Prosci certification a plus High degree of sensitivity to challenges inherent to culture changes Demonstrated ability to engage stakeholders and bring initiatives to fruition. Excellent facilitation, communication, and influential leadership skills. Comfortable in a matrix environment, with a proactive, solution-oriented approach As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 4 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 5 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 07) $92,150.00 - $115,190.00 - $138,230.00 Job Profile: MPEX-ADM3A - General Administration - Senior Professional/Manager Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Director, Process Improvement and Client Orientation Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-06 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Hosmer-House/Fall-2026-Grader---SPOT_JR0000082852-1": {
      "title": "Fall 2026 Grader - SPOT",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: School of Physical & Occupational Therapy Position: Grader Employment Period: October 26th, 2026 – December 31st, 2026. Rate of Pay: $18.00/hour Duties: Grading of student assignments . There will be no contact with the students enrolled in the course. Meeting with course supervisor before and after grading of answers.   OCC1 620 Course Coordinator: Sara Saunders Hours: 50 Positions Available: 1 Requirements : Must have clinical experience as an occupational therapist and previous teaching experience at a university level. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hourly Salary: $18.00 Position Start Date: 2026-10-23 Position End Date: 2026-12-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-05 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
