    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Duff-Medical-Bldg/Research-Associate_JR0000079870": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary Research Program Development Develop and conduct research projects, protocols, methodologies and standardized procedures supporting BRAVO research objectives. Design and implement innovative research methods and quality monitoring approaches for advanced therapeutics and organoid biomanufacturing. Analyze and interpret research data and prepare scientific reports, publications and presentations. Ensure the quality, integrity and reproducibility of research activities and outcomes. Research Coordination and Infrastructure Coordinate the implementation of BRAVO research infrastructure across participating institutions. Collaborate with investigators, partner institutions and technical personnel to support research infrastructure integration. Participate in equipment evaluation, procurement, qualification and implementation activities. Develop and maintain Quality Management System (QMS) procedures and monitor project milestones to support research objectives. Research Leadership and Knowledge Translation Provide functional supervision, guidance and training to Research Assistants, trainees and technical personnel. Develop standardized research procedures and promote collaboration across multidisciplinary teams. Disseminate research findings through publications, presentations, reports and stakeholder engagement. Prepare grant applications, progress reports and other scientific documentation. Strategic Research Development Establish collaborative partnerships with academic, clinical, government and industry stakeholders. Participate in provincial and national research networks supporting advanced therapeutics and regenerative medicine. Contribute to the sustainability and continuous improvement of BRAVO research programs and infrastructure. Support strategic planning and identify future research and funding opportunities. Qualifications PhD in Bioengineering, Biomedical Sciences, Chemical Engineering, Biological Sciences, or a related field, with at least 5 years of related research experience . Experience in one or more of the following areas: advanced therapeutics, organoid technologies, biomanufacturing, or GLP/GMP-regulated research. Demonstrated experience developing research methods, analyzing scientific data, preparing publications, and contributing to grant applications. Strong scientific writing, analytical, organizational, interpersonal, and research coordination skills. Excellent written and spoken English; French is an asset. Assets: Experience in translational research, technology transfer, or the biopharmaceutical sector. Experience supervising or training research personnel. Certification or training in Good Manufacturing Practices (GMP) or stem cell research (e.g., CASTL, CATTI, NIBRT, EATRIS, ASQ, GpX, Stem Cell Network, ISCT, or ISSCR). Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 4 on a scale of 0-4. Hourly Salary: $35.37 Hours per Week: 35 (Full time) Location: Duff Medical Bldg. Supervisor: Professor Position Start Date: 2026-10-26 Position End Date: 2027-10-25 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-28 This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Education-Building/Course-Lecturer-EDEC-201-760-762-OFNIE-W27_JR0000082801": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer EDEC 201 760/762 OFNIE W27",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Office of First Nations and Inuit Education (OFNIE) Course Number-Section and Title: EDEC 201 760/762 First Year Professional Seminar Course Description: Orientation to the culture and community of school and to teaching as a profession. Focus on the general functioning of schools and complexity of the teacher role. Competencies and working professional portfolios will be addressed . Posting Dates : October 23 - November 6, 2026 Application Deadline: November 6, 2026; 11:59 p.m. CL Credits: 1 (This course is not team-taught) Number of Positions Available: 2 Term: Winter 2027 Course Dates : January 5 - 22, 2027 Contract dates : December 21, 2026 - February 5, 2027 Schedule: Every Tuesday for 4 weeks of 3h 15: 6:00 p.m. - 9:15 p.m. Location: Kahnawà:ke Teaching Qualification Requirements (TQRs): Education A Master’s degree (in exceptional circumstances, applicants without a Master’s degree but with significant research or professional experience related to the course content may be considered). Experience Expert knowledge of course content as outlined in the course description. Knowledge of the Quebec Education program (QEP) and the curriculum of the partnering school board with whom this course is being giving in collaboration. Familiarity with the 13 professional competencies specified in the Ministère de l’Éducation Knowledge of the impact of colonialism on Indigenous communities and proven record integrating pedagogical approaches anchored to equity, diversity and inclusion. Experience as a teacher and/or consultant in the partnering school board for which this course is being delivered. Experience teaching in the Quebec elementary school system. Previous successful experience teaching similar course(s) to pre-service teachers. Previous successful experience teaching similar courses in Indigenous communities. Experience developing curriculum and/or instructional approaches grounded in Indigenous knowledge and pedagogies. Other Adherence to evolving program requirements concerning course content and program-developed competencies; course content and references must reﬂect the most current advances in the ﬁeld. The course instructor is expected to demonstrate capacity and willingness to modify the course to the educational, social, and cultural context of the community in which it is being delivered. Availability to participate in course coordination and planning activities prior to the course start date (approximately two weeks in advance; online format). Demonstrated knowledge and/or experience with Indigenous issues in First Nations and Inuit contexts, for example, through prior teaching, community engagement, or relevant academic or professional work.   Information supplémentaires/Additional Information:   Effective dates of the appointment are December 21, 2026 - February 5, 2027. Course dates are January 5 - 22, 2027. This course will be offered in-person in Kahnawà:ke following the schedule above. You are covered by the working conditions specified in the McGill Course Lecturers and Instructors Union collective agreement; https://www.mcgill.ca/hr/files/hr/mcliu_collective_agreement_-_final_version_for_printing_0.pdf . McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English Language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing.  Please contact ofnie.education@mcgill.ca  with any questions you may have. Hiring Unit: Bureau de l’éducation des Premières Nations et des Inuit (OFNIE) Course Title: Séminaire professionnel 1 Subject Code: EDEC 201 760/762 Location: Kahnawà:ke Schedule: Tous les mardis pendant 4 semaines, de 18 h à 21 h 15 (3 h 15 par séance). Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-06 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Burnside-Hall/Research-Assistant-2_JR0000082239": {
      "title": "Research Assistant 2",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-23\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Mathematics and Statistics Position Summary: Develop methods for uncertainty quantification and calibration to ensure reliable predictions and risk control. Oversees and assists with the design of active-learning strategies and experiments to improve data efficiency and guide experimental decisions. Develop and maintain Python pipelines for statistical modelling, experimentation, and analysis. Contribute to scientific publications, conference presentations, and grant reporting. Develop verifiable retrieval and memory methods for scientific large language model (LLM) agents. Qualifying skills: A PhD in Statistics, Electrical Engineering, Materials Science, Mechanical Engineering, or a related field, with research experience in AI or machine learning, is an asset. Knowledge of machine learning, probabilistic modelling, uncertainty quantification, and model interpretability. Experience in machine-learning-guided materials design or optimization, demonstrated through peer-reviewed publications. Proficiency in Python and scientific machine-learning tools, including reproducible research software development. Experience and Education: A PhD in a related field is an asset. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 4 on a scale of 0-4. Hourly Salary: $32.15 Hours per Week: 5 (Part time) Location: Burnside Hall Supervisor: Professor Position Start Date: 2026-10-13 Position End Date: 2027-02-26 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-29 This position is covered by the Association of McGill University Research Employees (AMURE) collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
