    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Laird-Hall/Night-Steward--Sessional-_JR0000075366": {
      "title": "Night Steward (Sessional)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). This position is based at MacDonald Campus in Sainte-Anne-de-Bellevue, Quebec H9X 3V9 Hiring Unit : Student Housing & Hospitality Services Macdonald Campus Position Summary: Under the direction of the immediate supervisor, performs surveillance duties in University buildings and grounds in order to ensure protection against fire, theft, vandalism, or any other damage. Responds to emergency calls. Major Duties and Responsibilities: Static security agent. Enforces residence rules and regulations. Makes rounds and inspects assigned locations and grounds reporting in by 2-way radio and/or registering on a time clock as necessary. When encountering occupants outside normal hours, verifies their identity, destination, asks for proof of authorization, ensures their registration and expels intruders. Verifies that the doors and windows of premises are locked according to established schedule. Is responsible for surveillance related to crime prevention (such as thefts, break-ins, vandalism, etc.) by immediately contacting McGill security and/or the police. Responds to emergency calls requiring immediate intervention. Verifies the closure of gas valves and all other machinery or equipment that should not be in operation at the time of verification. Reports any unusual or urgent situation, and/or takes appropriate measures to remedy the situation. In the case of emergency, will or may be called upon to assist with the evacuation of staff, students and clients during a fire alarm and direct occupants to the exits. Performs first aid procedures if necessary. Provides information to visitors, answers the telephone, and transmits messages. Completes incident reports. Verifies the condition of fire prevention equipment and alarm systems and vehicles used to make the rounds. On occasion, clears building entrance and spreads materials to prevent slipping. May be called upon to intervene in the event of water damage. Attends courses on security, prevention, first aid and participates in training exercises. The list of duties and responsibilities outlined above is representative and not a complete and detailed list of tasks which may be performed by an employee whose position has been matched to this generic job description. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities: First aid certificate required. Virtual Fire Prevention training session will be scheduled. Must be service oriented and able to maintain a professional manner and appearance at all times. Must have a valid Security Certification. Ability to work as part of a team. Able to remain calm in emergency situations and to solve problems in area of responsibility. Must be able to work closely with the Res Life Team. Oversees the well-being of students within Residences and intervenes whenever necessary. Static security agent. Enforces residence rules and regulations. Makes rounds and inspects assigned locations and grounds reporting in by 2-way radio. Verifies identity, destinations and proof of authorization when encountering occupants outside normal hours. Responsible for surveillance related to crime prevention by immediately contacting McGill Security and or police. Verifies condition of fire prevention equipment, alarm systems, gas valves, machineries or other equipment’s accordingly. You can be assigned to any buildings within SHHS. Reports any unusual or urgent situations. Excellent interpersonal skills. Knowledge of French and English.  McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 4 on a scale of 0-4. Additional information: Evening/overnight sessional position August 16 - May 15 (may be subject to change). Work schedule: Sunday to Thursday, from 8:00 p.m. to 4:00 a.m. (may be subject to change). This position is based at MacDonald Campus. Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 6 Months Related Experience / Hourly Salary: (MUNACA Level B) $23.31 - $28.88 Hours per Week: 40 (Full time) Sunday, Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday Supervisor: Protection Supervisor Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-06 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McGill-College-2001/Teaching-Assistant---INDS-302---W2027_JR0000082812": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - INDS 302 - W2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Equity, Ethics and Policy Course Title: Medical Ethics and Health Law Course Code: INDS 302 Estimated Number of Positions: 2 Total Hours of Work per Term: 180 Position Summary: Facilitating discussions with small groups of medical students. Recruiting and corresponding with student facilitators for discussion sessions. Grading assignments and providing feedback to students. Assisting with the development of examination questions. Selecting relevant case studies and developing accompanying case analyses. Attending lectures and introducing guest and faculty lecturers. Sending reminders and course-related communications to faculty lecturers and student facilitators. Responding to inquiries and communicating with students, administrators, and the course director regarding course-related matters. Converting PowerPoint presentations into standardized course formats. Attending meetings related to course planning, curriculum development, and administration. Performing other course-related duties as assigned by the course supervisor. Qualifications: Must be a registered McGill graduate student. Academic background in a relevant field, such as medical ethics, bioethics, health law, health policy, medicine, public health, philosophy, or a related discipline. Having taken the INDS 302 course is preferred Previous coursework or demonstrated knowledge in medical ethics and/or health law. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Strong organizational and time-management abilities. Ability to work independently and collaboratively in an academic environment. Experience facilitating group discussions and/or teaching is considered an asset. Experience grading academic work is considered an asset.   Please note that Teaching Assistants will be required to be on campus to perform the bulk of their tasks in person. How to Apply To be eligible for a Teaching Assistant position you must be registered at McGill University as a Graduate Student in the term to which you are applying. McGill student not currently employed at McGill University, must apply via external postings. The website will direct you to create a Workday account. Please note, you must use a personal email address when creating this account. Do not use @mail.mcgill.ca or @mcgill.ca email addresses. Candidates who are current McGill employees receiving a salary (not a graduate student stipend) must apply through their already active Workday account. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research are conducted in English, thereby requiring English communication, both verbal and written. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-01 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-02 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Sherbrooke-680/Office-Assistant-C_JR0000079831": {
      "title": "Office Assistant C",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Undergraduate Medical Education **This is a work-study position. Only McGill Students with work-study confirmation letter covering the contract period will be eligible to apply. ** Under the direction of the immediate supervisor, the incumbent may perform some or all of the listed general administration functions. These functions are of high complexity and the incumbent performs them using general instruction. Typical functions include: · Assisting in peak periods with activities such as preparing packages for students and professors, labeling, photocopying, etc. · Assisting in the distribution of packages and other materials during lectures and small group sessions. · Collating, transcribing, and graphing course evaluation info. · Assisting with exam invigilation. · Entering data such as schedules and other information into various systems, generating reports. · Helping with website reviews and updates. · Helping with preparations for graduations activities. · Filing and maintaining records. · Assisting on projects and in the absence of other employees. · Meeting transcription (minute taking). · Zoom session management and set up (breakout rooms etc.) · Other duties as required. Required Skills: Data entry, Word, Excel, web maintenance skills. French. Strong organizational skills, multitasking, attention to detail. Initiative and creativity. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # 4(qualifier) on a scale of 0-4. For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 2 Years Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE C) $17.25 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 15 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-08-26 Position End Date: 2026-12-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-28 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Duff-Medical-Bldg/Teaching-Assistant---PATH-300---W2027_JR0000082786": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - PATH 300 - W2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Pathology Course Title: Human Disease Course Code: PATH 300 Estimated Number of Positions: 2 Total Hours of Work per Term: 90 Position Summary: Must attend scheduled course lectures. Provide weekly office hours to support student learning and answer course-related questions. Assist with the organization and administration of assignments and examinations. Invigilate midterm and final examinations. Assist with grading and marking of examinations and assignments, as directed by the course supervisor. Support the instructor with lecture-related and administrative tasks as required. Qualifications: Must be a registered McGill graduate student. Graduate student in Pathology, Pharmacology, Anatomy, or a closely related biomedical science discipline is preferred. Strong academic record and knowledge of human disease processes. Having taken the PATH 300 course is preferred Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Strong organizational and time-management abilities. Ability to work independently and collaboratively in an academic environment. Experience facilitating group discussions and/or teaching is considered an asset. Experience grading academic work is considered an asset.   Please note that Teaching Assistants will be required to be on campus to perform the bulk of their tasks in person   How to Apply To be eligible for a Teaching Assistant position you must be registered at McGill University as a Graduate Student in the term to which you are applying. McGill student not currently employed at McGill University, must apply via external postings. The website will direct you to create a Workday account. Please note, you must use a personal email address when creating this account. Do not use @mail.mcgill.ca or @mcgill.ca email addresses. Candidates who are current McGill employees receiving a salary (not a graduate student stipend) must apply through their already active Workday account. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research are conducted in English, thereby requiring English communication, both verbal and written. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2027-01-05 Position End Date: 2027-04-30 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-02 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/James-Admin-Bldg/Assistant-Vice-President--Research-Strategy-and-Services_JR0000081050": {
      "title": "Assistant Vice-President, Research Strategy and Services",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). POSITION SUMMARY The Assistant Vice-President, Research Strategy and Services, reports to the Vice-President (Research & Innovation) and is a member of the senior leadership team responsible for advancing the University's research enterprise. The incumbent provides strategic and operational leadership for the University’s research development and sponsored research services and oversees a complex portfolio supporting researchers, research teams, faculties, affiliated hospitals, and research institutes throughout the research funding lifecycle. The incumbent develops a strong culture of service, collaboration, and accountability. He/ she will establish expectations, service standards, and effective ways of working to ensure that researchers and academic units receive timely, clear, coordinated, and solutions-oriented support and that institutional requirements are translated into effective and workable processes. Working closely with the Vice-President (Research & Innovation), Deputy Vice-President, Associate Vice-Presidents, Deans, Directors, and other senior leaders, the Assistant Vice-President provides strategic advice on institutional research priorities, governance, policy, infrastructure, performance metrics, and emerging funding opportunities. The incumbent serves as a catalyst for institutional collaboration, fostering interdisciplinary partnerships and driving continuous improvement in the University's capacity to secure and successfully manage major research investments. The Assistant Vice-President represents the University and the Research & Innovation portfolio on internal and external committees, and works collaboratively with governments, funding agencies, partner institutions, foundations, industry, and other stakeholders to advance the University's research mission and strategic objectives.   PRIMARY RESPONSIBILITIES Strategic Research Development and Major Initiatives Provide strategic leadership for the identification, development, prioritization, and execution of major institutional research initiatives and funding competitions, including CFI, CERC, CFREF, NFRF and other strategic funding opportunities. Lead the development of high-value institutional funding proposals, partnership agreements, and implementation plans that position the University for success in competitive national and international funding programs. Identify opportunities to strengthen interdisciplinary research and foster collaboration across faculties, research institutes, affiliated hospitals, and external partners. Research Services Lead the University’s sponsored research services and oversee effective delivery across the full funding lifecycle, including funding opportunity identification, application review and submission, sponsored research agreements, amendments and ongoing award administration. Oversee service delivery for research software, cloud-, and advanced research computing services to researchers. Ensure responsive, consistent, and high-quality services to individual researchers, research teams, Faculties, affiliated hospitals, and research institutes. Establish clear service standards, accountabilities, decision pathways, and escalation mechanisms, and use service data and feedback to improve the researcher experience. Lead and develop high-performing teams and strengthen organizational capacity, project management, coordination, and continuous improvement across the portfolio.   Institutional Research Strategy and Planning Lead the development and implementation of institutional policies, programs, procedures, and best practices that support research excellence and strengthen the University's research environment. Lead institution-wide strategic projects related to research planning, -infrastructure, and capacity-building, including the University's Strategic Research Plan and the implementation and monitoring of related action plans. Advise the Vice-President (Research & Innovation) on institutional research priorities, emerging opportunities, policy issues, infrastructure, and other complex matters affecting the research enterprise. Prepare related briefings, reports and recommendations. Monitor developments in the provincial, national, and international research funding landscape and assess their implications for the University. External Relations and Institutional Representation Build and maintain productive relationships with federal and provincial governments, funding agencies, foundations, industry partners, research organizations, and peer institutions to advance institutional research priorities. Represent the University and the Research & Innovation portfolio on internal governance committees, external advisory bodies, and sector-wide initiatives.    The ideal candidate will possess a graduate degree and a demonstrated record of progressive senior leadership in research administration, research strategy, or research development within a large, complex organization, preferably in a university, research-intensive, or public-sector environment. They will bring deep knowledge of the Canadian research funding landscape, experience leading institutional research initiatives and partnerships, and a proven ability to navigate complexity while driving organizational effectiveness and service excellence. Success in this role will require strategic leadership, exceptional relationship-building skills, sound judgment, and the ability to lead high-performing teams while aligning people, processes, and resources in support of the University’s ambitious research priorities. Bilingualism in English and French is required.   McGill University has partnered with the leadership advisory firm Odgers for this recruitment. Applications are encouraged immediately to Michael Williams, Andrea Patrick and Krutika Hotwani at  https://careers.odgers.com/en-ca/job/32099 . This is an existing vacancy. The new AVP will assume the position in early 2027. Inquiries or accommodation requests can be directed to  mcgillavp@odgers.com . McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McGill-College-2001/Office-Assistant-C_JR0000082784-1": {
      "title": "Office Assistant C",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-22\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Faculty of Dental Medicine and Oral Health Sciences Position Summary: Assist with student records, admissions, scheduling, Banner data entry. Assist with student and academic events such as orientation, White Coat ceremony, graduation, research events Assist with digital file cleanup and archiving. Assist with creating and maintaining handbooks. Provide assistance during staff absences and vacations. Respond to general queries from students and faculty. Support Student Affairs special projects as needed. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Knowledge of Office suite. Familiarity with Banner and Minerva is an asset. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 3 (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 2 Years Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE C) $17.17 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 35 (Full time) Position Start Date: 2026-10-26 Position End Date: 2027-04-26 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-27 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
