    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Trottier-Building/TISED-Winter-2027-Teaching-Assistants_JR0000082731": {
      "title": "TISED Winter 2027 Teaching Assistants",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). TISED Winter 2027 Teaching Assistants   The Trottier Institute for Sustainability in Engineering and Design (TISED) is searching for eligible candidates as Teaching Assistants for the Winter 2026 term.   Courses include: SEAD 530 – Economics for Sustainability in Engineering and Design (80 hours) SEAD 550 - Decision-Making for Sustainability in Engineering and Design (2 x 50 hours)   Position Summary: Assisting instructor of the course with instruction and evaluation of students. Qualifications: In accordance with the specific needs of the course.  Proficiency in English required as assessed by the instructor.   Estimated Number of Positions: 3 Total Hours of Work per Term: Varies from term to term.   Hourly Salary: $ 38.46   Position Start Date: 2027-01-05   Position End Date: 2027-04-30   Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-03 Position Summary: Assisting instructor of the course with instruction and evaluation of students. Qualifications: In accordance with the specific needs of the course.   HOW TO APPLY   Internal Applicants: Internal applicants are McGill employees who currently hold an ACTIVE contract at McGill University and are working during the fall semester. Knowledge Base Guide on how to apply: https://www.mcgill.ca/politicalscience/files/politicalscience/how_to_apply_for_a_job_for_internal_candidates.pdf Current McGill employees must apply via internal posting. These are accessed using the 'Find Jobs' report in Workday.   External Applicants: External applicants are any applicant who do not currently hold an ACTIVE contract at McGill University. This applies for employees who may have had contracts in the past, but do not currently hold an active contract. They cannot apply internally. Knowledge Base Guide on how to apply: https://www.mcgill.ca/anthropology/files/anthropology/job_application_for_external_candidates-en.pdf   The website will direct you to create a Workday account. McGill Careers website: https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/fr-CA/mcgill_careers   Please note, applicants must use a personal email address when creating this account. Do not use @mail.mcgill.ca or @mcgill.ca email addresses. Applicants must use their legal names when creating their Workday candidate home account.   PLEASE BE ADVISED APPLICATIONS VIA EMAIL WILL NOT BE ACCEPTED McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Leacock-Building/Teaching-Assistant-History---Classical-Studies-510--180-hours--Winter-2027_JR0000082726": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant History & Classical Studies 510 (180 hours) Winter 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: History & Classical Studies Course Title: Environmental History of Latin America (Field) Course Code: HIST 510 001 Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 0 The Department of History & Classical Studies is searching for eligible candidates as a Teaching Assistant for HIST 510 (Environmental History of Latin America - Field) for the Winter 2028 term.   Applicants must be students registered in a McGill MA or PhD graduate program in the Winter 2028 term . ·         Verify Minerva that any TA positions you plan to register for in Winter 2028 don’t conflict with your own courses. Position Summary: Teaching Assistant needed for a 3-week intensive field course in Panama. Duties include organizing logistics for a group of 25 students and staff on the road between remote communities; coordinating with local Panamanian experts and community representatives; helping conduct applied research exercises. Qualifications: Postgraduate training in environmental studies, experience in field-based research and collaboration, excellent Spanish skills, valid driver's license, ability to travel to Panama and work and live in the field in February 2027. Legend: HIST = History   Position available: HIST 510 Environmental History of Latin America (Field), Daviken Studnicki-Gizbert (180 hours) Hourly Salary: $38.46 Total Hours of Work per Term: 180 Position Start Date: 2027/01/05 Position End Date:  2027/05/07 Deadline to Apply: 2026/11/03   HOW TO APPLY   If you have an active job at McGill, you can apply as an INTERNAL APPLICANT : log in and apply through   Workday , using your McGill username and password. IMPORTANT: You MUST first connect to McGill's VPN before you log in to Workday ( instructions on how to download VPN) . If you do not have an active job at McGill, you must apply as an EXTERNAL APPLICANT : navigate to the McGill Career website . First, sign in to your candidate account or create one, as applicable. IMPORTANT: To create your account, make sure to use a personal email address (and NOT an @mail.mcgill.ca or @mcgill.ca email address). Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2026-10-21 Position End Date: 2027-05-07 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-03 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Redpath-Library/Office-Assistant-C--Exams-Packing-Room-_JR0000082635-1": {
      "title": "Office Assistant C (Exams/Packing Room)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: The Office for Student Accessibility & Achievement (SAA) provides support services and reasonable accommodations to McGill students of all levels of study with documented disabilities of either a permanent, temporary, or episodic nature. ​They foster an accessible and inclusive university experience for McGill’s diverse student population in collaboration with other Student Services and the wider McGill community through resources and services. Position Summary: Performs various clerical duties including data entry, support of administrative activities, and verifying documents. Responds to general and student inquiries. Completes administrative forms, keeps records, inputs and verifies data. Assists with the organization of exams and invigilation. Maintains inventory of supplies. Sorts and prepares exams for distribution and delivery. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Possess an understanding of and sensitivity to the needs of students with disabilities. Demonstrated ability to prioritize. Ability to work independently while taking some direction. Must be customer-focused and service-oriented, assisting all clients with tact and diplomacy while maintaining a professional manner and appearance at all times.   Demonstrated resourcefulness and ability to clearly receive, retain and transmit information with a proven ability to communicate clearly, efficiently, effectively and respectfully with diverse stakeholders. Must be accurate and possess superior attention to detail. Demonstrated ability to provide support for various administrative activities. Proven ability to work autonomously and as a member of a team. Excellent problem-solving skills, demonstrated ability to assess and appropriate react in stressful situations.  Strong organizational skills with ability to multi-task, prioritize and work under pressure to meet multiple deadlines in a fast-paced, dynamic environment. Proven discretion in dealing with confidential information.  Demonstrated ease with technological applications with a proven ability to work in a PC environment using, MS Word, Excel and Outlook. Up to 40 hours per week, depending on operational needs. Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day-to-day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 4 (Fluent) on a scale of 0-4 Anticipated Start Date: November 25, 2026 Schedule: Will vary based on exams and other priorities; Additional time may be required. Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 2 Years Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE C) $24.00 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 20 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-11-25 Position End Date: 2026-12-23 Deadline to Apply: This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McConnell-Eng-Bldg/Assistant-Professor_JR0000082008": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). FACULTY POSITION IN MECHANICAL ENGINEERING   Position Description: The Department of Mechanical Engineering at McGill University invites applications for one tenure-track position in Mechanical Engineering, preferably at the Assistant Professor level.   The Department seeks candidates whose research advances the dynamics, control, and autonomy of aerospace systems, with particular interest in unmanned/autonomous aerial vehicles or space systems engineering.   We are particularly interested in candidates working in areas including, but are not limited to, guidance, navigation, and control (GNC), autonomous systems, flight dynamics, and spacecraft dynamics. Examples may include autonomous navigation, multi-agent or cooperative systems, intelligent and resilient autonomy, launch vehicle dynamics and control, proximity operations and on-orbit servicing, and experimental or hardware-in-the-loop validation.   Candidates whose work bridges these areas or integrates emerging approaches such as machine learning, artificial intelligence, perception, or data-driven methods within aerospace systems are also encouraged to apply.   The Department is committed to excellence in teaching in its undergraduate and graduate programs in Mechanical Engineering and values service contributions of its faculty members to the University, the profession and society at-large.   Qualifications and Education Requirements: Candidates must have a Ph.D., preferably with a first degree in Mechanical or Aerospace Engineering, and a strong commitment to excellence in research and teaching. Evidence of outstanding research achievements, or research potential, is essential.   Preferred qualifications include dedication to an environment that supports diversity of population and perspectives; as our students come from a wide range of disciplines, cultures, and backgrounds, we invite candidates to address in their application their ability to teach, supervise, and mentor in this context. Membership or eligibility for membership in a Canadian professional engineering association is required. Proficiency in both English and French, or a demonstrated willingness and ability to acquire French proficiency, is considered an asset, but is not required.   Job Duties: Successful candidates will have a strong commitment to the mission of the University, through outstanding activities in the areas of research, teaching and service.   Faculty/Department/Unit: Faculty of Engineering Department of Mechanical Engineering   Job Type: Tenure Track   Rank: Assistant Professor Salary: Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience.   Expected Start Date: August 1, 2027   Posting Period: Applications will be reviewed starting November 15 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Enquiries can be addressed to Professor Luc Mongeau ( facultysearch.mecheng@mcgill.ca ).   Required Documents: Interested candidates should submit applications using APPLY NOW button in Workday.   Applications should include: Cover letter (1 page); Curriculum vitae including a list of publications; Research achievements and future plans (5 years) including research vision and a description of representative research directions (max 5 pages); Teaching statement (2 pages); A statement on how equity, diversity and inclusion practices are incorporated in professorial activities (1/2 page); Names and email addresses of three to five referees; Copies of up to three recent publications.   McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities.   At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry.   McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record.   McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca .   All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority.   McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McLennan-Library/Indigenous-Initiatives-Librarian_JR0000082708": {
      "title": "Indigenous Initiatives Librarian",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Deadline to apply:  November 27, 2026, 11:59 pm Assistant Librarian or Associate Librarian (tenure track) Duration: three years, with possibility of renewal Salary: salary commensurate with experience    McGill University is committed to building relationships with Indigenous communities and individuals and advancing the ongoing work of truth and reconciliation. To that end, we are hiring an Indigenous Initiatives Librarian to join the McGill Libraries team and bring additional experience to a staff that already provide a number of Indigenous-focused services. This position, for an individual with experience working with Indigenous communities or organizations, is offered with multiple options for the job portfolio; applicants should identify three to four areas of interest from the list of job duties below in their application materials.     Indigeneity in and around McGill and Montreal   McGill University is on unceded land which long served as a site of meeting and exchange amongst Indigenous peoples, including the Haudenosaunee and Anishinabeg nations. McGill is located closest to the Kanien'kehá:ka Nation communities at Kahnawá:ke, Kanehsatà:ke and Akwesasne. The Kanien'kehá:ka Nation is a founding nation of the Haudenosaunee/People of the Longhouse (Iroquois) Confederacy.   The Hochelaga Monument on McGill’s Lower Campus Field commemorates the Iroquoian village of Hochelaga visited by Jacques Cartier in 1535, which was situated in the area around Mont Royal. The newly redeveloped Y-intersection (Tsi Non:we Onkwatonhnhets) nearby features a central pavement design of 13 works of art by Oneida artist Alanah Astehsi Otsistohkwa Jewell.   McGill University features a number of Indigenous programs, including an undergraduate Minor in Indigenous Studies and ad-hoc MA and PhD programs in Indigenous Language Revitalization, numerous research centres including a new Faculty of Arts Institute for Indigenous Research and Knowledges , the Centre for Indigenous Peoples’ Nutrition and Environment on the Macdonald campus, and Indigenization of professional training as part of the Social Work, Education, Law, Nursing, and Medicine programs, and in Continuing Education. The Faculty of Education, through its Office of First Nations and Inuit Education (OFNIE), provides in-community teacher certificate programs, and the School of Social Work has recently expanded its Indigenous Access McGill program to include an online Bachelor of Social Work degree delivered in Cree . In addition, McGill is working to enrol and graduate more Indigenous students through the Indigenous Tuition and Housing Strategy programs . The Office of Indigenous Initiatives (OII), guided by its Onkwehón:we Advisory Council , is the central hub for Indigenous strategies and initiatives at McGill, including the First People’s House . McGill’s Indigenous programs and units all operate within the context of the United Nations Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples , the OCAP principles for Indigenous data sovereignty, and the Truth and Reconciliation Commission’s 94 Calls to Action and McGill’s parallel 52 Calls to Action .   The McGill Libraries host a growing suite of Indigenous-focused collections. In addition to extensive primary materials documenting the fur trade from the colonizer’s perspective, we have collections of Indigenous language dictionaries and bibles, a selection of Northern newspapers, a strong circulating collection in 20th-century history of Indigenous peoples, an up-to-date selection of Indigenous children’s literature, a growing collection of Indigenous-authored memoirs and novels, and works by Indigenous artists in our Visual Arts Collection. The Libraries maintain an active partnership with the Office of Indigenous Initiatives, periodically invite Indigenous speakers to deliver lectures, participate in cataloguing projects to make Indigenous materials more easily findable, and provide liaison services on Indigenous topics.     THE McGILL UNIVERSITY LIBRARIES   Located in Montréal, one of the world’s great multicultural and multilingual cities, McGill University is internationally recognized for its excellence as a leading institution of higher education and research. For nearly 200 years, through the work of dedicated people, McGill has been breaking ground in diverse fields and contributing solutions to some of the world’s most significant issues. McGill is also the most internationally diverse research-intensive university in Canada, with over 32% of its students coming to McGill from more than 150 countries. The University systematically appears on the annual list of the best employers in Montreal.   The McGill University Libraries contribute to society by advancing teaching, learning, and research in a supportive and welcoming environment. We anticipate and respond to the needs of users by providing outstanding collections, excellent services, and spaces that inspire. We work in collaboration with global scholarly and library communities to enhance and facilitate access to knowledge.   The McGill University Libraries has the largest collection in Quebec and is one of Canada’s largest academic libraries with over six million monograph titles and 150,000 e-journals and databases. The Libraries are a member of the Canadian Association of Research Libraries (CARL), the Association of Research Libraries (ARL), the Partenariat des bibliothèques universitaires du Québec (PBUQ), the Center for Research Libraries (CRL), and the HathiTrust Digital Library.     INFORMATION FOR PROSPECTIVE STAFF   Information about the University and the McGill University Libraries can be found on the University’s web site. The McGill Libraries have developed Strategic Priorities for 2025-2030 to guide its actions. As a tenure stream appointee, the successful candidate is expected to satisfy the requirements in the Regulations Relating to the Employment of Librarian Staff. DUTY STATEMENT The portfolio for this position will be selected from the following list. Candidates should identify three to four areas of interest from the list below in their application materials. Part of the job interview will involve mutually exploring the selected areas of interest and meeting with stakeholders from those areas. A more targeted job description will be developed to meet the strengths and interests of the candidate as discussed in the interview, with a written statement of job duties to be negotiated as part of the hiring offer. Reporting line will be dependent on the selected portfolio and will also be documented in the hiring offer. Possible duties for inclusion in the job portfolio:   Collaborate with librarians supporting the schools of Social Work, Education, Law, Medicine, Nursing, and Continuing Education to provide course instruction and collection development, as they pertain to the Indigenization of professional training. Collaborate with librarians supporting faculties with Indigenous-related programs of study and research centres, such as the Indigenous Studies Minor, the ad-hoc MA and Ph.D. program, and the Institute for Indigenous Research and Knowledges, to provide research support, course instruction, and collection development on Indigenous topics. In collaboration with other Libraries’ staff, develop projects and programming related to Indigenous peoples, languages, and cultures, to support Indigenous faculty, staff, students, and community members. Lead these efforts as assigned. Participate in collaborative Indigenous event and exhibition planning, in conjunction with the Exhibition Program Committee and the Libraries’ Events Team. Lead these efforts as assigned. Increase discoverability and access for library materials relating to Indigenous topics. Provide guidance and leadership to Libraries’ staff to advance integration of Indigenous epistemologies, pedagogies, and ways of knowing into Library resources, polices, services, and spaces. Maintain relationships with Indigenous members of the McGill, Montreal, and Quebec communities, include the Office of Indigenous Initiatives, the Onkwehón:we Advisory Council, First People’s House, and proximate Indigenous communities. Chair or be a member of the Libraries' Indigenous Initiatives Committee.     Participate in ceremonial gatherings or cultural activities in community of origin or communities proximate to Montreal. The following more general job duties also apply: Maintain current awareness of emerging technologies and developments in the library, education, and information environments and develop possible responses. Serve on various library, faculty and university committees. Represent the Libraries on university and external committees, as required. Engage in scholarly activities and professional service and develop a dossier for promotion and tenure, consistent with University Regulations.  Advance equity, diversity, inclusion, and accessibility as well as reconciliation with Indigenous Peoples through the McGill University Libraries' services and of the Libraries as a workplace. The duties listed above are representative and not exhaustive; responsibilities may evolve in response to institutional and library needs.     SELECTION CRITERIA   Required:   A Master’s degree in Library or Information Studies (MLIS, MIS or MI) or equivalent. Candidates who are scheduled to receive their qualifying degree by May 2027 will be considered. Experience working with Indigenous communities or organizations is required, with lived experience within a First Nations, Inuit, or Métis community a strong asset. Together, these experiences are vital to understanding the lived realities of Indigenous students and to fostering cultural safety within this position. Hiring will be subject to verification of Indigenous identity as described in McGill’s Policy on Indigenous Membership/Citizenship Validation. Cultural fluency with Indigenous world views, cultures, and values, and recognition of their distinctiveness. Knowledge, understanding, and/or experience of Indigenous scholarship, epistemologies, pedagogies, and ways of knowing in what is now called Canada. User-focused orientation in provision of library services. Sound information and communication technology skills. Excellent organizational, user service, and teamwork skills. High level of professionalism, a commitment to the organization and its mission, and the ability to work flexibly in a changing environment. Ability to understand and respond to priorities and trends in the Library and University environments. Demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity, inclusion, and accessibility of services or work environment. Knowledge of the history of Indigenous communities in Canada, including residential schools, and commitment to reconciliation. Fluency in oral and written English is required. Desired:   Professional experience serving library users in an academic setting. Familiarity with, or training in, OCAP, the First Nations Principles of Ownership, Control, Access, and Possession. Oral and written abilities in French. The McGill Libraries are committed to advancing open, equitable, and sustainable research practices and welcomes candidates who will contribute to these priorities.   APPLICATION PROCEDURE   Applications should address the above selection criteria, be accompanied by: a letter of interest, identifying three to four areas of interest from the list of job duties a curriculum vitae, the names and addresses of three referees.     *** Applicants are encouraged to indicate any accommodation needs related to job duties. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Sherbrooke-1010/Access-Services-Advisor_JR0000082675": {
      "title": "Access Services Advisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit:   Student Accessibility & Achievement   Student Accessibility and Achievement provide an array of learner support services to McGill students of all levels of study, including reasonable academic accommodations for those with documented disabilities of either a permanent, temporary, or episodic nature. ​They foster an accessible and inclusive university experience for McGill’s diverse student population in collaboration with other Student Services and the wider McGill community through resources and services.   Additional Information: Overtime, including evening and weekend hours may be required during peak periods. Position Summary: Under the supervision of the Associate Director, Advising and Learning Supports, the Access Services Advisor serves as a primary point of contact for students with disabilities experiencing barriers to their learning and full participation at McGill. Ensures appropriate support and academic accommodations are identified and implemented to facilitate academic engagement and achievement. Conducts need assessments, provides expert advice to faculty, and collaborates with internal and external stakeholders to ensure seamless service delivery and proper case management. Additionally, the role involves facilitating outreach and education initiatives to raise awareness and foster an inclusive campus environment. Duties and Responsibilities: Conduct comprehensive needs assessments with students facing academic or physical barriers due to disabilities, mental health conditions, chronic illnesses, or other impairments, ensuring accommodations align with both the student’s needs and medical documentation. Collaborate with internal and external professionals when necessary to clarify diagnostic details and ensure a fulsome understanding of the barriers being faced. Advise faculty and appropriate University departments on the development and implementation of reasonable accommodations in the classroom and other university settings. Act as a liaison between students and faculty to ensure effective communication and accommodation provision. Collaborate with key departments within Student Services and across the university to promote understanding and support for students with disabilities. Actively engage in initiatives to enhance campus-wide accessibility and inclusion. Manage a portfolio of outreach, promotional and educational activities designed to increase awareness of services and foster a supportive environment for students with disabilities. Stay current with best practices and emerging trends in disability services, ensuring that all accommodations and services are compliant with legal and university standards. Undertake special projects and initiatives as assigned by the Access Services Advisor – Team Lead, contributing to the continuous improvement of disability support services. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Graduate degree in Educational Psychology, Counselling Psychology, Inclusive Education or other psychosocial field, required.  Experience working in a professional service provider role with people who have disabilities. Experience working on a one-to-one basis with a potentially high stress, constantly changing clientele. Ability to advise faculty and administrators on student disability-related academic accommodations and issues. Proven proficiency in crisis intervention and de-escalation required. Proven experience reviewing medical documentation required.  Experience in the disability field with a particular emphasis on current trends in post-secondary disability service provision, including the Social Model, Universal Design and ecological modes of intervention. Knowledge of post-secondary Universal Design for Learning principles. Must be self-directed and able to work autonomously and as part of a multi-disciplinary team. Tact, diplomacy, excellent interpersonal, organizational and communication skills are essential. Experience leading discussion groups and classes. Awareness and knowledge of adaptive technology for students with disabilities. Proven presentation skills.  English and French, spoken and written. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 4 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 05) $73,470.00 - $91,840.00 - $110,210.00 Job Profile: MPEX-SAF2B - Student Services - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Assoc Dir Stu W Disabilities Position End Date (If applicable): 2027-12-31 Deadline to Apply: McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Park-3625/Building-Supervisor_JR0000082687": {
      "title": "Building Supervisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-20\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Student Housing & Hospitality Services Position Summary: Under the general supervision of the Senior Maintenance Manager.   Undertake building inspections and projects.  Plan, prioritize and execute maintenance schedules and preventative maintenance contracts.  Ensure that facilities are presentable and functional at all times.  Confer with contractors and McGill staff in order to plan and co-ordinate manpower needs.  Assess and resolve administrative problems.   Oversee expenditures within Residence’s operating guidelines.  Purchase supplies and monitor use of materials used in maintenance/repair operations.  Coordinate maintenance with Redbourne and the Le Cite complex for infrastructure services. Directly supervise maintenance staff and schedule/assign workload in different buildings for student housing.  Primary Responsibilities: Provide the best possible research and learning environment for students, staff and visitors through quality services, reliable and cost-effective maintenance, renovations, construction projects and building operations of the University’s physical resources.  Provide the planning, development, implementation, and required to ensure the protection, safety and maintenance of university property and resources. Foster a safe and healthy environment for students, staff, and visitors.  Maintain and improve the standards for the protection, security and maintenance of university property.  Provide quality business operations to support the daily needs of students and staff, such as Buildings and Grounds, Residences, Food Services, Housekeeping, Parking, Mailroom, Printing and Bookstore operations, Athletics and Recreation, etc. Ensure that facilities, operations and maintenance tasks and projects meet or exceed established quality standards. Ensure that regulations, legislation, policies, and procedures concerning buildings and grounds are respected. Directly supervise employees, assist in training, meet regularly to assign, verify work. Prepare performance reviews, probation, trial period documents and weekly work schedules. Approve vacation and absences. Knowledge of French and English. McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Three (3) years related experience. Required to be on site 5 days out of the week. Project management in the construction field, an asset. Able to respond requests and questions from stakeholders and clients on a timely fashion. Must be bilingual. Specialization in the construction field (i.e. Carpentry, plumbing, electrical, HVAC, etc) preferable. Computer literacy, required. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: DEC III 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 03) $63,350.00 - $74,530.00 - $85,710.00 Job Profile: MPEX-LOG1A - Facilities Operations & Maintenance - Professional 1 (Excluded) Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Senior Maintenance Manager Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-30 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
