    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/JGH---Jewish-General-Hosp/Assistant-or-Associate-Professor--Clinical---Department-of-Otolaryngology---Head-and-Neck-Surgery--C1-261008-_JR0000082506": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor (Clinical), Department of Otolaryngology - Head and Neck Surgery (C1-261008)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Description: The McGill University Department of Otolaryngology – Head and Neck Surgery in the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences invites applications for a full-time Contract Academic Staff position at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor (Clinical) with a focus in Head and Neck Surgery and Microvascular Reconstruction. The professorial rank will be determined according to the successful qualifications and experience. This will be a Contract Academic Staff position and does not confer eligibility for tenure. The Department of Otolaryngology – Head and Neck Surgery is a leader in academic medicine. Our educational and research missions support excellent and innovative clinical programs across our teaching hospitals. We offer a dynamic, multidisciplinary, clinical, academic, and research environment that fosters clinical excellence, innovative teaching, and clinical research opportunities. The successful candidate will join a collegial, academically engaged department with a reputation for clinical excellence and innovation. The position will be primarily based at the Jewish General Hospital—a major teaching hospital affiliated with the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences at Canada’s internationally renowned McGill University, located in the vibrant and multicultural city of Montreal, in the province of Quebec.   Job Duties: The successful candidate will be expected to provide comprehensive care in head and neck surgical oncology including reconstructive services with strong emphasis on microvascular free flap reconstruction. In addition, the incumbent will play a key role in further development of head and neck oncology services within a multidisciplinary service with strong collaborations with medical and radiation oncology, and allied health services at the Jewish General Hospital.  In helping to further develop otolaryngology-head and neck surgery services and academic programs at the Jewish General Hospital, the successful candidate will foster collaboration with hospital leadership and allied health teams. The incumbent will also be expected to foster collaborations with the head and neck services at the McGill University Health Centre within the McGill Head and Neck Cancer Program, including the education and the research mission of the program.   The successful candidate must provide full-spectrum head and neck surgery and reconstructive services, with emphasis in microvascular free flap reconstruction, participate in head and neck multidisciplinary tumor board and all initiatives of the head and neck services at the JGH.   The successful candidate must participate in providing emergency otolaryngology consultations across the hospital inpatient, outpatient, ICU, and emergency departments. The incumbent will also be required to teach and supervise medical students, residents, and fellows in clinical and academic settings; participate in clinical or translational research, quality improvement, and scholarly activities in head and neck oncology; contribute actively to departmental, university, and hospital committees or service activities; and, support the growth and development of the Jewish General Hospital’s ENT programs, strengthening its role within McGill’s academic health network.   The incumbent will also be expected to be actively involved in relevant aspects of McGill’s academic mission (e.g., research, teaching, supervision of students and residents, and involvement in academic and administrative committees.   McGill faculty members are expected to contribute to service activities within their units, the University, and the wider scholarly community. A demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity, and inclusion is also expected. Qualifications and Education Requirements: Applicants must hold a medical degree and must hold, or be eligible for, licensure in the province of Quebec;   Applicants must have completed training in Otolaryngology-Head and Neck Surgery with subspecialty training in Head and Neck Surgery and Microvascular Reconstruction, and be certified by the Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons of Canada (or equivalent); Applicants must have completed a 12-month fellowship in Head and Neck Surgery and Microvascular Reconstruction by the start dat e; Additional graduate training in the form of a master’s degree or PhD degree would be an asset;        Applicant must demonstrate excellence in general ENT practice, teaching, and academic productivity;    Strong interpersonal skills and commitment to collaborative, patient-centered care;  Bilingual language skills (French and English) are preferred, or a commitment to acquire the necessary language skills to practice medicine in Quebec. Candidates should be able to interact in English and in French with patients, families, and health care professionals. Faculty/Department/Unit: Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences, Department of Otolaryngology – Head and Neck Surgery Employee Type: Contract Academic Staff ('Academic Contractual') Rank: Assistant/Associate Professor Salary: Remuneration will be according to Quebec health care fee schedule. Job Status: Full-time Posting Period: Applications must be received within 30 days of posting Required Documents: The following supporting documents are required: cover letter and curriculum vitae statement of research names and contact information of three referees Use a personal email address when creating an account in Workday to submit your application. Do not use @mail.mcgill.ca or @mcgill.ca email accounts to apply. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. L’Université McGill est une institution de langue anglaise dans laquelle la majorité des activités d’enseignement et de recherche s’effectuent en langue anglaise, rendant essentielle l’utilisation de l’anglais pour la communication orale et écrite. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Peel--1430/Director-Donor---Vol-Engagement_JR0000082353": {
      "title": "Director Donor & Volunteer Engagement",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). University Advancement (UA) promotes McGill to its global community and to the public, engaging alumni, volunteers and friends of the University, and building on McGill’s record of fundraising success in support of University priorities. By joining UA, you can be part of a dynamic and exciting team of professionals that is making an extraordinary impact on the world by advancing the philanthropic and engagement ambitions of McGill University. To find out more about UA, please visit us at www.mcgill.ca/advancement/ua-careers . Position Context: Under the direction of the Senior Director, Bequests & Planned Gifts and Donor & Volunteer Engagement and in collaboration with the Assistant Vice-President, Development and Volunteer Partnerships, lead the development and execution of a strategy for the substantive recognition and engagement of donors and senior volunteer leaders across the University. Primary Responsibilities: Lead the development and execution of a strategy for the substantive recognition and engagement of donors to deepen their relationship and amplify their giving to the University. Lead the development and execution of a strategy for the substantive engagement of senior volunteer leaders across the University who can assist in achieving current campaign objectives and moreover be nurtured for enhanced and sustained leadership service in support of the University’s philanthropic goals. Build and maintain effective collaboration and communication with development staff, Advancement Operations, Advancement Communications, and Alumni Engagement to ensure alignment of activities and timelines and optimal cross-unit efficiencies. Exercise innovative leadership in the development of long-term and annual plans and provide progress reports towards goals.  Identify emerging methods and opportunities to recognize and engage McGill’s donor and volunteer community. Conceive, develop and implement innovative plans and activities to support and promote such methods and opportunities.  Serve and work in close collaboration with Advancement’s development team and the Faculties to ensure effective recognition and engagement of our donors and volunteers.  Build productive relationships with fundraisers, faculty leadership and administrative units to ensure each donor is meaningfully and strategically recognized and engaged. Play a lead role in the development and strategic use of events that convene donors and volunteer leaders across the University, such as the Leaders Alliance. Identify the need for technology to support the mission of the department and create greater efficiencies; work with colleagues within University Advancement to build systems and reports and source external vendors and platforms, as necessary. Lead two teams of advancement professionals – Donor Relations and Volunteer Partnerships.  Ensure objectives and performance are aligned with strategic priorities of the Development Unit and of University Advancement as a whole. Act as a subject-matter expert and an ambassador of Advancement. Establish productive relationships with external and internal stakeholders in order to advance Advancement’s mission as appropriate.  Provide leadership, professional guidance and strategic advice to senior colleagues in the area of donor relations and volunteer partnerships. Perform other related duties as required.   Donor Relations specific: Foster a culture of philanthropy across the University that promotes the importance of donors in advancing the University’s priorities. Lead the development and execution of a donor-centered stewardship and recognition framework that enhances donor engagement, satisfaction, and long-term philanthropic commitment, while balancing institutional priorities and resources. Work in close partnership with other Advancement units, specifically the Fundraising and Compliance functions, to create synergies in all aspects of donor recognition and engagement. Work closely with the Communications team to ensure that tailored and broad-reaching reporting and recognition communications are closely aligned with strategic objectives and priorities. In collaboration with other Advancement departments, develop strategies and metrics to understand, measure and monitor the output and impact of the Donor Relations program.   Volunteer Engagement specific: Foster a culture across the University that promotes “careers” of volunteer service through increased levels of responsibility and engagement in programs of varied institutional focus. Help to establish a culture that values volunteerism and recognizes the role of volunteers in advancing the University's mission.  Contribute to volunteer identification and recruitment and develop and implement training programs for volunteer service and leadership development, as well as training for Advancement and University staff who work with volunteers. Oversee the building, management and growth of the Leaders Alliance program, including minimum standards, protocols and materials that support consistent engagement across all stages of the volunteer cycle. Mentor and collaborate closely with senior development leads and the Vice-President (Advancement) to ensure effective management of Faculty Advancement Boards (FABs) and Regional and National Advancement Boards (RABs and NABs). Collaborate with the Vice-President (UA) and Assistant Vice-President on the strategy and management of the Advancement Council, Council of Chairs and Emeriti Council. Other Qualifying Skills And/Or Abilities:  Extensive experience leading the strategic development, implementation, management and evaluation of comprehensive volunteer engagement and donor relations programs, preferably in an academic/higher education setting.  Proven ability to establish and execute long-term strategies that enhance donor engagement, volunteer participation, philanthropic outcomes, and institutional reputation. Expertise in overseeing complex, donor-facing initiatives, events, and recognition programs from strategic conception through implementation and evaluation. Strong understanding of the role of leadership volunteers and of donor experience and stewardship within an integrated fundraising framework, and the ability to align programs and resources with institutional priorities and advancement objectives. Knowledge and understanding of McGill University’s mission, strategic priorities and advancement goals are an asset. Demonstrated success leading, developing, and inspiring high-performing professional teams, fostering a culture of collaboration, accountability, innovation, and continuous improvement. Demonstrated ability to operate effectively in a complex and evolving environment, balancing competing priorities while leading multiple large-scale initiatives and delivering timely results. Exceptional strategic thinking, organizational leadership, planning, and project management capabilities, with a proven ability to translate vision into actionable plans and measurable outcomes. Highly developed judgment, analytical, and problem-solving skills, including the ability to address complex and sensitive issues, identify opportunities, manage risk, and drive organizational change. Strong relationship-building and influencing skills, with the ability to engage, advise, and collaborate effectively with senior administrators, academic leaders, volunteers, donors, and internal and external stakeholders. Client-centered and service-oriented, with exceptional interpersonal skills, professional credibility, political acuity, diplomacy, and discretion. Excellent verbal, written, and presentation communication skills in both French and English, with demonstrated ability to represent the University with confidence and professionalism before diverse audiences. Strong proficiency with constituent relationship management (CRM) systems, fundraising and engagement technologies, data-informed decision-making, and the Microsoft Office suite. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level #4 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 8 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 09) $115,590.00 - $144,490.00 - $177,940.00 Job Profile: MPEX-COM4C - Development & Alumni Relations - Director or equivalent (C) Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Senior Director Planned Giving & Donor and Volunteer Engagement Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-30 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McGill-College-2001/Teaching-Assistants---EBOH---Winter-2027_JR0000082547": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistants - EBOH - Winter 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Epidemiology, Biostatistics and Occupational Health Positions available : For position details, see : Teaching Assistants | Epidemiology, Biostatistics and Occupational Health - McGill University BIOS 602            Epidemiology: Regression Models (1 position) BIOS 642            Health Data: Statistical Learning and Model Visualization (1 position) BIOS 643            Theory to Practice: Analysis and Reporting (1 position) EPIB 521             Regression Analysis for Health Sciences (1 position) EPIB 603             Intermediate Epidemiology (4 positions) EPIB 605             Critical Appraisal in Epidemiology (2 positions) EPIB 621             Data Analysis in Health Sciences (4 positions) EPIB 638             Mathematical Modeling of Infectious Diseases (1 position) EPIB 705             Doctoral Level Epidemiologic Methods 2 (2 positions) PPHS 593           Special Topics in Population and Public Health 1 : Qualitative and Mixed Methods Research (1 position) PPHS 612           Principles of Public Health Practice (2 positions) PPHS 613           The Practice of Global Health (1 position) PPHS 614           Knowledge Translation and Public Health Leadership  (1 position) Estimated Number of Positions: 22 Total Hours of Work per Term: 45 to 125 Position Summaries and Required Qualifications: For details, see : Teaching Assistants | Epidemiology, Biostatistics and Occupational Health - McGill University Please note that Teaching Assistants will be required on campus to perform their tasks in person. How to Apply To be eligible for a Teaching Assistant position you must be registered at McGill University as a Graduate Student in the term to which you are applying. McGill student not currently employed at McGill University, must apply via external postings. The website will direct you to create a Workday account. Please note, you must use a personal email address when creating this account. Do not use @mail.mcgill.ca or @mcgill.ca email addresses. Candidates who are current McGill employees receiving a salary (not a graduate student stipend) must apply through their Workday employee account. Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 05 January, 2027 Position End Date: 23 April, 2027 Posting Date: 19 October, 2026 Deadline to Apply: 02 November, 2026 It is the responsibility of all members of the Faculty to adhere to the Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences “Code of Conduct” which can be found at the following link: Code of Conduct | Faculty of Medicine and Health Sciences -McGill University. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
