    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Sherbrooke-680/Business-Development---Partnerships-Officer_JR0000082404": {
      "title": "Business Development & Partnerships Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Under the supervision of the Associate Dean, Strategic Partnerships and International Education, the Business Development and Partnerships Officer will support the Strategic Partnership Unit (SPU) in achieving the School of Continuing Studies’ business development objectives. They will promote growth by identifying new opportunities, tracking performance, preparing reports, and coordinating with internal teams to ensure timely delivery and client satisfaction. They will participate in the development and refinement of SCS processes for custom solutions development and delivery by keeping abreast of industry trends, labour market developments, and government funding programs. Primary Responsibilities: Identify business development opportunities and respond to requests for custom learning solutions by proactively engaging with organizations and assessing client needs, context, and constraints. Develop proposals, budgets, and service agreements in coordination with internal teams, ensuring alignment with Academic Domains, Finance and Administration, SSEM, Strategic Communications, and other SCS and McGill units. Liaise between clients and academic domains to coordinate and assist with the implementation of approved corporate training initiatives. Contribute to SPU’s business development pipeline by identifying prospects, tracking leads, and supporting outreach efforts. Maintain client relationships through consistent follow-ups, feedback assessment, and data-informed program improvements. Support back-office systems and processes to strengthen efficiency, reporting, and project management. Monitor industry trends, labour market developments, government funding programs, and educational technologies. Contribute to refining SCS processes for custom learning solutions development and delivery. Perform other activities to support SPU operations as needed. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities: Demonstrated experience in designing and delivering educational programming, professional development, or corporate learning solutions. Strong project management skills with ability to manage competing priorities, meet deadlines, and problem-solve. Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively in multi-stakeholder projects and communicate effectively with clients, internal and external stakeholders, and other constituent groups. Experience developing proposals and managing budgets. Familiarity with Quebec and Canadian labour market and government funding programs. Attention to detail in planning and executing projects and events. Commitment to equity, diversity, inclusion, and ethical applications of AI/technology. Commitment to the values of university continuing education. Intrapreneurial mindset with ability to work independently. Experience collaborating across departments and disciplines. Bilingual (French and English); English communication required for day-to-day duties. Flexibility to accommodate clients in different time zones. Knowledge of federal and/or provincial contracting is an asset. Experience with social media platforms and website management tools is an asset. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 04) $66,190.00 - $82,740.00 - $99,290.00 Job Profile: MPEX-ADM2A - General Administration - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Faculty Lecturer Position End Date (If applicable): 2027-11-27 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-12 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bronfman-Building/Business-Development-Officer_JR0000080495": {
      "title": "Business Development Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Reporting to the Director, Desautels Career Management Services, the Desautels Toronto Lead (DTL) is responsible for growing student outcomes in the Greater Toronto Area, including full-time job placements, internships, mentoring relationships and alumni engagement levels. The DTL will nurture and expand employer partnerships, increase alumni engagement events, and establish career development initiatives. The role serves as a strategic bridge between students, alumni, employers, and the broader business community to create meaningful career opportunities and strengthen long-term institutional relationships. The candidate will seek out, develop, expand and maintain strategic partnerships with corporations, industry associations, alumni, and community organizations to support recruitment, experiential learning, mentorship, and employment outcomes for undergraduate and master's students. In addition, the DTL will provide ad hoc career coaching support to students and alumni while fostering a vibrant alumni engagement ecosystem that advances mentorship, networking, and employer connectivity. This highly external-facing role requires strong business development, relationship management, and coaching capabilities, along with a deep understanding of Toronto's labour market and corporate landscape. Primary Responsibilities: Employer Partnerships & Business Development (~70%) Works in close collaboration with the Career Management Services’ Business Development team to identify opportunities for development of internship, experiential learning, and full-time employment opportunities for undergraduate and master's students in the greater Toronto Area. Create and maintain strategic relationships, partnerships, and alliances with targeted industries, employers, business associations, and community organizations throughout the GTA. Develop diversified new business contacts aligned with student interests, career aspirations, and institutional priorities. Promote the Desautels Faculty of Management’s programs and talent pipeline to existing and prospective employers and increase visibility within high-growth employment sectors. Identify and develop opportunities to connect students, employers, alumni, and industry leaders. Monitor labour market trends and provide relevant market intelligence to students, career coaches, faculty leadership, and stakeholders. Works with the Career Management Services team to ensure that opportunities are promoted effectively through career portals, newsletters, and targeted outreach. Validate key employment information directly with employers, including hiring processes, recruitment timelines, salary information, technical requirements, and desired competencies. Collaborate with student clubs, academic programs, employer partners and the Career Events Officer to deliver networking events, employer information sessions, industry treks, and recruitment initiatives. Track employer engagement activity, recruitment outcomes, and business development metrics through designated CRM platforms. Represent the institution at industry conferences, networking events, employer forums, and professional association meetings. Alumni Engagement & Mentorship (~20%) Collaborate with Alumni Relations team to develop and implement coordinated outreach strategy and identify collaborative opportunities that support student success and alumni engagement objectives (and track results). Develop and execute strategies to re-engage alumni and strengthen their connection to McGill and Career Services. Build and maintain relationships with alumni across key industries and organizations throughout Toronto and beyond and facilitate alumni engagement initiatives including mentorship programs, coffee chats, networking events, career panels, employer spotlights, and industry roundtables. Design and manage structured mentorship programs connecting students, recent graduates, and experienced alumni. Recruit, support, and recognize alumni volunteers serving as mentors, speakers, recruiters, panelists, and industry advisors. Encourage alumni participation in recruiting, experiential learning, sponsorship, and industry engagement opportunities. Support the maintenance of a common (single) current database of alumni contact information Develop industry-specific alumni networks that foster professional development and long-term engagement. Other: Career Coaching & Talent Development (~10%) Provide ad hoc one-on-one career coaching and advising to master's students seeking careers in Toronto, interns, and alumni. Support students with career exploration, job search strategies, networking, personal branding, resume development, interview preparation, and career transitions. Serve as an additional coaching resource for MBA students during periods of high demand and coaching capacity constraints. Deliver occasional workshops, webinars, group coaching sessions, and career development programs focused on employability and professional growth, focusing on the Masters programs. Collaborate with Career Services colleagues to identify student needs and develop targeted career programming. Support alumni seeking career advancement, career transitions, leadership development, and professional growth opportunities. Contribute to the development of scalable career services initiatives that extend support beyond graduation and promote lifelong career development. Evaluate coaching outcomes and continuously improve service delivery based on market needs and stakeholder feedback. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Education Master's degree in Business Administration, Higher Education, Human Resources, Career Development, or a related field. Career coaching certification is considered an asset. Experience Minimum 7 years of progressive experience in business development, employer relations, career services, talent acquisition, alumni engagement, or a related field. Demonstrated success building strategic partnerships with employers and external stakeholders. Experience developing and managing alumni engagement, mentorship, or volunteer programs. Experience providing career coaching, talent development, or professional advising services. Strong understanding of Toronto labour market trends, employer recruitment practices, and industry sectors. Key Competencies Business Development and Relationship Management Employer Partnerships and Corporate Engagement Alumni Relations and Volunteer Management Career Coaching and Talent Development Strategic Networking and Influence Stakeholder Engagement Communication and Presentation Skills Program Development and Project Management Data Analysis and Performance Measurement Initiative, autonomy, organizational skills, business savvy As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 05) $73,470.00 - $91,840.00 - $110,210.00 Job Profile: MPEX-SAF2B - Student Services - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Associate Professor Position End Date (If applicable): 2027-10-11 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-10 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/James-Admin-Bldg/Project-Manager--Harmonization_JR0000081821": {
      "title": "Project Manager, Harmonization",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Reporting to the Vice-President, Communications and Institutional Relations (CIR) and Executive Sponsor, the Project Manager, Harmonization leads the planning, oversight, and execution of a major organizational transformation initiative. The role provides strategic leadership, project governance, and operational direction to advance organizational integration, harmonization, and business process optimization. The incumbent develops and manages project plans, schedules, scope, resource allocations, budgets, and deliverables; co-leads project meetings with CIR's Change Management Lead; monitors progress, risks, issues, and dependencies; and provides regular updates and recommendations to stakeholders. Working in close partnership with the Change Management Lead, the Project Manager drives change implementation and the successful delivery of project objectives. Primary Responsibilities: Manage and implement transformation, including operating model redesign, service delivery enhancement, and process optimization etc. aligned with the harmonized business model, using established project management methodologies, including project planning, scheduling, monitoring, and execution. Develop measurable project goals, objectives and benefits and monitor progress.  Identify and manage project dependencies and critical path activities to ensure projects remain on schedule and meet established goals/objectives.  Provide senior professional expertise; communicate goals, expectations, roles and status updates; follow up on outstanding action items and collaborate with stakeholders to identify and resolve issues as needed.    Work collaboratively with Change Management Lead to identify risks and develop mitigation strategies and contingency plans to address project challenges. Assess operational impacts and implementation requirements in collaboration with functional experts. Identify and realize opportunities to improve operational efficiency, improve client experience, and achieve cost savings. Leverage operational data, procurement expenditures, and vendor contract analyses to assess performance, and inform business transformation and continuous improvement efforts.   Coach and guide teams on best practices in organizational transformation and continuous improvement. Partner with the Change Management Lead to align project timelines and implementation activities while maintaining clear accountability for respective workstreams. Establish and maintain effective relationships, communication tools and systems with stakeholders to ensure that required information, resources and expertise are available to support the achievement of project milestones and schedules.  Liaise with stakeholders, partners and other interested parties; collaborate with multidisciplinary teams and provide cross-functional guidance. Enable timely resolution of project-related concerns through effective coordination with CIR and McGill community stakeholders. Drive consistency and quality of implementation through the execution of project management best practices.  Ensure project deliverables are documented, approved, and transitioned appropriately to operational teams. Knowledge and Skills Strong project planning and execution skills. Knowledge of project management methodologies, frameworks, and best practices. Excellent organizational and time-management skills. Strong analytical, problem-solving, and decision-making abilities. Exceptional verbal and written communication skills. Ability to manage competing priorities in a fast-paced environment. Proficiency with project management software and reporting tools. Ability to influence and build consensus across diverse stakeholder groups. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 4 (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 5 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 07) $92,150.00 - $115,190.00 - $138,230.00 Job Profile: MPEX-ADM3A - General Administration - Senior Professional/Manager Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Vice-President (Communications & Institutional Relations) Position End Date (If applicable): 2026-10-16 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-11 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/James-Admin-Bldg/Multimedia-Producer_JR0000082933": {
      "title": "Multimedia Producer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Reporting to the Manager, Video Production the Multimedia Producer will provide support and expertise in the field of video production and editing of communication material that promotes McGill’s research, teaching, innovations and accomplishments. Working closely with the Manager, Video Production, he/she will assist in the planning and execution of video shoots, providing creative input as needed. The incumbent will possess a combination of instructional design and graphic design skills to help produce videos, primarily for University online education programs.   Primary Responsibilities: Working closely with the Manager, Video Production, plan and execute of video projects, by meeting with clients to determine needs, prepare budget estimates and delivery schedules. Ensure that projects meet established timelines and specifications Maintain records of costs and time schedules of projects Generate ideas, develop storyboards and scripts for a range of video genres Film and edit educational and promotional videos Schedule and supervise additional personnel as needed on video shoots (e.g. student actors) Design and integrate graphics in accordance with McGill’s Visual Identity guidelines and motion graphics Work with professors, lecturers and researchers to adapt PowerPoint presentations for use in video productions Develop and implement an organization system for multimedia files and create relevant metadata records and upload videos to YouTube and other social media platforms Provide professional expertise re: post-production software solutions, best practices and industry trends Adapt creative approach to project constraints As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 4 (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 04) $66,190.00 - $82,740.00 - $99,290.00 Job Profile: MPEX-COM2A - Internal External Relations - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Manager Video Production Position End Date (If applicable): 2027-10-27 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-11 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Education-Building/Senior-Advisor--Transformation-and-Culture_JR0000082905": {
      "title": "Senior Advisor, Transformation and Culture",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary   Reporting directly to the Dean, the Senior Advisor, Transformation and Culture provides senior professional leadership for selected cross-Faculty initiatives that advance the Faculty’s strategic priorities, strengthen organizational culture, and improve institutional effectiveness. The role operates across academic and administrative environments to translate strategic priorities into practical implementation plans, lead and facilitate change, improve alignment, and foster a culture of collaboration, accountability, and service excellence. The incumbent also leads the restructuring of Faculty communications, including external outreach, constituency engagement through targeted campaigns and space development initiatives, and internal communications. The incumbent acts as a senior advisor and strategic partner to Faculty leaders, managers, and central University stakeholders to advance major initiatives. The role also works in ongoing collaboration with the Office of the Provost and the Horizon McGill offices to ensure alignment between Faculty-level transformation priorities and broader institutional change initiatives.   Strategic planning, implementation and project leadership Lead communications strategy and restructuring, aligning outreach, engagement campaigns, space development, and internal communications with Faculty priorities. Ensure the translation of Faculty strategic priorities into actionable implementation plans, including timelines, milestones, and accountability mechanisms. Advance the execution of the Faculty’s strategic plan across key priority areas. Oversee cross-functional initiatives and the implementation of cross-unit initiatives related to academic programming, student experience, accreditation, and service delivery to ensure alignment across academic leadership, administrative services, and central University stakeholders. Support alignment between Faculty implementation priorities and relevant University-wide transformation and continuous improvement initiatives, including those advanced through Horizon McGill. Establish and maintain implementation frameworks, tools, and reporting mechanisms to support decision-making and track progress of key initiatives. Monitor implementation progress, identify risks and issues, and adjust approaches to ensure timely and effective delivery of initiatives. Advance initiatives that strengthen the Faculty’s public presence and institutional coherence. Oversee implementation planning related to staff development, engagement, recruitment, and culture-building. ​ Transformation, culture, and change management Lead changes to communications and shared spaces that strengthen constituency engagement, collaboration, and belonging. Develop and implement change management approaches to support the adoption of new ways of working across the Faculty with institutional OD practices. Collaborate and align proactively with the Organizational Development team to ensure that Faculty initiatives, change strategies, and implementation approaches are consistent with the institutional HR, OD vision and standards. Contribute to institutional knowledge sharing and change-management practices by collaborating, as appropriate, with central offices, communities of practice, and Horizon McGill-related initiatives. Lead stakeholder engagement processes to ensure alignment, clarity of expectations, and adoption of change initiatives. Assess organizational readiness, climate, and impact of change initiatives, and provide insights to inform leadership decisions.   Institutional effectiveness and service improvement Restructure and manage external and internal communications roles, visual identity and public image rehaul, workflows, and channels for identity consistency and quality maintenance. Manage the improvement of information sharing across the Faculty.  Oversee the review and improvement of key processes and services to enhance efficiency, effectiveness, and service quality. Identify and implement improvements to workflows, roles, and accountabilities across units to strengthen coordination and operational effectiveness. Ensure cross-functional alignment and resource consideration in the implementation of improvement initiatives. Establish tools and practices that support consistency, knowledge management, and effective handling of complex initiatives.    Dean-level advisory support Advise the Dean on communication needs across the Faculty, public image related matters, as well as internal restructuring of workflows across all Faculty units.  Provide strategic advice to Dean on matters related to transformation, culture, and organizational effectiveness. Prepare decision-support materials to inform senior leadership discussions and governance processes and make recommendations. Represent the Dean in internal and external forums to advance Faculty priorities. Qualifications and Experience Master’s degree required in education, higher education, public administration, organizational development, business administration, human resources, interdisciplinary studies, or another related or interdisciplinary field. Demonstrated experience leading communications strategy, including practical experience graphic design, social media content and web design development, and visual identity creation and management. Substantial progressively responsible experience in strategic implementation, organizational transformation, institutional effectiveness, change management, or a related area in a complex organization. Demonstrated ability to lead cross-functional initiatives and to work effectively with senior leaders, academic administrators, professional staff, and multiple stakeholder groups. Experience in process improvement, service redesign, consultation, organizational analysis, and the use of evidence to support institutional decision-making. Prior experience in organizational transformation in the domain of higher education is an asset. Excellent judgment, discretion, relationship-building ability, and capacity to manage sensitive issues with diplomacy and professionalism. Exceptional oral and written communication skills, including the ability to prepare clear briefings, reports, presentations, and implementation materials for varied audiences. Full bilingual proficiency in English and French is essential, including the ability to advise, facilitate, write, and present in both languages. Demonstrated commitment to equity, inclusion, respectful engagement, and culturally responsive institutional practice. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents One (1) day of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 5 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 07) $92,150.00 - $115,190.00 - $138,230.00 Job Profile: MPEX-HRM3F - Organizational Development & Talent Management - Senior Professional/Manager Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Professor Position End Date (If applicable): 2027-10-25 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-10 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/McIntyre--Medical/Course-Lecturer---Physiology---PHGY560---Light-Microscopy-for-the-Life-Sciences_JR0000081457": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer - Physiology - PHGY560 - Light Microscopy for the Life Sciences",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The Department of Physiology of the Faculty of Medicine, McGill University, is seeking applications for a Course Lecturer for the 2026-2027 academic year to teach PHGY560, Light Microscopy for the Life Sciences . Responsibilities Teach 2x2 hour lectures per week. Implement 5 tests and 6 assignments throughout the term. Choose seminar journal articles for students. Guide students for oral presentations and evaluate seminar presentations. Run hands on laboratory activities as part of the course. Supervise two teaching assistants who will contribute as lab assistants and markers for the course. Implement and mark an end of term exam. Provide final marks to the department of physiology. Coordinate with core facilities for lab activities requiring research infrastructure.   Other main responsibilities include: Upload teaching PowerPoint presentations to MyCourses Oversee teaching assistant marking of tests and assignments, marking final exams Enter grades on MyCourses Record lectures on Zoom and upload recorded content if appropriate Interact with students and approve class absences as appropriate Experience Experience with a wide range of imaging instruments and biological samples would be an asset. Teaching Qualification Requirements: Ph.D. in bioimaging or related field or MSc plus practical experience totaling 5 years. Experience in coordinating and teaching is an asset. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hiring Unit: Physiology Course Title: PHGY560 Light Microscopy for the Life Sciences Subject Code: PHGY 560 Location: MCMED 1101 or 1131 Schedule: Monday and Wednesday 11h35-13h25 Deadline to Apply: McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Peterson-Hall/Postdoctoral-Researcher--Group-C--Campbell_JR0000082111": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Researcher (Group C) Campbell",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). The Indian Ocean World Centre (IOWC), Department of History & Classical Studies, invites applications for two Postdoctoral positions to research the history of the Indian Ocean World (IOW), a macro region running from East Asia to Eastern Africa, in the period c.1800 to the present. The posts are to examine the impact of, respectively, (i) sulphur-rich volcanic eruptions and (ii) cyclones. This research opportunity explores the socio-economic and political impacts of such volcanism/cyclonic activity. We are particularly interested in candidates whose work engages with the experiences of peoples and communities indigenous to the IOW, from gender, age, ethnic and wealth disparity perspectives. Research topics may include, but are not limited to: agricultural production trade health, disease and medicine demography migration religious beliefs revolt and political change This position is unionized, and the incumbent will hold dual status as both a student and a McGill employee, and as a member of the AMURE (Postdocs) bargaining unit. For interested applicants, you must include: 1) a cover letter that includes a description of current research, future plans for research, and interest in the position; 2) a curriculum vitae; 3) one sample of scholarly writing (a published article, unpublished paper or chapter); and 4) the names of three referees to provide letters upon request.  Applicants who do not have a completed PhD at the time of application must include a letter from the Department Chair specifying the date of the dissertation defence. Please upload them all to Workday when you apply. Refer to the following link for further information on eligibility criteria for postdoctoral appointments . RESPONSIBILITIES The successful candidate will: Develop and lead an innovative, independent research program focused on the impacts in the IOW of either sulphur-rich volcanic eruptions or cyclones Design and conduct qualitative, ethnographic, historical, or interdisciplinary research projects examining the intersections of such volcanic and cyclonic events with socio-economic and political structures and transformations. Collaborate with faculty supervisors and other researchers within the IOWC and the Department of History & Classical Studies on ongoing research initiatives and grant-funded projects. Collect, analyze, and interpret research data using appropriate methodological and theoretical approaches. Prepare manuscripts for publication in peer-reviewed journals and contribute to other scholarly outputs, including book chapters, reports, and policy-oriented publications. Present research findings at national and international conferences, workshops, seminars, and public-facing events. Contribute to the intellectual and research environment of the IOWC and Department through active participation in seminars, reading groups, workshops, and collaborative activities. Assist in the editorial management of the Journal of Indian Ocean World Studies. Support knowledge mobilization activities and contribute to the dissemination of research findings to academic, policy, and public audiences. Assist in mentoring graduate and undergraduate students involved in related research projects, as opportunities arise. QUALIFICATIONS The candidate must have recently been awarded (within 5 years) a PhD or equivalent in History, Anthropology, Sociology, Science and Technology Studies (STS), Health Humanities, or a closely related discipline. The PhD must be completed before the appointment start date of 14 March 2027. Please note that candidate eligibility to hold a Postdoctoral Research Fellowship expires 5 years after the date on which their PhD was awarded, regardless of when they are hired.  Demonstrated research expertise in the environmental, economic, religious, or political history of the Indian Ocean World or a region thereof. Strong record of scholarly achievement relative to career stage, evidenced by peer-reviewed publications, conference presentations, or other research outputs. Experience conducting independent research using qualitative, ethnographic, interpretive, historical, philosophical, or mixed-methods approaches. Excellent written and oral communication skills, including the ability to communicate complex ideas to academic and non-academic audiences. Demonstrated ability to work independently while contributing effectively to collaborative and interdisciplinary research environments. Hiring Unit:  Indian Ocean World Centre (IOWC), Department of History & Classical Studies Number of Positions:  2  Supervisor:  Professor Gwyn Campbell Total Hours Per Week:  35 Salary:  $55,000/year  Planned Start and End Date:  22 March 2027 to 21 March 2028 Location:  Peterson Hall, 3460 McTavish Street Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-27   Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Annual Salary: $55,423.08 Hours per Week: 35 (Full time) Location: Peterson Hall Supervisor: Professor Position Start Date: 2026-10-08 Position End Date: 2028-03-21 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-27 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Stewart-Biology-Bldg/Assistant-Professor-in-Computational-Neuroethology_JR0000082218": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Computational Neuroethology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Biology Position Description: The Department of Biology at McGill University invites applications for a tenure-track position at the rank of Assistant Professor in computational neuroethology with a target start date of August 1, 2027. The ideal candidate will develop an innovative, interdisciplinary research program that integrates computational or theoretical approaches with modern systems neuroscience to address fundamental questions on the neural basis of naturalistic animal behavior. We also welcome candidates whose research programs focus on the development and application of computational methods for the study of brain and behaviour through collaborations and secondary data. McGill has a deep history and continued prominence in neuroscience, and the Biology Department is part of this expansive neuroscience community. In addition, the department has ties to the growing computational neuroscience community centered around the Montreal Institute for Learning Algorithms (MILA), a joint McGill and Université de Montréal initiative bringing together scientists focused on Artificial Intelligence.  We offer access to state-of-the-art core facilities including a host of advanced microscopy resources (light sheet, super-resolution, and advanced confocal microscopy), next-generation sequencing, and animal facilities, among others. Overall, the department provides a unique and stimulating research environment that is highly conducive to collaboration and interdisciplinary approaches. In addition to its research mission, the Department of Biology plays a key role in undergraduate education in the biological and biomedical sciences. Faculty will also have the opportunity to supervise and mentor graduate students.  About us :  McGill University is a leading research university in Canada. Its downtown campus sits at the foot of Mont Royal, a large green space overlooking the heart of downtown Montréal. A UNESCO City of Design, Montréal is world-renowned for its high quality of life and arts, design, food, and festivals. Despite its global reputation, the city is one of the most affordable and livable large metropolitan areas in Canada and the US. Job Duties: The successful applicant will be expected to conduct a rigorous program of independent, externally funded research. The successful applicant will also contribute to teaching in the Biology core curriculum, which includes foundational undergraduate courses in neurobiology and behavior, as well as courses offered through joint programs and at the graduate level. Preference will be given to applicants who can demonstrate a commitment to teaching a diverse student body. Qualifications and Education Requirements: Applicants must possess a Ph.D. or equivalent degree in Biology or a related discipline, as well as postdoctoral experience and a significant track record of research excellence as demonstrated by scientific publications. Faculty/Department/Unit: Science/Biology Job Type: Tenure-track Rank: Assistant Professor Salary: Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Application deadline: To ensure full consideration, complete applications should be received by December 1, 2026, 11:59 pm . Please note that the Workday posting closes on December 2 at 12:00 am. Applications should be submitted by December 1, 2026 at 11:59 pm. Contact: Enquiries should be directed to info.biology@mcgill.ca with “ Computational Neuroethology Search ” in the subject line. How to apply to Workday: Microsoft Word - Workday_Guide for Candidates_Extenal_ENG.docx . Required Documents: Persons wishing to be considered for this position should provide a one-page summary of the application package (briefly describing academic background, major research contributions, and teaching experience) ; a curriculum vitae (including a list of publications) ; a research statement including previous contributions and future plans, as well as any leaves contributing to career interruptions or slowdowns (maximum 3 pages); a statement of teaching philosophy and experience, including an account of a commitment to inclusive teaching, learning, and supervision (maximum 1 page), and PDF files of up to 3 major publications. After submitting your application, you may be contacted to provide the name and email address of at least three referees, who can provide confidential letters to accompany your application. Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Currie-Gymnasium/Administrative-Student-Affairs-Coordinator_JR0000082906": {
      "title": "Administrative Student Affairs Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Summary: Under the direction of the immediate supervisor, the Administrative Student Affairs Coordinator provides administrative and secretarial support for student and departmental operations, ensuring the smooth functioning of the unit. The role supports students from admission through graduation and serves as a resource on policies and procedures, acting as a liaison between students, faculty, and administrative units. Responsibilities include coordinating activities related to graduate admissions, registration, program progression, and degree completion; maintaining student records and unit documentation; and advising students on their files. The incumbent will also supports administrative operations through tasks such as purchasing, processing transactions, maintaining accounts, and assisting with basic financial tracking, as well as reviewing and editing documents for accuracy.   Primary Responsibilities: Provides front-line support and guidance to students on admissions, registration, academic policies, and graduate studies procedures. Coordinates graduate admissions, recruitment, orientation, internships, placements, and student communications. Monitors student progress, academic milestones, thesis processes, comprehensive examinations, and degree completion requirements. Administers course and program registration, enrolment, academic records, grading processes, and graduation audits. Supports the administration of scholarships, fellowships, awards, and student funding programs. Acts as a liaison between students, faculty, university offices, and external institutions, ensuring compliance with academic policies and procedures. Coordinates hiring and administrative processes for course lecturers, teaching assistants, graders, and guest lecturers. Provides departmental administrative and financial support, including procurement, expense management, travel coordination, accounts payable, and facilities requests. Organizes departmental meetings, events, and committee activities; prepares reports, correspondence, records, and other administrative documentation.   Qualifications Skills and Abilities Demonstrated proficiency in university administrative systems, including Slate, Banner, Minerva, MyProgress, MyThesis, SIS, Workday, SharePoint, and Microsoft Office Suite. Experience with university financial and budget systems, including Banner FIS, Minerva, and MMP. Demonstrated knowledge of university policies, procedures, collective agreements, and hiring processes for Teaching Assistants, Graders, Research Assistants and Associates, and Course Lecturers. Strong knowledge of graduate admissions processes, including application review, credential evaluation, degree assessment, and verification of academic eligibility and program requirements. Experience supporting graduate student administration, including recruitment, orientation, advising, registration, academic records management, milestone tracking, and student lifecycle processes. Experience administering course registration, enrolment, permit approvals, internship and practicum tracking, grading processes, transcript verification, deferred exams, and graduation audits, while liaising with key university stakeholders. Familiarity with scholarships, awards, fellowships, and student funding programs, including federal, provincial, university, faculty, and departmental funding sources. Exceptional organizational, analytical, and time-management skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and meet strict deadlines. Strong attention to detail, proofreading and editing skills, and the ability to maintain accurate records and handle confidential information with discretion. Excellent communication and interpersonal skills, with demonstrated professionalism and the ability to effectively support students, faculty, researchers, and administrators. Proven ability to work independently and collaboratively in a team environment (as this role will liaise with GPS, ED, and SSAO). Demonstrated ability to coordinate meetings, prepare agendas, and record accurate meeting minutes. Minimum Education and Experience: DEP - Office Systems 4 Years Related Experience / DEP - Secretarial Studies Hourly Salary: (MUNACA Level G) $31.19 - $38.65 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Administrative Manager Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-05 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Education-Building/Internships-Supervisor_JR0000082908-1": {
      "title": "Internships Supervisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Reporting to the Manager Internships and Student Affairs, the Internships Supervisor is responsible for developing, implementing and overseeing all aspects of B.A.(Education), Major in Education in Global Contexts students’ internships as well as all related processes programs and special projects.  The Internships Supervisor will also be responsible for the student affairs services for internship courses, including advising, student records, and registration. The Internships Supervisor will establish and develop partnerships within various communities and/or organizations in order to provide quality, impactful and innovative internship opportunities to support to B.A.(Education) students. Primary Responsibilities: Develop and oversee the  Internship program for B.A. (Education), Education in Global Contexts program Responsible for the building and development of partnerships with private sector, government and non-profit organizations with the aim of securing internship placements for students of B.A.(Education), Major in Education in Global Contexts. Assess the suitability of potential internships for B.A.(Education) students. Implementing and overseeing moderately complex administrative procedures and systems in support of the internships operations (e.g. placement process, supervision assignments, student teaching management IT system, course creation, web and email communications). Advise and mentor students on internship requirements, application process, selection, internship registration, deadlines, requirements, required evaluations. In collaboration with the B.A. (Education) Advisor validate if students completed the pre-requisites of the internships. Ensure that the policies and procedures of the program are communicated to students, community partners, supervisors, internship host organizations/sites, and government parties receive proper information and direction.  Establish and foster effective working relationships with host organizations/site administration by identifying needs and providing support. Liaise with government officials if required. Ensure that student teaching internship practices are in compliance with ISA, University, and government directives and policies. Meet with students to review their internship-related records and needs, including accommodation plans co-created with the Office for Students with Disabilities; provide personalized advising to students based on their specific contexts. Address issues (incidents in the field, student behaviour issues, student performance issues ) raised students, supervisors, and internship host sites (e.g. incidents at internship). Develop, oversee and organize internship-related events (pre-internship orientations)and trainings (New supervisor training, pre-internship supervisor briefings, online trainings for students)for students and supervisors. Oversee the promotion and administration of internship-related student awards. Compile internship-related statistics and data for inclusion in annual reports for Faculty and external reports , including official government reporting (placements completed in academic year sent to Ministère de l’Éducation) as well as conduct data analysis and creates reports as needed. Acts as a key member of the Internship and Students Affairs Office team and collaborates with various McGill departments, Faculties and Units, as well as with external expert service providers. Assess and resolve administrative problems related to internships and student affairs. When appropriate, inform supervisor and formulate recommendations. When needed, provide support to Student Affairs Administrator and B.Ed. Internships Supervisor in the management of their projects and functions. Supervise and oversee the work of clerical staff, including casuals. Assist employees in their training and continued professional development. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Experience in internship programming with demonstrated ability to  develop, implement, monitor and evaluate a strategic plan; Self-starter with proven experience executing new projects under tight timelines without compromising quality assurance. Experience in a leadership and project management role is an asset Master’s or Graduate Certificate Degree (Leadership/ Education/Management) and knowledge of undergraduate and graduate education is an asset. Demonstrated organizational, communication, interpersonal skills, and leadership abilities. Proven ability to take initiative, work independently in problem solving and managing multiple projects. Sound judgment and an ability to perform well under pressure and to meet strict deadlines. Excellent time-management skills with an ability to multitask and adjust to changing priorities; Demonstrated experience liaising with multiple stakeholders with an ability to build and maintain effective partnerships with external organizations; Demonstrated ability to work in a computerized environment using Microsoft Office suite(advanced Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook), Adobe and other software; knowledge of McGill’s Student Information System (Banner/Minerva) and McGill’s student teaching management system (STEPS) is an asset. Knowledge of French and English. McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 1 Year Related Experience with Bachelor’s Degree / DEC III 3 Years Related Experience with DEC III Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 03) $63,350.00 - $74,530.00 - $85,710.00 Job Profile: MPEX-SAF1B - Student Services - Professional 1 (Excluded) Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Manager Internships and Student Affairs Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-10 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/James-Admin-Bldg/Project-Manager--Graduate-Education-Initiatives_JR0000082858": {
      "title": "Project Manager, Graduate Education Initiatives",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Graduate and Postdoctoral Studies Position Summary: Reporting to the Director Strategy & Operations, the Project Manager, Graduate Education Initiatives provides advising, planning, policy and project management support on a range of dossiers and initiatives related to graduate education and graduate student success at McGill. The incumbent participates in the preparation of planning documents, reports, briefing notes, data analysis and presentations that inform decision-making by the Dean and Associate Deans of Graduate and Postdoctoral Studies.   Primary Responsibilities: Manage and lead high-impact projects and initiatives, defining scope, objectives, success metrics and delivery timelines. Monitor progress to provide clear updates, insights, and recommendations to unit leadership. Serve as the primary point of contact for internal and external stakeholders, ensuring alignment and effective communication through the project lifecycle. Design and launch new initiatives informed by strategic priorities of the unit (e.g. Graduate Supervision; Program Climate; Graduate Outcomes). Conduct in-depth, and at times ad-hoc, research on graduate education topics to inform decision-making in the unit. Benchmark McGill against peer institutions. In collaboration with Analysis, Planning and Budget (APB), design, conduct, analyze and report on surveys about graduate education at McGill. Formulate data-informed recommendations for policy changes and policy implementation, improvements to program delivery, and other unit priorities. Ensure appropriate follow-up to policy changes and new regulations (e.g. mandatory supervision orientations). Write reports, briefing notes and other documentation appropriately adapted to internal and external audiences. Develop and deliver workshop presentations. Stay abreast of new trends and developments in graduate education to inform decision-making at the senior administration level (e.g. academic debates about supervision, graduate degrees, post-graduation outcomes etc.). Support the preparation of the Dean and Associate Deans for presentations, speeches, participation in important meetings, advocacy efforts etc. Build and maintain strong relationships with key internal and external stakeholders (e.g. faculties, graduate program directors, students, central units, and partner organizations) to advance Graduate and Postdoctoral Studies priorities. Participate in meetings and consultations with internal and external stakeholders to gather input, align interests, and support consensus‑building on graduate education initiatives. Translate stakeholder feedback into actionable insights to inform policy development, project design, and decision‑making. Manage small teams to achieve defined project objectives by setting priorities, assigning responsibilities, establishing timelines and monitoring progress. Provide day-to-day guidance and problem-solving support to ensure successful project implementation.     Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Graduate degree an asset. Advanced professional proficiency in spoken and written French and English, with the ability to develop communications, resources, and partnerships in support of francization and bilingual initiatives. Strong analytical skills with the ability to work with quantitative and qualitative data, identify trends, generate insights and develop data-informed recommendations. Background or interest in data analysis an asset. Strong understanding of the structure of the academic milieu and of graduate education; experience in a university setting, particularly in graduate studies, an asset. Experience managing projects from planning through implementation, including coordinating small teams, setting priorities and monitoring progress. Strong organizational skills and attention to detail, with the ability to manage multiple projects, establish priorities and meet tight and competing deadlines. Proven ability to conduct in-depth research and critically analyze, synthesize, and communicate complex information to support decision-making and strategic initiatives. Ability to work independently and demonstrate initiative. Proven ability to creatively solve problems and demonstrate “out-of-the-box” thinking. Ability to work with abstract, broad, intangible concepts and flesh out concrete action plans. Excellent communication, interpersonal and relationship-building skills, with the ability to develop and nurture partnerships with diverse internal and external stakeholders and to adapt written and oral communications to diverse audiences, including senior leadership. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days Two (2) floating holidays Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day Paid time off over the December holiday period Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Up to two (2) days of remote work per week where the position permits Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 4 (qualifier) on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 04) $66,190.00 - $82,740.00 - $99,290.00 Job Profile: MPEX-ADM2A - General Administration - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Director Strategy & Operations Position End Date (If applicable): Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-12 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Duff-Medical-Bldg/Teaching-Assistant---BMDE-517---Electromagnetic-Technologies-for-Biomedicine_JR0000082911": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant - BMDE 517 - Electromagnetic Technologies for Biomedicine",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Biomedical Engineering Course Title: Electromagnetic Technologies for Biomedicine Course Code: BMDE 517 Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 20 Job Description: Testing and optimizing computational assignments Responding to students’ questions Grading assignments, midterms, and finals. Qualifications: Only registered Graduate McGill students can be hired as TAs Ideally, must have previously passed the course. Alternatively, can have good knowledge of the course. Good communication skills. Hiring Unit: Biomedical Engineering Course Title and Course Number: BMDE 517 - Electromagnetic Technologies for Biomedicine Estimated Number of Positions: 1 Total Hours of Work per Term: 20 hours Hourly Salary: $38.46 Position Start Date: 2026-11-02 Position End Date: 2026-12-18 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-28 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
