    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Ferrier-Building/Building-Operator_JR0000080640": {
      "title": "Building Operator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Facilities Management and Ancillary Services - HVAC Operations Position Summary: Under the supervision of the HVAC Supervisor, Zone East – the Building Operator is responsible for the operation and maintenance of heating, refrigeration and other building engineering equipment assigned under his care. Qualifications: Must possess valid certificates of Fourth Class Stationary Engineering along with a class “B” (Refrigeration Equipment Operations). English and French functional spoken and read. English or French functional written. Additional Details: Unit: HVAC Premium: Certification (0.60$/hour) Status: Full Time Employment type: Contract Hours of Work: 38.75 hours/week, day shift from 7:30 am to 3:45 pm; the incumbent will be required to serve on a call-in list. A meal time period of thirty (30) minutes is unpaid. The incumbent may be assigned to work in a hospital environment where building operations support patient care and research activities and may be required to respond to urgent operational requirements in a fast-paced environment. The incumbent will be required to wear a mask and other required personal protective equipment (PPE) when working in designated areas. As one of Montreal's Top Employers, here is what we offer: Competitive benefits package (Health, Dental, Life Insurance) (if eligible) Defined contribution pension plan (with employer contribution up to 10%) (if eligible) Group Registered Retirement Savings Plan (RRSP) and Tax Free Savings Account (TFSA) Competitive vacation policy Two (2) personal days (if eligible) Two (2) floating holidays (if eligible) Nine (9) \"Summer Fridays\" - paid days off between the St-Jean Baptiste holiday and Labour Day (if eligible) Paid time off over the December holiday period (if eligible) Tuition waiver for regular employees and their dependents Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. Knowledge of English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing. The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level 2 on a scale of 0-4 . For a definition of our language proficiency levels, please click here . Hourly Salary: $35.48 Hours per Week: 38.75 (Full time) Location: Ferrier Building Supervisor: Supervisor Building Operations Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-19 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Bronfman-Building/Masters-Career-Coach_JR0000079493": {
      "title": "Masters Career Coach",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nFull time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary: Reporting to   the   Associate   Director , Career coaching   of   Desautels   Career   Management   Services   (CMS) ,   and working closely with the   Academic Director, Program Director,   Technical Interview Coach,   and Industry Liaison for the Master of Management in Analytics (MMA) program ,   the  Masters Career Coach (MMA)   leads the program’s general career development and career-readiness   services.   The incumbent   provides individualized coaching ,   designs   program-wide   programming , and   translates labor-market intelligence into student-facing   support   so students receive coherent   and relevant guidance. Primary Responsibilities: Career   advisement   and individualized coaching   Support the overall plans and annual   objectives   for MMA career development.   Provide   individualized   consultation and coaching   to   MMA students   to clarify career goals , target roles and sectors,   develop a   professional value proposition, and   build   practical job-search plans.     Advise   students on   résumés, cover letters, LinkedIn profiles ,   networking   skills , personal branding ,   employer communications, self-directed job search, behavioral   interviews, offer evaluation, and transition to employment.   Establish a proactive coaching cadence,   monitor   engagement, and develop targeted interventions for students who are not progressing or who face barriers in the job search.   Liaise with the Technical Interview Coach on referrals for coding assessments, technical cases, analytics problem-solving, and other specialized technical preparation . Respond to routine student inquiries   regarding   recruiting timelines, application processes, networking opportunities, and career resources.    Support special projects and initiatives related to student success and career development.    Manage student communications related to career services, internships, and recruiting opportunities.   Career programming and student readiness   In consultation   with the instructor for the MMA careers course, d esign and implement a consistent, integrated MMA career-planning program across the academic year, including workshops, resources, recruiting preparation, and just-in-time interventions aligned with recruiting cycles.   M aintain a global overview of M M A-related Career Services programming and collaborat e   with M M A Program and Academic Directors   and the Technical   Interview Coach   to   plan   offerings that meet market demands and ensure the highest degree of client satisfaction among students .   Prepare students for employer presentations, career fairs, networking activities, alumni interactions, interviews, and other recruiting touchpoints;   facilitate   structured preparation and debriefing where   appropriate .   Explore career-development best practices, platforms, facilitators, and external providers; manage approved vendor relationships and recommend offerings based on student needs, service quality, and available budget.   Employer and Recruitment Support   Work closely   with the MMA Industry Liaison and   CMS   Business Development team   to share aggregate student interests, target sectors , and   assess   readiness needs; use employer and industry feedback to adapt coaching and programming .     Monitor employer recruiting timelines and ensure students receive   timely   information.   Represent CMS at internal or external meetings as   appropriate   and provide subject-matter input to Faculty communications, annual reports, ranking submissions, and accreditation materials.     Outcomes, evaluation, and continuous improvement   Ensure   accurate   collection and analysis of internship and post-graduation employment outcomes, including placement status, role and sector, compensation data, and other measures used for management, rankings, accreditation,   comparison with peer institutions,   and institutional reporting.   Monitor changes in recruiting practices and employment trends in analytics, artificial intelligence, data science, consulting, technology, and related sectors, and adapt student-facing services accordingly.   Conduct market research on employment trends in analytics, artificial intelligence, data science, consulting, and technology sectors.    Support   key   initiatives aimed at improving student placement outcomes and employer engagement.   Collaborate with other members of CMS to share best practices and provide occasional back up/ support as needed   Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Bachelor's degree in Business , Human Resources, Education, Career Development, Communications,   Analytics,   Information Systems, or a related discipline.    A m inimum   of   three (3) years of experience in career services or career coaching at a   leading   university   or business school   and/or in corporate recruiting, talent development, human resources consulting, executive search, or a related field .   Demonstrated experience   in   career   coaching ,   learning -program design, facilitation, or a related field, preferably with graduate or professional students.    Strong professional demeanor, trustworthiness, and ability to manage sensitive information with discretion.   Excellent written and verbal communication skills   in English and French , with strong attention to detail .    Strong   relationship building skills ,   client orientation,   professionalism, and discretion ; ability to engage with an international and diverse clientele .    Comfort with ambiguity and the capacity to manage several projects and priorities simultaneously, including   a workload that varies with recruitment cycles.    Strong data literacy and   prof ic iency   with Microsoft Office, particularly Excel,   database,   career management platforms , CRM systems (Salesforce), survey tools, and related technologies.      Willingness to work flexible hours, particularly during peak recruitment periods.   In -depth knowledge of   current recruitment practices in analytics, consulting, technology, and AI-related industries   is strongly preferred .   Strong understanding of graduate and professional recruitment processes, labor-market dynamics, and employer expectations. Knowledge of analytics, artificial intelligence, data science, consulting, technology, or related employment markets is strongly preferred.   #LI-Hybrid Minimum Education and Experience: Bachelor's Degree 3 Years Related Experience / Annual Salary: (MPEX Grade 05) $73,470.00 - $91,840.00 - $110,210.00 Job Profile: MPEX-SAF2B - Student Services - Professional 2 Hours per Week: 33.75 (Full time) Supervisor: Associate Director Career Coaching Position End Date (If applicable): 2027-09-27 Deadline to Apply: 2026-11-05 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Penfield-1085/Course-Lecturer---LING-371---Winter-2027_JR0000080507-1": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer - LING 371 - Winter 2027",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Course Lecturers - Department of Linguistics Winter 2027 Term Course: LING 371 – Syntax 1 (3 credits) section 001 Course details: Introduction to the study of generative syntax of natural languages, emphasizing basic concepts and formalism: phrase structure rules, transformations, and conditions on rules. Teaching Schedule : Monday, Wednesday, Friday 02:35PM - 03:25PM Course Start/End Date: January 5, 2027 to April 14, 2027 Contract Dates: January 5, 2027 - May 7, 2027 Location: TBA Salary (current rate for 3-credit course teaching): $11,824 Posting Period: October 27, 2026 - October 28, 2026 Application deadline: October 28th, 2026, 11:59 PM Position Summary: A Course assignment includes all or part of the following teaching activities: a) preparation of the Course and Course plan;  b) delivery of the Course;  c) evaluation of students;  d) submitting to the University a report on student attendance and student grades;  e) supervision of Teaching Assistants or any other teaching support staff;  f) upon request, review of students’ grades;  g) meeting with students, being available to answer questions and give advice concerning Course content and requirements;  Employees shall perform their duties in compliance with the Pedagogical Regulation and as specified in the administrative directives of the Hiring Unit. An Employee shall use appropriate pedagogical methods and material. Teaching Qualification Requirements (TQRs): Successful completion of at least PhD Evaluation 1 or equivalent. Experience: University classroom teaching experience or equivalent. Documented research experience in syntax. Other: Successful completion of graduate level coursework that covers current issues in syntax and its interface with related fields, theoretical foundations in syntactic theory, basic tools of syntactic analysis, and the development of argumentation in syntactic theory. Required Application Documents:  Applicants must submit the following documents: 1. an updated CV, 2. a letter of introduction stating their teaching philosophy and how they would approach the teaching of a particular course , and 3. a syllabus for the course which must follow the template provided by the McGill course outline guidelines ( https://www.mcgill.ca/tls/instructors/course-design/outline ). Before applying, please note that to work at McGill University, you must be both authorized to work in Canada and willing to work in the province of Quebec at the campus where the position is based / located. McGill University is an English-language University where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing. Hiring Unit: ASC 680 Course Title: Syntax 1 Subject Code: LING 371 001 Location: RPHYS 114 Schedule: Mondays, Wednesdays, Fridays, 02:35 pm-03:25 pm Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-28 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Education-Building/Field-Supervisor_JR0000072339": {
      "title": "Field Supervisor",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-27\nPart time\n\n​ Please refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Position Summary & Primary Responsibilities - McGill's Internships & Student Affairs Office (ISA) engages Field Supervisors to work throughout and beyond the Greater Montreal region to:  In collaboration with the cooperating teacher: Support and mentor Student Teachers, in relation to their development as future educators Observe, assess, and evaluate Student Teachers in relation to their progressive development of Professional Competencies, both with oral and written feedback, per an assessment schedule Proactively communicate and collaborate with the host school(s), including mediating any potential challenges or obstacles as they arise As a representative of McGill University: Serve as a liaison between McGill and the host school(s) Ensure that ISA and McGill policies and procedures are implemented and enforced Verify that the student teaching placement(s) conform to university guidelines Eventually, as a seasoned Field Supervisor, mentor and support new Field Supervisors Required Experience and Other Qualifying Skills And/Or Abilities - Student teaching supervision is a demanding but rewarding role requiring a complex blend of soft skills, knowledge and professionalism. Candidates require:  Required: Demonstrated ability and willingness to mentor and support student teachers as professional learners Ability to deliver quality, constructive feedback designed to improve the pedagogical learning of student teachers Strong organizational, interpersonal, communication, and time management skills Available during the entire duration of a given Field Experience and/or Internship (Dates and lengths of Field Experiences and Internships can be viewed  here .) Exceptional professional judgment, and ability to work independently, and an ability to facilitate challenging professional conversations Access to a vehicle and willingness to travel up to 1 hour each way to a host school Minimum 5 years of school-based experience in an elementary and/or high school setting, preferably in Quebec Deep understanding of the  Quebec Education Program  including the Subject Competencies, Broad Areas of Learning, and Cross -Curricular Competencies as well as and related professional curriculum documents Preferred: Substantial prior experience as a cooperating teacher to student teachers Demonstrated ability and comfort to work in a computerized environment using a variety of systems and new technology, including but not limited to Microsoft Office, email, Adobe Acrobat/Reader (fillable PDFs), webforms/online assessments Bilingualism – French/English (written and spoken), an asset Ability to exercise authority and accountability in situations of conflict involving multiple parties McGill University is committed to equity and diversity within its community and values academic rigour and excellence. We welcome and encourage applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to engage productively with diverse communities. At McGill, research that reflects diverse intellectual traditions, methodologies, and modes of dissemination and translation is valued and encouraged. Candidates are invited to demonstrate their research impact both within and across academic disciplines and in other sectors, such as government, communities, or industry. McGill further recognizes and fairly considers the impact of leaves (e.g., family care or health-related) that may contribute to career interruptions or slowdowns. Candidates are encouraged to signal any leave that affected productivity, or that may have had an effect on their career path. This information will be considered to ensure the equitable assessment of the candidate’s record. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated equity groups to self-identify. It further seeks to ensure the equitable treatment and full inclusion of persons with disabilities by striving for the implementation of universal design principles transversally, across all facets of the University community, and through  accommodation policies and procedures . Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca . All qualified applicants are encouraged to apply; however, in accordance with Canadian immigration requirements, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Stewart-Biology-Bldg/Animal-Care-Assistant-B_JR0000082522": {
      "title": "Animal Care Assistant B",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Department of Biology (Professor Melanie Guigueno) Position Summary: Assists with behavioural testing. Keeps clear and detailed records of bird care and behavioural testing. Monitors and cares for live birds on weekdays and weekends. Keeps animal spaces clean and tidy. Other Qualifying Skills and/or Abilities Attention to details. Available on weekends. Reliable. Comfortable handling live birds. Education/Experience: Background in behavioural ecology or neuroscience. Currently enrolled in an undergraduate degree. Location:   This position is located in Montreal, Quebec and the work for this position is expected to be performed in Quebec.   Knowledge of French and English: McGill University is an English-language university where day to day duties may require English communication both verbally and in writing.  The level of English required for this position has been assessed at a level # (4) qualifier on a scale of 0-4.   Minimum Education and Experience: High School Diploma 1 Year Related Experience Hourly Salary: (AMUSE B) $16.85 Estimated Number of Work Hours per Week: 10 (Part time) Position Start Date: 2026-10-16 Position End Date: 2026-12-31 Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-30 This position is covered by the AMUSE collective agreement. McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    },
    "https://mcgill.wd3.myworkdayjobs.com/mcgill_careers/job/Education-Building/Course-Lecturer-EDEC-201-776-OFNIE-F26_JR0000082922": {
      "title": "Course Lecturer EDEC 201 776 OFNIE F26",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-26\nPart time\n\nPlease refer to the  How to Apply for a Job (for External Candidates)   job aid for instructions on how to apply. If you are an active McGill employee (ie: currently in an active contract or position at McGill University), do not apply through this Career Site. Login to your McGill Workday account and apply to this posting using the Find Jobs report (type Find Jobs in the search bar). Hiring Unit: Office of First Nations and Inuit Education (OFNIE) Course Number-Section and Title: EDEC 201 776 First Year Professional Seminar Course Description: Orientation to the culture and community of school and to teaching as a profession. Focus on the general functioning of schools and complexity of the teacher role. Competencies and working professional portfolios will be addressed . Posting Dates : October 26 - 28, 2026 Application Deadline: October 28, 2026; 11:59 p.m. CL Credits: 1 (This course is not team-taught) Number of Positions Available: 1 Term: Fall 2026 Course Dates : November 2 - 5, 2026 Contract dates : November 2 - 20, 2026 Schedule: Nov 2, 3, 5, online from 6-9 pm and Nov 4 in person 5-9 pm Location: Waswanipi Teaching Qualification Requirements (TQRs): Education A Master’s degree (in exceptional circumstances, applicants without a Master’s degree but with significant research or professional experience related to the course content may be considered). Experience Expert knowledge of course content as outlined in the course description. Knowledge of the Quebec Education program (QEP) and the curriculum of the partnering school board with whom this course is being giving in collaboration. Familiarity with the 13 professional competencies specified in the Ministère de l’Éducation Knowledge of the impact of colonialism on Indigenous communities and proven record integrating pedagogical approaches anchored to equity, diversity and inclusion. Experience as a teacher and/or consultant in the partnering school board for which this course is being delivered. Experience teaching in the Quebec elementary school system. Previous successful experience teaching similar course(s) to pre-service teachers. Previous successful experience teaching similar courses in Indigenous communities. Experience developing curriculum and/or instructional approaches grounded in Indigenous knowledge and pedagogies. Other Adherence to evolving program requirements concerning course content and program-developed competencies; course content and references must reﬂect the most current advances in the ﬁeld. The course instructor is expected to demonstrate capacity and willingness to modify the course to the educational, social, and cultural context of the community in which it is being delivered. Availability to participate in course coordination and planning activities prior to the course start date (approximately two weeks in advance; online format). Demonstrated knowledge and/or experience with Indigenous issues in First Nations and Inuit contexts, for example, through prior teaching, community engagement, or relevant academic or professional work.   Information supplémentaires/Additional Information:   Effective dates of the appointment are November 2 - 20, 2026. Course dates are November 2 - 5, 2026. This course will be offered in-person in Waswanipi following the schedule above. You are covered by the working conditions specified in the McGill Course Lecturers and Instructors Union collective agreement; https://www.mcgill.ca/hr/files/hr/mcliu_collective_agreement_-_final_version_for_printing_0.pdf . McGill University is an English-language university where most teaching and research activities are conducted in the English Language, thereby requiring English communication both verbally and in writing.  Please contact ofnie.education@mcgill.ca  with any questions you may have. Hiring Unit: Bureau de l’éducation des Premières Nations et des Inuit (OFNIE) Course Title: Séminaire professionnel 1 Subject Code: EDEC 201 776 Location: Waswanipi Schedule: Les 2, 3 et 5 novembre, en ligne, de 18 h à 21 h; le 4 novembre, en personne, de 17 h à 21 h Deadline to Apply: 2026-10-28 McGill University hires on the basis of merit and is strongly committed to equity and diversity within its community. We welcome applications from racialized persons/visible minorities, women, Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, ethnic minorities, and persons of minority sexual orientations and gender identities, as well as from all qualified candidates with the skills and knowledge to productively engage with diverse communities. McGill implements an employment equity program and encourages members of designated groups to self-identify. Persons with disabilities who anticipate needing accommodations for any part of the application process may contact, in confidence, accessibilityrequest.hr@mcgill.ca ."
    }
  },
